\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18There is no ban\u8217\'20\'19: Republicans test-drive new  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  messaging\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,356 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2023\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ad released in Virginia part of well-funded effort to portray Democrats as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists and Republicans as reasonable compromisers\par \par \uc2 The ad opens with the sound of a fetal heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost people believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment of birth is wrong, far beyond any reasonable limit. Not Virginia Democrats,\u8221\'20\'1D a female narrator says, just before the sound of a baby cooing and crying. \u8220\'20\'1CThey fought to make late-term abortions the rule, not the exception.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the end of the ad, the heartbeat flatlines.\par \par \uc2 The digital ad, released in the last few weeks by the Virginia Republican party, is part of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/new-gop-ad-campaign-control-virginia-centers-abortion-limits-rcna111492"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-figure effort}}}\uc2  to win over voters ahead of Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s state legislative elections on 7 November. It aims to portray Democrats as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists and Republicans as champions of a reasonable compromise on a notoriously controversial issue. And it\u8217\'20\'19s a new, risky Republican strategy \u8211\'20\'13 led by Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin \u8211\'20\'13 to recast \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a winning issue, after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade last year led the GOP to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/11/abortion-midterm-elections-republicans-women-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 underperform}}}\uc2  in the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 If this strategy works in Virginia, it won\u8217\'20\'19t stay there. The Virginia Republican party is hoping to retake control of the state senate after winning both the house of delegates and the governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion in 2021. If they succeed, Republicans across the country will likely push the 15-week ban in their own 2024 races.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBoth parties will claim, if they win, that, as goes Virginia, so goes the rest of the country. But what you really get, I think, is the testing of the messaging in Virginia,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Stephen Farnsworth\uc2 , director of the Center for Leadership and Media Studies at \uc2 University of Mary Washington\uc2  in Virginia. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s clear that Republicans have struggled to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the supreme court decision, and this will be the latest test of Republican anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  messaging.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Before Roe fell, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually motivated Republicans, not Democrats, to head to the ballot box; while only a minority of Americans totally oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , those voters are some of Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 most committed constituents. For decades, Republicans were able to appease them by passing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions \u8211\'20\'13 but were also largely able to evade any political fallout from those restrictions, because Roe stopped them from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 Those guardrails are now gone, and Virginia is the only southern state that hasn\u8217\'20\'19t significantly restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise. More than 70% of Democrats and half of Republicans in the state say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an important issue to them in this election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/tablet/2023/10/20/oct-11-16-2023-washington-post-schar-school-virginia-poll/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to an October poll}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and the Schar School of Policy and Government at \uc2 George Mason University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a winning issue for the Democrats, if they know how to message it properly,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mark Rozell, founding dean of the Schar School. \u8220\'20\'1CSo I think Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s strategy, and that of the Republicans in Virginia, is to frame the issue in a way that will not seem threatening to swing voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans are trying to reframe the issue by digging into the faultlines within Americans\u8217\'20\'19 support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although most Americans have long supported access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and even more support it in the wake of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s downfall \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/506759/broader-support-abortion-rights-continues-post-dobbs.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most people say they don\u8217\'20\'19t think}}}\uc2  the procedure should be legal past the first trimester of pregnancy. (While the majority of US abortions do take place in that first trimester, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and their supporters say that, post-Roe, more people are getting abortions later on in pregnancy, as stringent restrictions have slowed down their access to the procedure.)\par \par \uc2 At the same time, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a notoriously difficult subject to poll people about. Depending on how pollsters ask a question, respondents can give wildly different, even contradictory, answers.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Virginia law allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the second trimester of pregnancy, or up until about 27 weeks. In the October poll from the Schar School and the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , 49% of voters said they would like Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws to stay the same, while 24% said that they would like those laws to loosen. But in that same poll, support for a 15-week ban was evenly split \u8211\'20\'13 contradicting the widespread support for keeping or broadening Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe idea that advocating for commonsense protections for the unborn will doom Republicans is a narrative concocted by liberals, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t put much stock in it,\u8221\'20\'1D Ken Nunnenkamp, executive director of the Virginia Republican party, said in an email. It\u8217\'20\'19s Democrats, he said, who are refusing to be straightforward about their views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans have a responsibility to call out Democrat \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremism.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Virginia house Republican caucus and Virginia senate Republican caucus have bought a website, nolimitsvirginia.com, to target Democrats on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The website recycles {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/27/abortion-myths-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 common myths about \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , such as the use of the medically inaccurate term \u8220\'20\'1Clate-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and suggesting that Democrats support allowing abortions to take place \u8220\'20\'1Cuntil the moment of birth\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The website also quotes the Democratic state delegate \uc2 Kathy Tran\uc2 , who is now running for re-election and said in 2019 that a proposed bill would permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Call the way up to 40 weeks\u8221\'20\'1D. (Tran later {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/virginia-politics/lawmaker-at-center-of-abortion-bill-firestorm-elected-as-part-of-democratic-wave-that-changed-richmond/2019/01/31/d4f76ecc-2565-11e9-90cd-dedb0c92dc17_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Washington Post}}}\uc2  that she \u8220\'20\'1Cmisspoke\u8221\'20\'1D.)\par \par \uc2 Morgan Hopkins, the communications director for the Virginia house Democratic caucus, said that Democrats do not support any further restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats have always been clear that this decision should be between a woman and her doctor,\u8221\'20\'1D Hopkins said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no compromising on that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In another rhetorical tactic, Virginia Republicans are also striking the word \u8220\'20\'1Cban\u8221\'20\'1D from their vocabulary and replacing it with \u8220\'20\'1Climit\u8221\'20\'1D. In October, Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s political action committee, Spirit of Virginia, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/glenn-youngkins-pac-launches-first-tv-ad-november-election-focused-abo-rcna119759"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropped $1.4m}}}\uc2  into an ad buy that included a spot focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just not true. Their lies about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D a female voice says at the beginning of the ad. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s disinformation. Politics at its worst.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHere\u8217\'20\'19s the truth. There is no ban,\u8221\'20\'1D the ad\u8217\'20\'19s narrator continues, as a baby begins to gurgle in the background. \u8220\'20\'1CVirginia Republicans support a reasonable 15-week limit with exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a sign of just how central this message is to the Republican state senator Siobhan Dunnavant\u8217\'20\'19s re-election campaign, visitors to her website are greeted with a pop-up ad titled \u8220\'20\'1CNot a Ban \u8230\'20\'26 \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the ad, Dunnavant tells the camera: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t support an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Period.\u8221\'20\'1D The ad goes on to explain that Dunnavant, who is running for re-election in a district that includes part of the city of Richmond, Virginia, supports banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks, with some exceptions. (Dunnavant\u8217\'20\'19s team did not reply to multiple interview requests.)\par \par \uc2 Youngkin did not dream up the idea of a 15-week \u8220\'20\'1Climit\u8221\'20\'1D on his own. Instead, it is rapidly becoming a flagship Republican policy. The case that overturned Roe v Wade, Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, hinged on a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from Mississippi. \uc2 Susan B Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, a powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that operates nationwide, has also been pushing Republicans to support a 15-week federal ban.\par \par \uc2 Kaitlin Makuski, political director for Susan B Anthony Pro-Life America, applauded Virginia Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 support for what she called a \u8220\'20\'1Ccommonsense and compassionate 15-week limit\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCandidates across this country should take note of how Republicans in Virginia are leading on the issue of life by going on offense and exposing the left\u8217\'20\'19s radical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Democrats, meanwhile, are trying to bring up the word \u8220\'20\'1Cban\u8221\'20\'1D whenever they can.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA ban is a ban is a ban,\u8221\'20\'1D Hopkins said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat they\u8217\'20\'19re talking about is a ban on a right that so many women had access to for years. I\u8217\'20\'19m 24 in Virginia, and now I might have less rights than my mom did, who was born in 1968. To me, that is something that is absolutely insane.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231101ejb1001gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
