\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Kansas cannot enforce  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill law or impose 24-hour wait, judge rules\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 590 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2023\par 00:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Preliminary order before 2024 trial comes months after Kansans voted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in state constitution\par \par \uc2 Kansas cannot enforce laws that would force \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients to wait 24 hours for the procedure or be given anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  talking points, a judge ruled on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The move comes months after Kansans overwhelmingly voted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution.\par \par \uc2 Judge K Christopher Jayaram\u8217\'20\'19s preliminary injunction will freeze a host of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Some of the restrictions date back to the 1990s, such as a law that requires \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients to wait 24 hours between an initial consultation and their procedure. Others were passed as recently as this year, such as a law that would have required \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to indicate to patients that their pill-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be \u8220\'20\'1Creversed\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a claim that is not backed up by science.\par \par \uc2 The suspended \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions probably not only infringe on patients\u8217\'20\'19 constitutional right to bodily autonomy guaranteed by the Kansas state constitution, Jayaram said, but they probably violate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers\u8217\'20\'19 free speech rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Court has great respect for the deeply held beliefs on either side of this contentious issue,\u8221\'20\'1D Jayaram wrote in a 92-page order. \u8220\'20\'1CNevertheless, the State\u8217\'20\'19s capacity to legislate pursuant to its own moral scruples is necessarily curbed by the Kansas Constitution and its Bill of Rights. The State may pick a side and viewpoint, but in doing so, it may not trespass upon the natural inalienable rights of the people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Johnson county district judge\u8217\'20\'19s order is set to last until a trial in the case, which is scheduled for 2024.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Kansas became the first state to vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the fall of Roe v Wade. Usually a reliably red state, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kansas stunned the nation}}}\uc2  when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters won a landslide victory to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 The Monday ruling further highlights the importance of state constitutions in the post-Roe battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now that the US constitution no longer contains the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have sought to embed or defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in states\u8217\'20\'19 constitutions. So far, they have been successful, winning multiple voter referendums on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, Kansas has also become a haven for people fleeing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that now blanket much of the southern and midwestern United States. But there are only a handful of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Kansas, and they have been besieged by patients. One \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic involved in the lawsuit had its number of patients {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/KS-Hodes-Nauser-v.-Kobach_PI.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surge by 40%;}}}\uc2  almost two-thirds of the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s patients are traveling more than 100 miles for abortions.\par \par \uc2 Trust Women, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Wichita, Kansas, that was not a part of the lawsuit, now treats double to triple the number of patients the clinic saw before Roe fell. Seventy per cent of its patients come from out of state, and of those out-of-state patients, 70% come from Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are very much a regional healthcare provider now,\u8221\'20\'1D Zack Gingrich-Gaylord, Trust Women\u8217\'20\'19s communications director, said. Without these restrictions, Gingrich-Gaylord said, Trust Women should be able to see a few more patients each week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt creates a better atmosphere of comfort and safety and security for the patients that are coming in, to know that the state isn\u8217\'20\'19t coming into this clinic and trying to undermine their experience,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231101ejb10005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
