\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  on the ballot: six US states with elections in 2023\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,298 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2023\par 15:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what you need to know about where and how Americans will vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/09/virginia-election-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an off-off year}}}\uc2  for US elections, but somehow, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 A year after nationwide rage over the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade crushed Republican hopes for a \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D in the midterms, Democrats are trying to once again harness that anger to defeat Republicans in the relatively few states holding 2023 elections. The GOP, on the other hand, is still scrabbling over how to talk about an issue that could not only cost them seats this year but also lead them to lose the White House in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The elections in November will help both parties fine-tune their messaging on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as determine the future of some of the last states in the south and midwest that have access to the procedure. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what you need to know about where and how Americans will vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky\par \par \uc2 Last year, deep-red Kentucky stunned the nation when voters rejected a constitutional amendment that would have clarified that the state constitution does not protect abortions. Now, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be the deciding issue in the governor\u8217\'20\'19s race between the incumbent governor, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , a Democrat, and the current attorney general, \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , a Republican.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky law currently bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in almost all circumstances, including in cases of rape and incest. Cameron has long supported the ban. Although {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-kentucky-governor-race-8c0a143c252576f405b0dbda12e69a84"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he indicated in September}}}\uc2  that he would approve a bill adding exceptions for rape and incest, he suggested a few weeks later that he would do so only \u8220\'20\'1Cif the courts made us change that law\u8221\'20\'1D, according to a recording of a conversation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-kentucky-governor-campaign-ec767bf7802852d48ea150b7118fc90c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 obtained by the Associated Press}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Beshear has attacked Cameron for his longtime hardline stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , releasing an ad featuring a young woman discussing being raped by her stepfather.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was raped by my stepfather after years of sexual abuse. I was 12,\u8221\'20\'1D the woman says to the camera in the ad. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is to you, \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 . To tell a 12-year-old girl she must have the baby of her stepfather who raped her is unthinkable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hadley\u8217\'20\'19s story is important.\par \par \uc2 Under Kentucky\u8217\'20\'19s current law\u8212\'20\'14which \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2  supports and defended to the Supreme Court\u8212\'20\'14women and girls like her would have no options. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/U89QieNgr2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/U89QieNgr2}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  (@AndyBeshearKY) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AndyBeshearKY/status/1704499248165736614?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 20, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Mississippi\par \par \uc2 Mississippi, home to the case that toppled Roe as well as a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, is one of the few places where Democrats are not relying on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to win races. Instead, Brandon Presley is focusing his run for governor on his pledge to expand Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Presley is explicitly trying to cast himself as a maverick within the Democratic party when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a recent ad, Presley tapped a Bible and told the camera that the text was \u8220\'20\'1Cwhy I\u8217\'20\'19m pro-life\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey\par \par \uc2 In contrast with Democrats in Mississippi, Democrats in New Jersey are hammering Republicans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even though rights to the procedure are not at serious risk in the state. One Democrat running for state senate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qoBJb3m9lL0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released an ad accusing}}}\uc2  her opponent of wanting to \u8220\'20\'1Cgive aborted tissue legal personhood\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Republicans win our statehouse this year, we can expect Florida-style restrictions on our healthcare rights,\u8221\'20\'1D she warned in the ad.\par \par \uc2 Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 chances of winning control in New Jersey are slim. The state is home to nearly a million more registered Democrats than Republicans. Plus, New Jersey legislators have already passed laws recognizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a fundamental right.\par \par \uc2 Ohio\par \par \uc2 While 2022 saw a flurry of ballot initiatives on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Ohio is the only state this year to vote directly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , through a referendum on whether to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution. The state has a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which is frozen due to ongoing litigation. It could come roaring back to life if the referendum fails.\par \par \uc2 In August, Ohio voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/09/ohio-issue-1-abortion-rights-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defeated another referendum}}}\uc2  that would have made it far more difficult to amend the state constitution \u8211\'20\'13 which many saw as an attempt by the state GOP to hurt the November vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Republicans won a key victory this fall when they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/19/ohio-abortion-rights-referendum-language"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 successfully altered the text of the ballot}}}\uc2  to describe the referendum with language such as \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn child\u8221\'20\'1D. Proponents of the referendum say the new language of the ballot, which is what Ohioans will see in the voting booth, is misleading.\par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have so far won every ballot initiative in the country on the issue since Roe fell, and their chances look good in Ohio. Nearly 60% of Ohio voters plan to vote to amend the state constitution, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/news/2023/10/baldwin-wallace-poll-58-of-ohio-voters-supporting-abortion-amendment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a mid-October poll}}}\uc2  from Baldwin Wallace University.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, a historically purple state, has long been viewed as a bellwether for how issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will play with voters on the national stage. Now, eyes are on one race there: the battle for a seat on the state supreme court, between the Republican Carolyn Carluccio and the Democrat Dan McCaffery.\par \par \uc2 The election will not decide party control of the supreme court, because Democrats already have a 4-2 majority. But, since disputes over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights regularly end up in state supreme courts, one more Republican on the bench could make the difference in future fights.\par \par \uc2 McCaffery is making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a key part of his campaign, frequently bringing it up on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s pretty clear from a personal standpoint that I believe those particular issues are best decided between a woman, her conscience and her doctor,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2023/08/27/pennsylvania-supreme-court-abortion-00113074"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he told Politico in August}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Carluccio has tried to downplay her stance on the issue. Her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://keystonenewsroom.com/story/republican-nominee-for-pennsylvania-supreme-court-wiped-anti-abortion-language-from-website/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign website removed language}}}\uc2  that described her as a \u8220\'20\'1Cdefender\u8221\'20\'1D of \u8220\'20\'1Call life under law\u8221\'20\'1D, and demurred when a reporter asked whether the Pennsylvania state constitution contains privacy rights that may protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, as some experts have argued. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Pennsylvania constitution is different in certain ways,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wesa.fm/politics-government/2023-09-01/pa-supreme-court-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she told a Pennsylvania NPR affiliate}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Virginia\par \par \uc2 For the past few years, control over Virginia has flipped back and forth between Democrats and Republicans. Now, with the GOP in control of one legislative chamber as well as the governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion, they could once again win a trifecta in the state \u8211\'20\'13 and pass a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Virginia is one of the few places where Republicans are openly discussing not only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also their interest in banning it past 15 weeks of pregnancy. If that ban goes into effect, it will affect the entire south-east United States, if not the entire country. Virginia is the only southern state that has not significantly restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe fell, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers across the south are fleeing there for the procedure. Since it has become far more difficult to get abortions, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers say they are seeing patients later and later in pregnancy. For those providers, the fall of Roe has made preserving access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 15 weeks even more crucial.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one of the top issues in the election, and 72% of voters support keeping Virginia \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law as is or making it less restrictive, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cnu.edu/wasoncenter/surveys/archive/2023-10-17.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to recent polling from the Wason Center at Christopher Newport University}}}\uc2 . However, when asked about a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, voters were more circumspect. Just 54% said they oppose such a ban.\par \par \uc2 Republicans and Democrats are also in a dead heat: 42% of voters say they plan to support the Democratic candidate in their local race for the state legislature, while 41% say they will support the Republican candidate.\par 