\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Who won the Republican debate? Our panel responds\b0\par\par\uc2 Bhaskar Sunkara, Lloyd Green, Osita Nwanevu , Jill Filipovic and LaTosha Brown \par \uc2 1,803 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2023\par 17:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Yet again, Trump dominated a stage that he wasn\u8217\'20\'19t even on\par \par \uc2 Bhaskar Sunkara: \u8216\'20\'18 We\u8217\'20\'19re far away from a pro-worker Republican party\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 A new Republican party was supposed to be in the making. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president catered to corporate interests and the super-rich, but as candidate he wrote a playbook to winning over workers in greater numbers.\par \par \uc2 At tonight\u8217\'20\'19s debate, it\u8217\'20\'19s clear his would-be successors haven\u8217\'20\'19t read it. While Trump has been traveling around Michigan signaling his support for working people (though tellingly at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/27/us/politics/trump-autoworkers-detroit.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 non-union factories}}}\uc2  ), in California the rest of the field struck a far different note when discussing workers that will be key in battleground states like Michigan and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Scott openly attacked the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 demands and said that instead of showing up on their picket lines, President Biden should be protecting our southern border. Insurgent candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  had a similar response: \u8220\'20\'1CIf I was giving advice to those workers, I would say go picket in front of the White House in Washington DC,\u8221\'20\'1D advocating energy deregulation as an alternative solution to America\u8217\'20\'19s woes.\par \par \uc2 In between his anti-Trump posturing, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  joined in. \u8220\'20\'1CThis public school system is no longer run by the public. It is run by the teachers\u8217\'20\'19 unions in this country,\u8221\'20\'1D the former New Jersey governor said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd when you have the president of the United States sleeping with a member of the teachers\u8217\'20\'19 union, there is no chance that you could take the stranglehold away from the teachers\u8217\'20\'19 unions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The reference to Jill Biden has all the crassness of a Trump line, but none of the political acumen. It\u8217\'20\'19s no wonder no one on the stage is likely to square up against President Biden in November.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Bhaskar Sunkara is the president of the Nation, the founding editor of Jacobin, and the author of The Socialist Manifesto: The Case for Radical Politics in An Era of Extreme Inequalities\par \par \uc2 Lloyd Green: \u8216\'20\'18Cringe-worthy comes to mind\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won Wednesday night\u8217\'20\'19s debate. From 2,295 miles away, he dominated the seven Republicans who appeared at the Reagan library. His legal woes escaped real attention, albeit his latest posturing over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  less so. He narrowly leads \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and leaves the Republican pack in the dust. The race\u8217\'20\'19s dynamics remain unchanged.\par \par \uc2 On stage, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  got the airtime he craved but polls third in New Hampshire. Nikki Haley auditioned to be Trump\u8217\'20\'19s running mate but earned his campaign\u8217\'20\'19s ire as she was onstage. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is officially irrelevant and a bad joke-teller.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThank you for speaking while I\u8217\'20\'19m interrupting.\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s quote of the night was an instant classic. Tim Scott let us know that African Americans had a tougher time enduring the Great Society than slavery. Cringe-worthy comes to mind.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the evening definitely highlighted Biden\u8217\'20\'19s vulnerabilities. For starters, bedlam reigns at the Mexican border.\par \par \uc2 Upcoming legislative contests in Virginia offer a reality check. Republican victory would scream \u8220\'20\'1Cdanger\u8221\'20\'1D for the president and his party. A Trump-backed shutdown, however, might provide them with a lifeline. The Old Dominion is filled with government contractors and small businesses.\par \par \uc2 Early in his term, Biden mistakenly took a premature victory lap. \u8220\'20\'1CI am confident that Barack is not happy with the coverage of this administration as more transformative than his,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2022/may/01/this-will-not-pass-trump-biden-democrats-book-review"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he said}}}\uc2 . He also compared himself to FDR. Uh, that guy won four terms, assisted by convincing congressional majorities. Also, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t Obama\u8217\'20\'19s child.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Lloyd Green is an attorney in New York and served in the \uc2 US Department of Justice\par \par \uc2 Osita Nwanevu: \u8216\'20\'18Nothing but an act of God can dislodge Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lead\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Do debates matter? This is itself the subject of some debate among those who follow presidential elections closely. Commonsensically, big breakout moments for candidates that are replayed in the days after the big event can boost the profiles of candidates who manage to pull them off. And there did seem to be some movement in favor of at least Nikki Haley after the first debate of this cycle. But the race before the candidates in the Republican field hasn\u8217\'20\'19t fundamentally changed: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the last president of the United States and a now-heroic figure in the Republican party, retains and will continue to retain a commanding lead over his rivals that seemingly nothing short of an act of God, and maybe not even that, can dislodge.\par \par \uc2 Trump gained the most of any candidate in the field after the first debate. The huffing and puffing from the other contenders felt a bit different this time around, though. Tim Scott took center stage for much of the night \u8211\'20\'13 delivering not only his stock lines about how his own experiences disprove the existence of structural racism in American society, but hits against the records of his rivals, including Nikki Haley, who engaged him in an extended exchange about whether the state department paid for her curtains. Riveting stuff, but the substance really mattered less than the demonstration, to anyone watching, that he\u8217\'20\'19s still in the fight.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s absence, by comparison, was a theme many candidates hit upon, though frankly there were moments during the night when some of the candidates on the stage themselves seemed like nonentities. It took a strikingly long time for Fox\u8217\'20\'19s moderators to send questions \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s way \u8211\'20\'13 he acquitted himself well when they arrived, but not well enough that he\u8217\'20\'19s likely to see the bump in his standing he needed coming out of tonight.\par \par \uc2 The night\u8217\'20\'19s real surprise, really, was Fox\u8217\'20\'19s editorial line \u8211\'20\'13 there were questions early on about income inequality and the party\u8217\'20\'19s standing with Latino voters, for instance. It felt throughout like a debate aimed at pulling the primary towards the center from two different directions \u8211\'20\'13 the business conservatism that Trump upended with his victory in 2016 and the populist conservatism that hopes to succeed him. There are substantive ideological tensions aplenty to be wrestled with on the right at the moment and the powers that be in the heart of the conservative press are evidently interested in teasing them out. They did their best and so did the candidates.\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s still Trump\u8217\'20\'19s party and still Trump\u8217\'20\'19s race to lose.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Osita Nwanevu is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Jill Filipovic: \u8216\'20\'18These egomaniacs don\u8217\'20\'19t care about women\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 If there\u8217\'20\'19s one takeaway from the second Republican debate on Wednesday night, it\u8217\'20\'19s that this party of blustering egomaniacs simply does not care about women.\par \par \uc2 There was only a single woman on stage \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps not a surprise from a party that struggles to put women in office and to capture women\u8217\'20\'19s votes. Startlingly few questions were about the issues that animate the lives of so many American families, and women\u8217\'20\'19s lives especially: childcare, healthcare, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Republican lawmakers criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in conservative states, and while even voters in conservative states vote for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights when given the chance, the Republican hopefuls were wishy-washy on the issue \u8211\'20\'13 not wanting to be accountable for their party\u8217\'20\'19s own extremism, but also refusing to align themselves with America\u8217\'20\'19s pro-choice majority. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  turned a question about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into talking about defunding \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and wanting to fund drug treatment. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  simply refused to accept that pro-choice voters cost Republicans some midterm wins. There was only a single question about childcare, and it went largely unanswered. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , though, did find time to refer to the first lady, Jill Biden, as someone whom the president is \u8220\'20\'1Csleeping with\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The candidates had more to say about invading Mexico than invading women\u8217\'20\'19s uteruses \u8211\'20\'13 although most of them seemed to favor both.\par \par \uc2 Even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he of \u8220\'20\'1Cgrab \u8217\'20\'19em by the\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 you know \u8211\'20\'13 seems to understand the bind Republicans have put themselves in with their legislative misogyny. Trump isn\u8217\'20\'19t an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  moderate \u8211\'20\'13 he\u8217\'20\'19s the reason Roe v Wade was overturned, and he has voiced support for jailing women who have abortions \u8211\'20\'13 but the truth is that he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t really care; he\u8217\'20\'19s willing to do whatever he thinks will put him in power and keep him there. He is a keen observer of his own party, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/4219271-trump-tries-to-play-both-sides-on-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his flip-flopping on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  seems to signal that he sees the Republican\u8217\'20\'19s anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremism as a liability.\par \par \uc2 But he wasn\u8217\'20\'19t on stage tonight. Those who were seem committed to doubling down on their attacks on women\u8217\'20\'19s rights \u8211\'20\'13 or ignoring women altogether.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jill Filipovic is the author of the The H-Spot: The Feminist Pursuit of Happiness\par \par \uc2 LaTosha Brown: \u8216\'20\'18They are not willing to face real issues\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The debate was another display of Republican presidential candidates coddling Trump\u8217\'20\'19s legacy. Even in his absence, these candidates have chosen denialism and partisan rhetoric as a strategy for positioning themselves as the next best thing to Trump. It is clear they are not willing to face real issues, protect democracy, or hold themselves, and others like them, accountable for their actions. Each candidate on that stage lacked the fortitude to separate themselves from Trump \u8211\'20\'13 and, in a race for leadership, that is very telling.\par \par \uc2 The debate was so chaotic and disjointed, due to the candidates bantering back and forth and talking over each other, that debate moderators had to remind them: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re all speaking at the same time, no one can understand you.\u8221\'20\'1D It was like watching children fight for attention at the local schoolyard.\par \par \uc2 There appears to be an ongoing unwillingness to offer real solutions for real life for everyday people. One example is the candidates\u8217\'20\'19 refusal and unwillingness to seriously address the labor issues that have led to the current \uc2 UAW\uc2  auto workers\u8217\'20\'19 strike. Instead of suggesting any remedy that would provide millions of American households with job and economic security, several candidates suggested punishing workers or union-busting while ironically claiming to support the need to bring jobs back to America from places like China.\par \par \uc2 The candidates also refused to adequately address the impending government shutdown. They chose to lay the blame at Biden\u8217\'20\'19s feet without acknowledging Congressional Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 refusal to work out a solution and apparent hellbent determination to force an unnecessary shutdown.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s all smoke and mirrors. The Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s identity crisis was primetime viewing. The debate was not only a reflection of how broken and childish the Republicans are, but of their willingness to cannibalize each other for power and take the country down with them.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 LaTosha Brown is the co-founder of Black Voters Matter\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231002eja2002ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
