\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18You just have to speak up\u8217\'20\'19: Gloria Johnson aims to be Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s first Democratic senator in 28 years\b0\par\par\uc2 Mary Yang \par \uc2 1,269 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2023\par 09:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Johnson speaks with the Guardian one week after launching campaign, which is focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and gun reform\par \par \uc2 Gloria Johnson, who narrowly avoided expulsion after protesting guns on the state house floor, is running to be Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s first Democratic US senator in three decades.\par \par \uc2 Johnson spoke with the Guardian in an interview one week after officially launching her campaign, which is focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and gun reform.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee voters have almost exclusively elected Republicans for national office over the last two decades. But in March, a shooting at a Christian school in Nashville that left three children and three adults dead galvanized voters on both sides of the aisle to demand stricter gun laws in their state, where they are among the weakest in the nation.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Johnson, alongside Justin Pearson and Justin Jones, both Democratic state representatives, took to the house floor, linking arms and leading chants for gun safety, famously earning the nickname the \u8220\'20\'1CTennessee three\u8221\'20\'1D. Pearson and Jones, who are Black, were expelled by the Republican-dominated legislature for their protest while Johnson, who is white, kept her seat by a single vote.\par \par \uc2 Johnson said that her survival in the statehouse \u8220\'20\'1Cabsolutely\u8221\'20\'1D had something to do with the color of her skin: \u8220\'20\'1CMy two young colleagues, Representative Pearson and Representative Jones, get the worst part of it. And to the tune of \u8211\'20\'13 they have been called worthless, and things like that, by their colleagues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said the moment, however, also served as a crucial window for voters into the divisiveness that in recent years has plagued the Tennessee house of representatives, which currently seats 75 Republicans and 24 Democrats.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like the big windows around the capitol burst open and sunlight poured in, and all of Tennessee got to get a really close look at what was happening in their legislature,\u8221\'20\'1D said Johnson, referring to the expulsion votes. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople saw that they were silencing voices and they were killing legislation just because it came from someone across the aisle, not because it was bad legislation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At 6ft 3in (1.91 meters), Johnson has a voice that is naturally booming. She\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cbig into music\u8221\'20\'1D and said she would have loved to meet the late country musician Roger Miller, whom she praised for his songwriting and sense of humor. She speaks with a southern drawl, and chats candidly about getting teased as a kid for her height.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll through school, and even some adults did it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Johnson. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I was pretty tough and let it, you know, just roll off my shoulder.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her stature is even woven into her campaign slogan, \u8220\'20\'1CStanding Tall for Tennessee\u8221\'20\'1D, which she says is her attitude heading into the election. \u8220\'20\'1CYou just have to stand up and speak up. And if we don\u8217\'20\'19t do that, nothing\u8217\'20\'19s going to change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In her video announcing her campaign, Johnson targets \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2 , the incumbent Republican, who was elected to the Senate in 2018 and is up for re-election in 2024. Blackburn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wbir.com/article/news/local/10listens/10-listens-tennessee-lawmaker-nra-funding/51-56d066aa-02c1-4c93-8050-c06eec0c9699"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ranks 12th}}}\uc2  among lawmakers who\u8217\'20\'19ve received campaign contributions by the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  (\uc2 NRA\uc2 ), according to the non-profit OpenSecrets, and supports a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which Johnson strongly opposes.\par \par \uc2 For the lawmaker, reproductive rights are deeply personal. Johnson, 61, has Marfan syndrome, a genetic disorder that affects connective tissue and is associated with a high rate of complications during pregnancy. At 21, she had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after suffering an aortic aneurysm while pregnant, following a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s recommendation, she said.\par \par \uc2 You just have to stand up and speak up. And if we don\u8217\'20\'19t do that, nothing\u8217\'20\'19s going to change\par \par \uc2 Gloria Johnson\par \par \uc2 That might not have been possible under current state laws, Johnson said. Tennessee has one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the nation, with extremely few exceptions. In April, \uc2 Bill Lee\uc2 , the Republican governor, signed into law an amendment allowing doctors to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the life of the patient \u8211\'20\'13 but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains banned in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have survived for very long on my own, and carrying a pregnancy would have been even worse. There was no way I was going to survive that aneurysm while carrying a pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Johnson. \u8220\'20\'1CTo think that women today wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have that option, or a doctor would be afraid to make the choice he knew he needed to make because of a felony that allowed 15 years in prison, this is outrageous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Johnson was first elected to the Tennessee house of representatives in 2012 after spending 27 years as a special education teacher, when she mostly worked with teenagers dealing with behavioral and emotional issues, she said. She narrowly lost re-election bids to Republican challenger Eddie Smith in 2014 and 2016 before defeating him in 2018 by more than 10 percentage points. She won again in 2020, and in a new district in 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s such a huge difference between the first time I went in, in 2013, to now,\u8221\'20\'1D said Johnson, referring to rising polarization in the Republican-led state legislature. \u8220\'20\'1CWe could get along, and even if we disagreed \u8211\'20\'13 which we did on some things vehemently \u8211\'20\'13 we could joke around and have conversation, but that\u8217\'20\'19s just really gone for the most part now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Before Johnson has a chance to face Blackburn in the general election, she\u8217\'20\'19ll face Marquita Bradshaw, a Democratic challenger who in 2020 was the only Black woman to win the party nomination for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  but lost to Republican \uc2 Bill Hagerty\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When asked what makes her best poised for the party nomination, Johnson said she feels she is well known statewide after entering politics a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just think I\u8217\'20\'19m gonna be able to bring more people out to vote, and to rally people in all of these other counties where we have the ability to flip seats this time,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Charlane Oliver, a state senator who is co-chairing Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s US Senate campaign alongside Justin Pearson, agreed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am not interested in making this campaign about a Black woman against a white woman,\u8221\'20\'1D said Oliver. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is about who is the best candidate to beat \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2 . And I personally am interested in winning.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Johnson is well-acquainted with Republican antics. Cameron Sexton, the Tennessee house speaker, re-assigned her to a closet-sized office for a year, which she guessed was retaliation for refusing to vote for him, the only member to oppose his re-election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s not even 6ft for me to social distance. If someone comes in to visit me in my office, there\u8217\'20\'19s not 6ft,\u8221\'20\'1D said Johnson at the time. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m also six three with a 38-inch inseam, so come on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Johnson is also familiar with Republican-led redistricting, which compelled her to run in a new district in 2022. Voting rights groups are suing over the state\u8217\'20\'19s redrawn congressional maps, which created an additional Republican-leaning district while taking one from Democrats, saying they dilute the voting power of Black Tennesseans.\par \par \uc2 But if there\u8217\'20\'19s anything Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s record has proven so far, her allies say, it\u8217\'20\'19s that she runs into these systemic challenges headfirst.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe made the sacrifice to move to another district, and she won \u8211\'20\'13 again. Who else has said they\u8217\'20\'19ve done that?\u8221\'20\'1D Oliver said. \u8220\'20\'1CShe will actually fight for the people and is not concerned about how this benefits her personally.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230925ej9p002bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
