\par\par \uc2 Television & radio\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18You want to think America is better\u8217\'20\'19: can the supreme court be saved?\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 2,148 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2023\par 15:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Documentarian Dawn Porter\u8217\'20\'19s expansive new series Deadlocked looks back at the history of an institution that has shaped the US for better and, more recently, worse\par \par \uc2 When {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.trilogy-films.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dawn Porter}}}\uc2  studied law at \uc2 Georgetown University in Washington\uc2 , she would pass the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  every day. \u8220\'20\'1CYou walk by the marble columns, the frontage which has inspirational words, and you believe that,\u8221\'20\'1D she recalls. \u8220\'20\'1CYou think because of this court Black people integrated schools, because of this court women have the right to choose, because of this court, because of this court, because of this court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Its profound role in American life is chronicled in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sho.com/deadlocked-how-america-shaped-the-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deadlocked: How America Shaped the Supreme Court}}}\uc2 , Porter\u8217\'20\'19s four-part documentary series that traces the people, decisions and confirmation battles that have helped the court\u8217\'20\'19s relationship with politics turn from a respectful dance into a toxic marriage.\par \par \uc2 Porter, 57, an Emmy award winner who maintains her bar licence, remembers first year common law classes when she studied the court\u8217\'20\'19s landmark decisions. \u8220\'20\'1CLike most lawyers I have a great admiration for not only what the court can do but its role in shaping American opinion as well as American society,\u8221\'20\'1D she says via Zoom from New York, a poster for her film {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.johnlewisgoodtrouble.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Lewis: Good Trouble}}}\uc2  behind her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf there\u8217\'20\'19s a criticism of the court in this series, it comes from a place of longing, a place of saying we can\u8217\'20\'19t afford for this court to lose the respect of the American people. There\u8217\'20\'19s going to be decisions over time that people disagree with. That\u8217\'20\'19s not unusual. What\u8217\'20\'19s unusual is how cases are getting to the court, how they\u8217\'20\'19re ignoring precedent and the procedures by which the decisions are getting made. That\u8217\'20\'19s where I would love people to focus.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Deadlocked offers a visual montage of the court winding back in time: women and people of colour gradually disappear in favour of an all-white, all-male bench. They include Chief Justice Earl Warren, who heralded an era of progressive legal decisions such as Brown v Board of Education, a unanimous 1954 ruling that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/education/2023/feb/20/us-school-segregation-race-integration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 desegregated public schools}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Porter says of the paradox: \u8220\'20\'1COne of the things we were thinking is, isn\u8217\'20\'19t it ironic that this all-male, all-white court is responsible for Brown v Board and for Roe v Wade [which enshrined the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] and you have the right to an attorney, which is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.uscourts.gov/educational-resources/educational-activities/facts-and-case-summary-gideon-v-wainwright"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gideon v Wainwright}}}\uc2 , and you have the right to have your rights read to you. Yet when we have the most diverse court we\u8217\'20\'19ve ever had, we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing a rollback of some of these civil rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 1967, President Lyndon Johnson nominated the civil rights lawyer Thurgood Marshall to be the first Black man to serve on the court. A group of southern senators, almost all Democrats, sought to exploit riots in the major cities and fears about crime to try to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/ketanji-brown-jackson-race-and-ethnicity-racial-injustice-jackson-crime-e20eb531b5ff3d17544a79d7db33cafd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 derail his nomination}}}\uc2 . Marshall endured five days of questioning spanning three weeks and was finally confirmed by the Senate in a 69-11 vote.\par \par \uc2 There have only been two African American justices since: conservative \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and liberal \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . The first woman to sit on the court was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2018/oct/22/sandra-day-oconnor-first-woman-on-supreme-court-withdraws-from-public-life"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sandra Day O\u8217\'20\'19Connor}}}\uc2 , a moderate conservative appointed by the Republican president \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt takes a century of supreme court jurisprudence before we get a woman on the court. There\u8217\'20\'19s an irony there that we have the current composition of the court and yet we have probably one of the most least hospitable courts to individual rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s relationship with public opinion has been complex, leading at some times, following at others. In 2015, it ruled that same-sex couples {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2015/jun/26/supreme-court-same-sex-marriage-history-activism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had the right to marry}}}\uc2 . The 5-4 decision removed same-sex marriage bans in 14 states \u8211\'20\'13 an acknowledgment of shifting attitudes and the rise of the LGBTQ+ rights movement.\par \par \uc2 Porter observes: \u8220\'20\'1CThe court doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have an army. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t even have PR or a media representative. The supreme court can\u8217\'20\'19t change public opinion but what the court can do is either set an aspirational goal or it can reflect where the country is. For the gay marriage decision, that\u8217\'20\'19s where the country was. The country was supportive of same-sex marriage and the court ratifies that public opinion and makes it law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Opinion polls show that a majority of Americans have also consistently supported reproductive rights. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade in 1973}}}\uc2 , the court voted 7-2 that the constitution protects individual privacy, including the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Porter observes: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not that controversial a decision by that time. More than half the states had reproductive rights access so it was only going to affect some of the states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the time, Christian evangelicals were not opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. \u8220\'20\'1CEvangelicals historically were pro-choice. This is where politics comes in and is on this collision course with the judiciary. Evangelical leaders like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.liberty.edu/about/founder-jerry-falwell-senior/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jerry Falwell}}}\uc2  realised, oh, wait, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a wedge issue and there are all these Catholic voters. So they come together.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat the evangelicals want is tax exemption for religious schools. The Catholics don\u8217\'20\'19t want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and together they\u8217\'20\'19re a powerful voting bloc. They not only say we\u8217\'20\'19re going to try and get the supreme court to change but we\u8217\'20\'19re going to elect a president who is going to help us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 These religious groups duly turned against the Democratic president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/oct/18/jonathan-alter-jimmy-carter-donald-trump-reagan-bush-clinton-obama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jimmy Carter}}}\uc2 , an evangelical Sunday school teacher, in favour of the divorced former Hollywood actor Reagan. Porter continues: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat you see is kind of politics at work. How can we get power? How can we get what we want? How can we form alliances?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat alliance is very powerful because Reagan ends up having so many appointments to the court and you see the rightward shift of the court. These kinds of monumental changes don\u8217\'20\'19t happen quickly but building blocks are constructed in these earlier years, like in the 80s, and they\u8217\'20\'19ve continued to this day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s role as a political actor was never more stark than in 2000, when its ruling in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2000/dec/14/uselections2000.usa5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bush v Gore}}}\uc2  terminated the recount process in Florida in the presidential election, effectively handing the White House to \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 . Porter notes: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s 5-4 to step in and stop the voting to determine who would be the next president of the United States. \uc2 Sandra Day O\u8217\'20\'19Connor\uc2  later said she regretted voting with the majority.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlso, interestingly, Justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/03/opinion/john-roberts-supreme-court.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Roberts}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  are all working with the Republicans on the side of soon-to-be President Bush. Is that illegal? No. Is it impermissible? No. Is it unethical? No. Is it interesting? Yes!\u8221\'20\'1D Porter says with a laugh.\par \par \uc2 But the ever-growing politicisation of the court became turbocharged \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps irreversibly \u8211\'20\'13 by the death of the conservative justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  in 2016. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , then Republican majority leader in the Senate, committed a professional foul by refusing to act on President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2016/mar/16/obama-nominates-merrick-garland-supreme-court-dc-appeals-court-judge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nomination of Merrick Garland}}}\uc2  to replace him, insisting that the seat remain vacant in an election year.\par \par \uc2 Step forward \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Republican nominee for president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2016-election/trump-releases-list-potential-supreme-court-nominees-n576271"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who released a list}}}\uc2  of 11 potential supreme court nominees based on advice from conservative groups such as the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  and \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 . It was an unprecedented political masterstroke that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/05/trump-abortion-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comforted religious conservatives}}}\uc2  troubled by his unholy antics and past support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 McConnell is seen in Deadlocked asserting that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe single biggest issue that brought nine out of 10 Republican voters home to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8230\'20\'26 was the supreme court\u8221\'20\'1D. This clip is from an address he made in 2019 to the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , which has played a critical role in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/apr/29/trump-seizing-courts-democratic-win-november-could-stop-him"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tilting the court to the right}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The group was founded in 1982 under the mentorship of Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  to challenge what conservatives perceived as liberal dominance of courts and law schools. Among its most prominent members was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/06/leonard-leo-watchdog-complaint-investigation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Leonard Leo}}}\uc2 , who oversaw the rise in its influence at the expense of the more liberal \uc2 American Bar Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Porter says: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  is one of the most fascinating and yet not widely known political actors in our contemporary history. \uc2 The Federalist Society\uc2  realises: we can have influence in grooming judges and who\u8217\'20\'19s getting appointed to the lower courts. \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  takes that on steroids and eventually becomes the person who former president Trump looks to create his list of potential supreme court nominees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn recent years Leo has secured a multibillion-dollar war chest in order to continue to groom and populate the lower courts with very conservative ideologues. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/oct/26/amy-coney-barretts-past-calls-into-question-her-pledges-of-impartiality"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett}}}\uc2  is a product of that. Kavanaugh is a product of that. All the greatest hits are with \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  influence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sheldon Whitehouse, a Democratic senator, has called it \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/feb/22/the-scheme-senators-highlight-rightwing-influence-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the scheme}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D: a decades-long plot by rightwing donor interests to capture the supreme court and use it to accomplish goals that they cannot achieve through elected officials. \uc2 The Federalist Society\uc2  is a receptacle for \u8220\'20\'1Cdark money\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 millions of dollars in anonymous hidden spending.\par \par \uc2 Porter adds: \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem with private entities like the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  having so much influence and power is that there\u8217\'20\'19s no insight into the source of their funds. We certainly do know that it\u8217\'20\'19s not a coincidence that some of the interests of some of the most conservative folks seem to be being served by these appointments.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last year the rightwing forces achieved their greatest victory with a decision that once seemed unthinkable: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/10/divided-america-roe-v-wade-supreme-court-cold-civil-war"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the overturning of Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  after nearly half a century. Most Republican-led states moved to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with 14 banning the procedure in most cases at any point in pregnancy. About 25 million women of childbearing age now live in states where the law makes abortions harder to get than they were before the ruling.\par \par \uc2 Porter had wanted to believe the court she admired as a student was a bulwark in defence of individual liberties. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery pundit, every organisation, said Roe is going to be overturned and yet it was still hard to believe that 50 years later, when so many people rely on that decision, that it actually could be overturned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will say it really did personally impact my feeling about the court. Reading the decision, there\u8217\'20\'19s ignoring of history. It\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Legal-Analysis-What-Dobbs-Got-Wrong-3.15.23.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not a well-written opinion}}}\uc2 , it\u8217\'20\'19s not coherent, and that\u8217\'20\'19s really hard. We all need to believe in things and we all need to believe that these are the smartest people and that they\u8217\'20\'19re able to put aside their personal beliefs and that didn\u8217\'20\'19t seem to be the case.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was more than disappointing. It\u8217\'20\'19s somewhat comforting that we have such a strong reaction to it but I see the cases of the women who have been so harmed by this decision. There are people have been forced to carry pregnancies to term that were not viable, people who just stay pregnant who didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to be pregnant. You want to think America is better than that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As the final episode of Deadlocked acknowledges, the court faces a crisis of legitimacy. A series of extremist rulings out of whack with public opinion have come at the same time as ethics scandals involving the rightwing justices Thomas and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 . The share of Americans with a favourable opinion of the court has declined to its lowest point in public opinion surveys since 1987: 44% favourable versus 54% unfavourable, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2023/07/21/favorable-views-of-supreme-court-fall-to-historic-low/#:~:text=Fewer%20than%20half%20of%20Americans,Americans%20had%20a%20favorable%20impression."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Pew Research Center}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Porter adds: \u8220\'20\'1CEvery single person we spoke to for this series regardless of their political background \u8211\'20\'13 and we have Scalia\u8217\'20\'19s former clerk, who wrote the decision broadening access to guns; we have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gibsondunn.com/lawyer/olson-theodore-b/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ted Olson}}}\uc2 , who argued Bush v Gore for President Bush; we have Don Ayer, who was a Reagan justice department official \u8211\'20\'13 is concerned about the reputation of the court and what the future holds if the court continues to chart its own path and not realise the delicate balance of our tripartite system of government.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat if the court sides with a Trump who refuses to accept the results of the election next year? That\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about and a lot of the people who did the insurrection are still out there; we didn\u8217\'20\'19t arrest them all. We\u8217\'20\'19re in uncharted waters. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a game and I don\u8217\'20\'19t think anyone wants to actually put this to the test of: will our democracy survive?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sho.com/deadlocked-how-america-shaped-the-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deadlocked: How America Shaped the Supreme Court}}}\uc2  premieres on Showtime on 22 September with a UK date to be announced\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230921ej9l0020t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
