\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Our health data is about to flow more freely, like it or not\u8217\'20\'19: big tech\u8217\'20\'19s plans for the \uc2 NHS\b0\par\par\uc2 Cori Crider \par \uc2 5,089 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par 15:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The government is about to award a \u163\'00\'A3480m contract to build a vast new database of patient data. But if people don\u8217\'20\'19t trust it, they\u8217\'20\'19ll opt out \u8211\'20\'13 I know, because I felt I had to\par \par \uc2 Last December, I had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Most unwanted pregnancies set a panic-timer ticking, but I was almost counting down the seconds until I had to catch a flight from London, where I live, to Texas, where I grew up, and where the provision of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care was recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/25/us-abortion-trigger-bans-laws-tennessee-texas-idaho"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 made a felony}}}\uc2 . You bleed for a while after most abortions, and I was still bleeding when I boarded the plane home for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Going to Texas so soon after the procedure made me consider where the record of my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 my health data \u8211\'20\'13 would end up. When I phoned an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in late November to book an appointment, one of the first questions staff asked was: \u8220\'20\'1CMay we share a record of your treatment with your GP?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I hesitated. There\u8217\'20\'19s nothing wrong, in principle, with this question, and a lot that\u8217\'20\'19s right. It\u8217\'20\'19s not just that a complete health record helps my GP treat me. My Texan parents, both scientists, taught me that sharing information with organisations like the \uc2 NHS\uc2  can help it plan services and research ways to improve care. I\u8217\'20\'19ve joined clinical studies in the past.\par \par \uc2 But I also help run a legal campaign group, Foxglove, that takes action against the government and tech companies when they infringe people\u8217\'20\'19s rights. In a series of cases about \uc2 NHS\uc2  data since the start of the pandemic, we have defended people\u8217\'20\'19s right to a say about who sees their medical information. This work has exposed me to worrying details about how our medical data can be used, including the Home Office practice of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2023/aug/29/migrants-home-office-reference-number-nhs-england-records"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tracking migrants}}}\uc2  using their health records.\par \par \uc2 NHS\uc2  data is special. For decades the government has required GPs to store patients\u8217\'20\'19 records in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://digital.nhs.uk/services/terminology-and-classifications/snomed-ct"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 standardised way}}}\uc2  : as well as longhand notes, every interaction with a GP is saved on a computer database in a simple, consistent code. The \uc2 NHS\uc2  may hold the richest set of population-wide, machine-readable health data in the world. Many see in that data a source of immense potential \u8211\'20\'13 and profit. \uc2 Ernst & Young\uc2  has valued \uc2 NHS\uc2  patient data at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.digitalhealth.net/2019/07/nhs-data-worth-9-6bn-per-year-says-ernst-young/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u163\'00\'A39.6bn a year}}}\uc2 . It is particularly valuable to tech giants, who would like to get their hands on \uc2 NHS\uc2  datasets to build AI machine-learning systems.\par \par \uc2 As things stand today, I believe my local GP would safeguard the record of my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . By Christmas next year? I\u8217\'20\'19m not so sure. It may no longer be up to them. The government is seeking to overhaul the way it handles every health record in England, and its plans have filled some healthcare workers with alarm.\par \par \uc2 There is clearly a need to make patient data more consistent and accessible across the \uc2 NHS\uc2 . At the moment, caregivers in one part of the \uc2 NHS\uc2  often can\u8217\'20\'19t access the records of care their patients received elsewhere. Imagine you have had knee pain for years. Doctors want to operate. At various times you have seen your GP, and been to a hospital in south London, but the surgery is due to be carried out in a different borough. (This kind of thing happens all the time.) A doctor is trying to judge how risky your operation is, to decide if you can go to a health centre, where you might be seen sooner and released quicker, or must be sent to a hospital.\par \par \uc2 Because your health data is held in many places \u8211\'20\'13 at your GP and across various hospitals where you have been \u8211\'20\'13 there is not one complete picture of you as a patient they can use to check. Of course the clinician will ask about your health before the operation; but you may not remember, or even know, every detail that could affect how your surgery could go. If this data was better shared, they could create a clearer picture of your health. Without this information, their assessment of your risk for surgery is, at best, an educated guess.\par \par \uc2 This partial view of the patient is commonplace in the \uc2 NHS\uc2 , and it is one reason the government is trying to create a vast new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.digitalhealth.net/2023/05/nhs-england-says-all-nhs-trusts-to-get-own-fdp/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NHS England database}}}\uc2 , known as the federated data platform (FDP). (Health data is managed nationally, so this system is England-only.) If data from the nation\u8217\'20\'19s hospitals, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theregister.com/2023/06/23/nhs_data_platform/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 GPs}}}\uc2  and social care were fed into a single system, accessible in one place to health service doctors and planners, it could potentially help planners by showing trends across regions, and the population as a whole. The contract to build and run the platform is worth a huge sum \u8211\'20\'13 \u163\'00\'A3480m over five years \u8211\'20\'13 with the winning bidder expected to be announced any day.\par \par \uc2 Putting so much data under central control may increase efficiency \u8211\'20\'13 but it also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.eu/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/16/Foxglove-Palantir-report.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 risks failing}}}\uc2  because of poor consultation and low patient trust. Many would prefer that \uc2 NHS England\uc2  invested in its own capacity, instead of farming out to private enterprise. The frontrunner for the contract to run the FDP is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2022/nov/13/controversial-360m-nhs-england-data-platform-lined-up-for-trump-backers-firm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US tech firm Palantir}}}\uc2 , which has performed data analytics work for the US security services, border forces and police. If you don\u8217\'20\'19t know \uc2 Palantir\uc2 , you may be familiar with the company\u8217\'20\'19s chair, \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2 , a tech billionaire and Trump supporter, who has funded anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  candidates and invested in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thedailybeast.com/peter-thiel-funds-recharge-capital-targeting-womens-health-and-fertility"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anti-birth-control startups}}}\uc2 . Contacted for this article, \uc2 Palantir\uc2  emphasised that it is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot in the business of mining data, nor do we sell or monetise it in any way. What we do is provide tools that help customers understand and organise the information that they hold.\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 NHS England\uc2  said that it, not the platform, will control the patient data inside it. It also points to contractual safeguards \u8220\'20\'1Cto prevent any supplier gaining a dominant role in \uc2 NHS\uc2  data management\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Still, the FDP gives government a lot of power over health data \u8211\'20\'13 and it hasn\u8217\'20\'19t always earned our trust. In 2019, the head of \uc2 NHS England\uc2  and other top health officials discussed nine \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theregister.com/2019/12/12/nhs_england_database/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 commercial models}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for access to the nation\u8217\'20\'19s health data with tech and drug executives. A bill currently going through parliament, the data protection and digital information bill, would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/aug/06/uk-data-bill-favours-big-business-and-shady-tech-firms-rights-group-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 significantly dilute}}}\uc2  some of the laws that protect patient privacy. Data protection law \u8211\'20\'13 including the EU 2018 General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and its UK equivalent \u8211\'20\'13 limits what can be done with sensitive data such as health records. We mostly associate GDPR with infuriating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/askjack/2018/jul/05/what-should-i-do-about-all-the-gdpr-pop-ups-on-websites"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 website pop-ups}}}\uc2 , but it is a useful safeguard. It was one of the reasons the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic needed my permission to share my medical record with anyone, including my own doctor.\par \par \uc2 One of the most important of the proposed changes is the move to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/bills/cbill/58-03/0265/220265v2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 redefine what counts}}}\uc2  as personal data. Through a process of \u8220\'20\'1Cpseudonymisation\u8221\'20\'1D, the system can detach your name and other identifying details. But this process is imperfect and reversible \u8211\'20\'13 and patients consistently say they want control over how their health data is used. If the government amends or interprets data protection law to take this choice away from us, it is hard to see how patient confidentiality as we know it will still exist. Our health records are precious to us. We come to the doctor as supplicants. We share worry, fear, pain. Our ability to do so depends on trust, which itself depends on the guarantee of privacy.\par \par \uc2 After a summer of strikes over pay and conditions, everyone can see that the \uc2 NHS\uc2  urgently needs more resources. The \uc2 NHS\uc2  could use our data \u8211\'20\'13 with strict safeguards \u8211\'20\'13 to streamline clinical decision-making and take pressure off doctors and nurses. But the government\u8217\'20\'19s weakening of data protection rules, set against its cosying up to tech industry, is worrying. In the US in 2021, \uc2 Google\uc2  won a deal to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theverge.com/2021/5/26/22454817/google-hca-patient-data-healthcare-algorithms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access patient records}}}\uc2  at more than 2,000 clinics to develop healthcare algorithms. \uc2 Google\uc2 , like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 IBM\uc2 , seeks to access patient records at scale in order to train algorithms to develop apps or organise care. In this troubling scenario, my pain, and yours, may become training data for someone else\u8217\'20\'19s AI.\par \par \uc2 All this lingered in my mind on the call with the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI asked, was it all right if we shared this record with your doctor?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m sorry, but no,\u8221\'20\'1D I said.\par \par \uc2 Having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the most isolating experience of my adult life. It taught me how stigma lingers over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even in the UK, where treatment is widely available. It also changed how I think about health data: not only mine, but that of my family in Texas, and of patients across the UK. It spurred me to reflect on what is at stake if these changes to patient data go ahead.\par \par \uc2 With the drive towards {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/jun/26/ai-personalise-medicine-patient-lab-health-diagnosis-cambridge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AI in healthcare}}}\uc2  (from triage to diagnosis to drug research), we in England face a choice: will we privilege care and the public sector, or profit? People in the UK treasure the sanctity of our health record \u8211\'20\'13 we want control over who sees it, when, and why. What will it mean when we no longer believe a conversation with an \uc2 NHS\uc2  doctor is private?\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 As I was flicking through \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on the early morning train to the clinic, a promoted post I\u8217\'20\'19d never seen before popped up. It was a jokey meme showing a contraceptive pill pack, with the line: \u8220\'20\'1CTake your magic pills ladies, you don\u8217\'20\'19t want any Baby Jesuses for Christmas.\u8221\'20\'1D Living under surveillance capitalism makes you paranoid. I snapped a screenshot, sent it to two female friends on Signal, then panicked, deleted the picture and turned my phone off.\par \par \uc2 It was 8am when I sat down at the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Ealing, west London, where a range of women awaited their appointments. Some wore fur coats, others tracksuits and false eyelashes; a few had been crying. A middle-aged woman, who looked as if she was rushing out to her next meeting, just seemed very pissed off. The only man in the waiting room was a teenager who had come along with his girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 At first I thought my reluctance to share my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  record had mainly to do with data. I later realised it also had to do with shame. A cloud hangs over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There is a strong {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msichoices.org.uk/news/blog-smashing-six-abortion-myths/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 residual stereotype}}}\uc2  of the woman who aborts: someone young, a bit careless, who knows no better.\par \par \uc2 In the parallel system you enter in England to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the language reveals a world shadowed by female vulnerability and male violence. I could hear it when I phoned the clinic: \u8220\'20\'1CAre you alone?\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CAre you safe?\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CPlease choose a password and a memorable number; we will only use these to contact you about your treatment.\u8221\'20\'1D The clinic never used the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D by text or email. All this signals that you have ventured to a space outside normal healthcare. Nobody hassled me outside the clinic that day, but I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen the protesters with signs outside another London clinic.\par \par \uc2 Go for surgery, a friend at a party had whispered; the pills cause far more pain. I remembered lying on the bathroom floor a decade ago during a miscarriage, the weeks of bleeding and pain, and decided to investigate. At the clinic, a seasoned Danish nurse told me I needed an internal ultrasound to assess possible treatments. I asked how soon I could have the surgery. The nurse said there would be a wait, until just before I was due to go to Texas, where there would be no aftercare.\par \par \uc2 It was only then, when I was lying on the table and the nurse prodded my ovaries and my vagina, that the sobs came. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want another child. But bodies tend to hold on to the remnants of pregnancies, miscarriages and births; I remembered watching other ultrasounds: the child who developed and the child who did not. I lay on the shore of life and death and felt utterly alone.\par \par \uc2 The nurse told me it was early, about six weeks, and handed me a tissue. (Six weeks is the criminal threshold in Texas.) She had had this conversation thousands of times. \u8220\'20\'1CWere you hoping I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t see anything?\u8221\'20\'1D No, I blubbered, I am just tired. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t mention my rage: at every Baptist or Lutheran or Methodist minister in the small town where I was raised, at Texas, at myself. It was clear the surgery would be too late, as I had to fly to the US. Before leaving the clinic I took the first lot of pills.\par \par \uc2 You may support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on principle, as I do. You may also, as I do, admit privately that you find its ethics ambiguous. Having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  taught me that a woman\u8217\'20\'19s ability to sustain or end life is simultaneously an awful burden and an awful power. Mulling over the complexity of it all now seems like wallowing in a warm bath. When the reality appears, you are thrown in a cold river. The cells are dividing; a choice must be made. Swim on, or turn back? I felt the power and I hated and feared it, and I realised why so many men hate it too, and seek to control it.\par \par \uc2 In most of the UK, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still a technically a crime. The 1967 \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/aug/19/abortion-legal-great-britain-women-life-sentences-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 does not legalise \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  ; it excuses it if a doctor considers that pregnancy (before 24 weeks) poses a risk to the mental or physical health of the mother. (Northern Ireland is different, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal on demand to 12 weeks.) This teaches women that to access care they must construct a narrative that continuing the pregnancy would be damaging to mother or baby, or both.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps that\u8217\'20\'19s why I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want the clinic to share my data. Having reduced a complex experience into a just-so story once, I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want that story to be fed into a mass data system that could, I worried, one day be shared with the health division of \uc2 Google\uc2 , or the German drug giant Merck, or some other company. I saw my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as my story, and not one from which I was keen to permit any corporation, however modestly, to profit.\par \par \uc2 You emerge from this ordeal with a story you guard fiercely. This doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean you don\u8217\'20\'19t tell it to others: it means you want absolute authority to tell the story as and when you see fit. It was months before I told my Texan family about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; in the service of a wider point, I am writing about it now.\par \par \uc2 In German legal language, this is \u8220\'20\'1Cinformational self-determination\u8221\'20\'1D, or informationelle Selbstbestimmung. This idea means something slightly different to me from privacy. Privacy connotes a turning away, a hiding. But I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to hide my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : I wanted agency over when the story was told. Real data protection means you deal with your record on your terms.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 In some places, the data trails we leave have become a way to criminalise us. Earlier this year, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/20/us/celeste-burgess-abortion-pill-nebraska.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heartrending photo}}}\uc2  from Nebraska showed a girl of 19 sobbing as she was escorted from court after being sentenced to 90 days in prison after having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the start of her third trimester. Police had obtained her \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages with her mother about organising the pills. Her mother was given five years for procuring the pills and hiding the body.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, over Christmas lunch with my sister and her teenage daughter, I wondered what I would do if they needed help. On what platform could we speak safely? How could I send them information, or bootleg them pills? Texas, like most of the US, has no GDPR equivalent, meaning women have no real control over their data. This allows {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsweek.com/could-period-tracking-apps-dangerous-post-roe-v-wade-us-1704216"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 apps such as period trackers}}}\uc2  to share location and other data with the authorities when the law requires.\par \par \uc2 This summer, a British woman was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/12/woman-in-uk-jailed-for-28-months-over-taking-abortion-pills-after-legal-time-limit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sentenced to prison}}}\uc2  after her search history revealed that, during the pandemic, she had a late-stage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A couple of phrases typed into \uc2 Google\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 one was \u8220\'20\'1CI need to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but I\u8217\'20\'19m past 24 weeks\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 helped to convict her. (The sentence, which drew broad condemnation from MPs, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TheBMA/status/1668349580314828809?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doctors}}}\uc2  and feminist groups, was later suspended, but her criminal record stands.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fundamental thing to understand about \uc2 NHS England\uc2  is that it is the government,\u8221\'20\'1D says Marcus Baw, a GP and IT specialist. By this he means that officials, not clinicians, will make many of the critical decisions about which data flows into the new \uc2 NHS\uc2  platform. These officials, not doctors, will interpret the law about who will have access to it, including other government departments. I have yet to see an official give a clear answer to this question: once a copy of my health data has gone into the central platform, who decides what happens to it? The answer will seriously affect the public\u8217\'20\'19s trust in the scheme.\par \par \uc2 This is not a new problem. In 2013, NHS Digital was set up to develop and operate digital services for \uc2 NHS England\uc2 . Its first chair, Kingsley Manning, stepped down in 2016 because he said he got fed up with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2017/feb/01/home-office-asked-former-nhs-digital-boss-to-share-data-to-trace-immigration-offenders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Home Office demanding}}}\uc2  to see migrants\u8217\'20\'19 health data, to check their location for border enforcement. This, understandably, deterred so many migrants from seeking care that the government had to run an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-55978334"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 information campaign}}}\uc2  during Covid to encourage people who had migrated to Britain to come for their jabs.\par \par \uc2 NHS Digital was also set up to protect patient data, with decisions about data-sharing theoretically insulated from central government control. It was abolished last year, in a move Manning {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bmj.com/content/376/bmj.o361/rr-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 described}}}\uc2  as a \u8220\'20\'1Cretrograde step\u8221\'20\'1D with \u8220\'20\'1Cthe potential for undermining the relationship between clinicians and their patients\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It is jarring to set these serious questions of patient trust against \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s breezy promises, last November, about \u8220\'20\'1Cusing our new Brexit freedoms to create the most pro-innovation regulatory environment in the world in sectors like life sciences, financial services, artificial intelligence and data\u8221\'20\'1D. \uc2 Palantir\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO, \uc2 Alex Karp\uc2 , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailymail.co.uk/news/article-12176069/Boss-major-AI-firm-slaps-BBC-cynicism-salutes-UK-bastion-talent.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 praised}}}\uc2  the UK\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cpragmatic but serious understanding of data protection\u8221\'20\'1D. He recently told the \uc2 BBC\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re a pharmaceutical company and you want to do research on a medicine, you\u8217\'20\'19re going to be able to do things in the UK you would not be able to do easily in the continent.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The winning bidder for the contract to run the federated data platform will soon be announced. The data protection and digital information bill, which weakens individual control over personal data, has had a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://publiclawproject.org.uk/content/uploads/2023/04/PLP-Briefing-DPDI-Bill-No.2-Second-Reading-Final-1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 second reading}}}\uc2 , a key step on its way to passing into law. In the name of progress, our health data is about to flow more freely, like it or not.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 Palantir\uc2  has spent years preparing the ground for its FDP bid. At the start of the pandemic, with the normal procurement rules suspended, \uc2 Palantir\uc2  was given a contract to build a national Covid-19 data store for the \uc2 NHS\uc2  for the nominal fee of \u163\'00\'A31, collecting vast sets of patient data to track the pandemic and, for example, create a list of vulnerable people who needed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.adalovelaceinstitute.org/blog/covid-19-shielded-patient-list-inequalities/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shield}}}\uc2  at home. This data was used to distribute ventilators and manage the vaccine rollout. But the scale of the data collection caused alarm: one Whitehall observer described the level of sensitive patient data being swept into the Covid data store as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/apr/12/uk-government-using-confidential-patient-data-in-coronavirus-response"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unprecedented}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, and claimed there was insufficient regard to privacy and data protection. In December 2020, it was given a two-year, \u163\'00\'A323m contract to continue working on the project. That was renewed for another six months at a cost of \u163\'00\'A311.5m in January this year. Officials had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://digileaders.com/the-power-of-data-in-a-pandemic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 promised to delete}}}\uc2  that data store when the pandemic was over \u8211\'20\'13 instead, \uc2 Palantir\uc2  is being paid \u163\'00\'A324.9m to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theregister.com/2023/06/20/palantir_nhs_england_deal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 migrate}}}\uc2  its data store to the new system. The procurement {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/PRN00389-nhs-federated-data-platform-and-associated-services.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 documents}}}\uc2  describe the purpose of the FDP as \u8220\'20\'1Cto replace the Covid-19 datastore provided by \uc2 Palantir\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. If it\u8217\'20\'19s anything like its predecessor, the central platform of the FDP will retain a copy of at least some patient data for all of England.\par \par \uc2 The rules of the FDP \u8211\'20\'13 the framework for handling patient records \u8211\'20\'13 are crucial. In particular, the public needs to know exactly which parts of their health record are being pooled or accessed nationally. Patients are also likely to ask what specific uses the FDP will be put to, and what may be bolted on later. (The procurement documents for the FDP explicitly say the purposes are set to expand.) Once the FDP is built and a permanent national flow of patient data is established, the main thing standing between that data and other uses is the health minister. There will be information governance advisers, and a watchdog, the national data guardian, but at this scale, these soft power checks and balances will be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2023/may/14/nhs-england-data-privacy-confidentiality-records-addenbrookes-hospital"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 severely tested}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If the data protection and digital information bill passes, there will be even more reason to be concerned about the security of national health data. \u8220\'20\'1CTaken together, this bill maximises data and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medconfidential.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/DPDI-early-june.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 minimises governance}}}\uc2  of data fed to AIs,\u8221\'20\'1D says the privacy group medConfidential. Given Sunak\u8217\'20\'19s stated enthusiasm for the use of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/experts-to-lead-ai-safety-summit-preparations-as-new-funding-announced-to-modernise-healthcare"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AI in healthcare}}}\uc2 , the concern is that a senior official will later seek to open up the data for commercial purposes. Without clear, strong safeguards, more people will opt out, and the FDP may fail.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NHS\uc2  is a boneyard of failed data-centralisation programmes. The first version of a national IT structure for the \uc2 NHS\uc2  was proposed by \uc2 Tony Blair\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s government in 2002. The idea was to replace paper records with a single digital version accessible to patients and doctors. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2010/mar/21/nhs-national-program-problems"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Programme for IT}}}\uc2  ran into serious problems with software, and an over-centralised delivery. It cost more than \u163\'00\'A312bn over 10 years, and it failed partly because the proposed scale of data-sharing was seen as \u8220\'20\'1Chighly problematic\u8221\'20\'1D. In 2014, \uc2 David Cameron\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s government tried again, with a programme called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wired.co.uk/article/care-data-nhs-england-closed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 care.data}}}\uc2 . Again unresolved questions about corporate access, particularly insurance companies, sparked an outcry, provoking more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theregister.com/2016/04/20/one_million_patients_have_opted_out_of_caredata/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a million people to opt out}}}\uc2  of sharing health records. The government eventually gave up and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2016/jul/06/nhs-to-scrap-single-database-of-patients-medical-details"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cancelled the programme}}}\uc2  in 2016. Five years later, the government launched another project, General Practice Data for Planning and Research, to pool the most detailed data the \uc2 NHS\uc2  has: GP records. Following an outcry over government plans to make health data available to private companies, there was another huge wave of opt-outs. In August 2021, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/aug/22/nhs-data-grab-on-hold-as-millions-opt-out"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scheme was paused}}}\uc2 . (My organisation, Foxglove, was involved in a legal case that precipitated the freeze.)\par \par \uc2 This adds up to more than 20 years of health centralisation plans \u8211\'20\'13 costing billions \u8211\'20\'13 and all of it foundered on the shoals of patient trust. Every scandal over centralising patient data also tends to increase the numbers of people exercising their right to \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medconfidential.org/how-to-opt-out/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opt out}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D of sharing.\par \par \uc2 Statistics for the national opt-out serve as a rough measure of patient trust. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a good picture: the number of patients who have opted out has ballooned over the years to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://digital.nhs.uk/dashboards/national-data-opt-out-open-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some 3.3 million}}}\uc2 . (More than a million of those were added during the row over GP records in 2021.)\par \par \uc2 Opting out is currently the only practical way to register disapproval with state health plans, but it\u8217\'20\'19s a blunt instrument. If you don\u8217\'20\'19t want to give \uc2 Google\uc2  access to healthcare records to develop its algorithms, for example, or support the tobacco firm Philip Morris to develop {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/09/16/1037845804/philip-morris-asthma-inhaler-company-health-tobacco"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inhalers}}}\uc2 , you\u8217\'20\'19re also refusing positive uses of health data \u8211\'20\'13 say, helping \uc2 NHS\uc2  wards to plan ahead for numbers of patients vulnerable to flu. The current rate of opt-out is \u8220\'20\'1Cincredibly problematic\u8221\'20\'1D, warns Dr Jess Morley, a researcher at \uc2 Oxford University\uc2  who has worked on patient data systems, because it makes the dataset \u8220\'20\'1Cmore random\u8221\'20\'1D and damages the value of the health data to the \uc2 NHS\uc2 . But to get these 3.3 million people to opt back in, the state will have to persuade them of its intentions \u8211\'20\'13 and its competence.\par \par \uc2 Recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.eu/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/16/Foxglove-Palantir-report.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polling}}}\uc2  by \uc2 YouGov\uc2  that Foxglove organised for a report also suggests that if the FDP were to go ahead in a form that is both managed by and accessible to private companies, around 48% of the residual population of England who have not opted out will do so. Anything like that figure will be disastrous for the \uc2 NHS\uc2 , because it would make the data useless.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 Palantir\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only contender for the platform. A team at \uc2 Oxford University\uc2  developed an open-source system called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opensafely.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 openSafely}}}\uc2  to support researchers during the pandemic. The system was secure and transparent \u8211\'20\'13 every question the system is asked is logged, and all approved projects are published. But it seems no one asked openSafely to help set up the FDP. There were other bidders, from Oracle Cerner to \uc2 IBM\uc2  to a \u8220\'20\'1Cbest of British\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/11f250df-5125-4bab-bbee-3c4324685c55"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 consortium}}}\uc2  of small UK companies and universities \u8211\'20\'13 but it\u8217\'20\'19s not clear any of them had a serious shot. By the time the procurement opened in January, \uc2 Palantir\uc2  had its feet under the \uc2 NHS\uc2  table.\par \par \uc2 Palantir\uc2  seems an odd choice of partner for the \uc2 NHS\uc2 . Originally {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2020/09/inside-palantir-technologies-peter-thiel-alex-karp.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 funded}}}\uc2  by the CIA\u8217\'20\'19s venture capital arm, \uc2 In-Q-Tel\uc2 , its early corporate history mainly involves supporting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theintercept.com/2017/02/22/how-peter-thiels-palantir-helped-the-nsa-spy-on-the-whole-world/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mass surveillance}}}\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wired.com/story/palantirs-gods-eye-view-of-afghanistan/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wars}}}\uc2  in Iraq and Afghanistan. In 2021, \uc2 Palantir\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CTO, \uc2 Shyam Sankar\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://washingtontechnology.com/2021/02/palantir-details-new-phase-of-federal-strategy/355117/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  he hoped to see \u8220\'20\'1Csomething that looks like \uc2 Palantir\uc2  inside of every missile, inside of every drone\u8221\'20\'1D. (The company notes its services are also used by Ukrainian armed forces to resist Russian aggression, and by the \uc2 World Food Programme\uc2 .) \uc2 Palantir\uc2  helped the US and UK\u8217\'20\'19s digital spy agencies (NSA and GCHQ) manage mass surveillance programmes such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/jul/31/nsa-top-secret-program-online-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 XKeyscore}}}\uc2 , a system that tracked millions of people online. In Los Angeles, the police have used \uc2 Palantir\uc2  to build a tool, Laser, that claimed it would \u8220\'20\'1Cextract\u8221\'20\'1D suspected offenders from the community like \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theintercept.com/2018/05/11/predictive-policing-surveillance-los-angeles/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tumors}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D until community organisers forced the \uc2 LAPD\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/nov/07/lapd-predictive-policing-surveillance-reform"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scrap it}}}\uc2 . Not once have I seen a senior British official publicly reflect on how this history may affect trust in public healthcare among, say, migrant groups, or Muslims.\par \par \uc2 Palantir\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s values appear to sit uncomfortably with the \uc2 NHS\uc2 . In January this year, Thiel described Britons\u8217\'20\'19 affection for the \uc2 NHS\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-01-18/peter-thiel-says-british-affection-for-nhs-is-stockholm-syndrome?leadSource=uverify%20wall"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stockholm syndrome}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D and said the best thing to do would be to \u8220\'20\'1Crip the whole thing from the ground and start over\u8221\'20\'1D. The company quickly distanced itself from its chairman\u8217\'20\'19s remarks, which they said he made as a private individual.\par \par \uc2 This summer Karp hosted Sunak at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/politics/rishi-sunak-meet-boss-scariest-30178412"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 baseball game}}}\uc2  in the US, one in a string of lobbying efforts over the past four years. In 2019, \uc2 Palantir\uc2  served {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thebureauinvestigates.com/stories/2021-02-24/revealed-data-giant-given-emergency-covid-contract-had-been-wooing-nhs-for-months"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 watermelon cocktails}}}\uc2  to the then-chair of the \uc2 NHS\uc2 , and \uc2 Palantir\uc2  representatives joined a closed-door {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.eu/article/uk-trade-department-courted-us-firm-palantir-over-untapped-nhs-data-potential/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chat at Davos}}}\uc2  with the then trade secretary Liam Fox and other tech executives. Fox\u8217\'20\'19s briefing notes for that meetings said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a huge and as yet untapped resource through the UK\u8217\'20\'19s single healthcare system \u8230\'20\'26 We aim to treble industry contract and R&D collaborative research in the \uc2 NHS\uc2  over 10 years, to nearly \u163\'00\'A31bn.\u8221\'20\'1D (Again, there\u8217\'20\'19s no suggestion that \uc2 Palantir\uc2  was seeking to sell data \u8211\'20\'13 just that UK officials were courting it and others to help the UK make health data commercially available.)\par \par \uc2 Views of \uc2 Palantir\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s usefulness to \uc2 NHS\uc2  staff vary. One hospital, Chelsea & Westminster, says its operating platform, called Foundry, which they have been trialling since early 2022, has helped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.healthcareitnews.com/news/emea/nhs-teams-palantir-tackle-elective-care-waiting-lists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut waiting lists}}}\uc2  by 28%. Other local trials of \uc2 Palantir\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s software were apparently less successful. An answer to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-questions/detail/2023-03-07/160180"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 parliamentary question}}}\uc2  earlier this year said that of the pilots of Foundry software at local \uc2 NHS\uc2  hospitals, 11 had been paused or suspended. Some have resumed, but a spokesperson for Liverpool Heart & Chest hospital stated that it had stopped using Foundry because staff had decided \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techmonitor.ai/policy/why-cant-the-nhs-quit-palantir?mrfhud=true"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it didn\u8217\'20\'19t meet our needs}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Other trusts said they had more pressing problems to fix, and at least one said they had no one who could manage the system. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://committees.parliament.uk/publications/41191/documents/202610/default/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more recent letter}}}\uc2  to the health and social care committee updated these figures to say there are now 36 trials underway at various stages of rollout, with three of those paused due to restart, and five still suspended.\par \par \uc2 Turning to \uc2 Palantir\uc2  for so much of England\u8217\'20\'19s infrastructure and analysis exposes a deeper problem: our own lack of data science staff. The \uc2 NHS\uc2  badly needs data scientists to make it fit for the future. (\uc2 Palantir\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-04-21/palantir-hires-ai-chief-from-nhs-in-u-k-as-it-bids-to-expand?leadSource=uverify%20wall"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hired}}}\uc2  two of its senior people.) In February, it was reported that the \uc2 NHS\uc2  has more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theregister.com/2023/02/22/nhs_tech_job_vacancies/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 3,000 vacant tech roles}}}\uc2 . \uc2 NHS\uc2  data scientists have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/2574c49d-2aa4-47e8-9923-35c7aeeb2d3f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warned}}}\uc2  that Foundry is not \u8220\'20\'1Cuser friendly\u8221\'20\'1D, and that the cost is \u8220\'20\'1Cridiculous\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What would a better future for \uc2 NHS\uc2  data look like? It would probably start more humbly, with broad democratic engagement. The government could ask the public how they would like their data managed. Who do you trust to handle it, who would you like to be able to use it, and under what conditions? Should the opt-outs be reformed to give you more choice?\par \par \uc2 With a \u163\'00\'A3480m contract about to be awarded, and the DPDI bill trundling ahead, expect to see a pitched battle over these questions in the coming months. It is a battle the government seemingly hopes to avoid, as it so often has in the past, by dodging debate rather than meeting tricky questions head on. Most people want to see the \uc2 NHS\uc2  use data better. But they also care deeply about who is given the keys to the kingdom.\par \par \uc2 Every prior effort to centralise \uc2 NHS\uc2  data has failed; this chapter is yet to be written. We can still get it right, and win trust for a better solution. If we don\u8217\'20\'19t, many patients will do as I did, and turn away.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 14 September 2023 to update the number of ongoing pilots of Foundry software.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Follow the Long Read on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/@gdnlongread"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @gdnlongread}}}\uc2 , listen to our podcasts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/series/the-long-read"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  and sign up to the long read weekly email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/ng-interactive/2017/may/05/sign-up-for-the-long-read-email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230914ej9e0018m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
