\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Anti-choice states aren\u8217\'20\'19t satisfied. Now they want to punish traveling for abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,060 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2023\par 16:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A husband who doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want his wife to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could sue the friend who offered to drive her, according to this legislation\u8217\'20\'19s own architect\par \par \uc2 How free can any woman be in a country where her right to control her body and family depends on the jurisdiction where she happens to live? Republicans are looking to find out. Over the past few weeks, as Republican officials in anti-choice states seek to make their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans enforceable and compel women into childbirth, a new front has opened up in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars: roads. The anti-choice movement, through a series of inventive legal theories and cynical legislative maneuvers, is now attacking women\u8217\'20\'19s right to travel.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/23932753-yellowhammer-fund-et-al-v-steve-marshall-and-west-alabama-womens-center-et-al-v-steve-marshall"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court filing}}}\uc2  last month, the Alabama attorney general, \uc2 Steve Marshall\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/08/31/politics/alabama-attorney-general-abortion-prosecute/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  that he believed his office had a right to prosecute those who help women travel across state lines in search of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The filing comes in a lawsuit from two women\u8217\'20\'19s health clinics and an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund, which sued Marshall after he publicly stated his intention to criminally investigate organizations like theirs, which provide financial and logistical help to pregnant patients seeking to leave the state. In his response, Marshall unequivocally stated that Alabama, which bans all abortions with no rape or incest exemption, views any effort to help women cross state lines as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccriminal conspiracy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn elected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  performed in Alabama would be a criminal offense,\u8221\'20\'1D Marshall\u8217\'20\'19s office writes. \u8220\'20\'1CThus, a conspiracy formed in the state to have that same act performed outside the state is illegal.\u8221\'20\'1D The filing goes on to dismiss the free speech, expression and association claims of the fund and the two clinics.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in Texas, two counties and two cities have passed laws banning so-called \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 that is, the transport or assistance of anyone seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 on the roads that pass through their territories. The \u8220\'20\'1Ctrafficking\u8221\'20\'1D in this moniker refers to the fetus: \u8220\'20\'1CThe unborn child is always taken against their will,\u8221\'20\'1D Mark Lee Dickson, the architect of these bills, told the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/09/01/texas-abortion-highways/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post}}}\uc2 . Like Texas\u8217\'20\'19s SB 8, the bounty-hunter ban that outlawed abortions in Texas at six weeks before the fall of Roe, these travel bans are also enforced via lawsuits by private citizens \u8211\'20\'13 the law is designed to allow those who are displeased by an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sue the friends, feminists and allies of the pregnant patient who helped her to get one.\par \par \uc2 Dickson and his political partner, the SB 8 architect Jonathan Mitchell, are pushing the provision in border cities and towns along major interstate highways. And like SB 8, the law is less likely to be used by strangers to prevent abortions than by abusers to punish ones that already happened. As an example of the ideal use of his bill, Dickson told the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  that a husband who did not want his wife to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could use it to sue the friend who offered to drive her \u8211\'20\'13 thus somewhat giving away the game that the goal of such a provision is to ensure that men\u8217\'20\'19s private domination and abuse of women is recognized as a right enforceable by civil law.\par \par \uc2 Texas and Alabama are not alone. Earlier this year, Idaho became the first state to criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related travel when it enacted a law making it a felony to help a minor cross state lines for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Meanwhile, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/03/08/missouri-abortion-ban-texas-supreme-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri}}}\uc2  made headlines last year when Republicans introduced bills that would criminalize anyone helping state residents to obtain abortions elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Is any of this constitutional? No. But that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean the laws will be struck down. The novel enforcement mechanism of the Texas laws, in particular, which are enforceable not by the state or municipality but only by the lawsuits of private citizens, make it hard for any pro-choice group to get standing to challenge them. That\u8217\'20\'19s the point: the bills are constructed to evade judicial review. And though Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , for one, has said that he would disapprove of anti-choice states\u8217\'20\'19 attempts to prevent women\u8217\'20\'19s travel, the supreme court, including Kavanaugh, has already blessed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban by civil suit with its sanction of SB 8.\par \par \uc2 But the point of these laws is not, exactly, to enforce them, except perhaps in the event when they are used by domestic abusers to further their control and torment of the women they\u8217\'20\'19ve imprisoned \u8211\'20\'13 as is already happening. The real point is to chill legal conduct, and to prevent the people, mostly women \u8211\'20\'13 the sisters, friends, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund staffers, colleagues and local feminists who any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient might turn to for help \u8211\'20\'13 from acting on their own moral convictions.\par \par \uc2 The law is punitive, not preventive: it is designed not to intervene in abortions before they happen so much as to punish them after the fact. It is designed, too, to frighten: the very vagueness of these laws, and the fear of punishments for violating them, will inevitably keep those who would assist an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from doing so. The point is to threaten with humiliating, ruinous lawsuits and life-altering criminal prosecutions any woman who might act on her conviction that another woman deserves to control her own body. The point is to punish and declare illegal women\u8217\'20\'19s friendship, confidences and feminist solidarity itself.\par \par \uc2 In their attempts to keep women walled inside anti-choice states, and to criminalize both friendship and flight, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel bans have been compared to the 19th century\u8217\'20\'19s fugitive slave law. I for one believe that American chattel slavery does not make a good comparison; its horrors fail as metaphor. But one does not need to draw any moral equivalence to see the parallels of the emerging political divide. An untenable conflict is arising between states where women are free to control their bodies and states where they are not, and the latter group is not respecting the laws of the former.\par \par \uc2 The nation cannot sustain this division, and it will not: either \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon become legal nationwide, or it will soon be banned nationwide. For their part, the anti-choice movement seems very confident which direction we\u8217\'20\'19re heading.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230912ej9c00139\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
