\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s off-off-year election is next big test for reproductive rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Joan E Greve in Orange, Virginia \par \uc2 2,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2023\par 12:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Flipping the state senate would allow Republicans to push through Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s plan for a 15-week ban\par \par \uc2 As she addressed about 120 Democratic voters at a rally in rural Orange, Virginia, Congresswoman \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  made a point to impress upon her constituents just how much is on the line this fall.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is personal. And politics is personal,\u8221\'20\'1D Spanberger told the crowd. \u8220\'20\'1CThis year more than any other year, this election matters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stepping up to the podium, one of the Democrats endorsed by Spanberger, the state senate candidate Jason Ford, warned that Republican victories in November could have devastating consequences across the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn Virginia, reproductive rights are on the ballot this fall,\u8221\'20\'1D Ford said. \u8220\'20\'1CProperly funding our schools [is] on the ballot this fall.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The messages encapsulated Virginia Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 broader pitch to voters as they look to regain control of the house of delegates and maintain their narrow majority in the state senate. They fear that, if Republicans can take full control of the general assembly, they will rubber-stamp Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s conservative agenda to lower taxes and bolster \u8220\'20\'1Cparents\u8217\'20\'19 rights\u8221\'20\'1D. But most importantly, Democrats expect Republican victories in November would jeopardize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Virginia, which has become a rare refuge for those seeking the procedure in thesouth.\par \par \uc2 With Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s victory, and if the Republicans achieve what they are setting out to do this year, I think all bets are off for 2024\par \par \uc2 Mark Rozell\par \par \uc2 The results also may provide the clearest indication yet of voters\u8217\'20\'19 sentiments ahead of the 2024 elections and determine whether Virginia can still be considered a battleground, considering Republicans have lost the state in every presidential race since 2008.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToo many people think Virginia is solid blue in the electoral college,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mark Rozell, dean of the Schar School of Policy and Government at \uc2 George Mason University\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWith \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s victory, and if the Republicans achieve what they are setting out to do this year, I think all bets are off for 2024.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An off-off-year\par \par \uc2 Virginia is one of just five US states that conduct off-year elections, meaning those that are held when no federal elections take place. History shows turnout in Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s state legislative races tends to be particularly low in so-called \u8220\'20\'1Coff-off-years\u8221\'20\'1D when there are no major statewide offices such as governor on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 But candidates and activists from both sides of the aisle insist it would be a mistake to downplay the importance of Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s elections this year. If Republicans can flip just two seats in the state senate, Youngkin will face few impediments in enacting his legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 Since taking office last year, Youngkin has already advanced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/virginia-transgender-students-schools-youngkin-ba073a1e8a9286456a7509688f40115b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 policies}}}\uc2  calling for transgender children to use bathrooms and locker rooms that correspond with their sex assigned at birth. On his first day in office, Youngkin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/16/virginia-governor-glenn-youngkin-sworn-into-office-critical-race-theory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed}}}\uc2  executive orders aimed at barring schools from teaching critical race theory and loosening public health mandates related to the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo get in and have the opportunity to take back the state senate and be able to support those initiatives is something that\u8217\'20\'19s really important,\u8221\'20\'1D said Steve Knotts, chair of the Fairfax county Republican party in northern Virginia. \u8220\'20\'1CI think that they\u8217\'20\'19ll be voting for Governor Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s initiatives and Republican control of the state legislature this year, but we have to do the work to get that message out because a lot of people forget there\u8217\'20\'19s an election.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 All 100 seats in the house of delegates, where Republicans held a 52-48 majority during the most recent legislative session, will be up for grabs. Democrats will be defending a 22-18 advantage in the state senate, where every seat will also be in play this November. The implementation of a new legislative map, which fueled a wave of retirements among veteran Virginia legislators, has injected more uncertainty into the elections.\par \par \uc2 As of now, control of the legislature appears to be a true toss-up. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.vcu.edu/article/2023/08/vcu-wilder-school-commonwealth-poll-shows-virginians-favor-youngkin-over-biden-for-president"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a survey}}}\uc2  conducted by \uc2 Virginia Commonwealth University\uc2  in July, 47% of Virginians want Republicans in control of the house of delegates, compared with 41% who would prefer a Democratic majority. When asked about control of the state senate, Virginians were evenly divided, with 44% of respondents preferring a Democratic majority and another 44% supporting a Republican takeover.\par \par \uc2 A fully red general assembly would be a gamechanger for Youngkin, who has faced a legislative blockade from Senate Democrats. A Republican takeover would erase the last major barrier in Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s quest to enact his full agenda \u8211\'20\'13 including a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis race is first and foremost about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Susan Swecker, chair of the Democratic party of Virginia. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it hadn\u8217\'20\'19t been for the Democratic blue brick wall in the state senate the last two years, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be banned in Virginia.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The last holdout in the\par \par \uc2 south\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision last year to overturn Roe v Wade, southern states controlled by Republicans passed a wave of laws severely restricting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or, in some cases, banning it altogether. Virginia \u8211\'20\'13 where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently legal until about 26 weeks \u8211\'20\'13 has become the last remaining southern state without extensive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, but that status could soon evaporate if Republicans have their way.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin has indicated he supports a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban with exceptions for rape, incest and the safety of the mother. The governor has framed the proposal, which senate Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-virginia-state-government-42791ab80e24a3c6944819ac820273a6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already defeated}}}\uc2  once earlier this year, as a point of consensus among a wide swath of Virginia voters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI believe that there\u8217\'20\'19s a moment for Virginians to come together over a very, very difficult topic, and Virginians elected a pro-life governor,\u8221\'20\'1D Youngkin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wset.com/news/local/governor-glenn-youngkin-virginia-15-week-abortion-ban-republicans-take-control-general-assembly-legislation-august-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  last month.\par \par \uc2 But polls challenge the perception of a 15-week ban as an area of common ground across the state. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2023/04/06/virginia-voters-youngkin-approval-poll/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Washington Post-Schar School survey}}}\uc2  conducted in March, 49% of Virginia voters support a 15-week ban with exceptions, while 46% oppose it. Voters also expressed criticism of Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with 33% approving of his handling of the issue and 45% disapproving.\par \par \uc2 People are scared, and people are worried, which is why they\u8217\'20\'19re turning out and they\u8217\'20\'19re voting on this issue\par \par \uc2 Sara Ratcliffe\par \par \uc2 Threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access appear to be weighing heavily on Virginians\u8217\'20\'19 minds as they head for the polls. Sara Ratcliffe, a Democratic candidate for the house of delegates who spoke at the Orange rally, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was often the first issue voters mention when she knocks on doors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs the dominoes have fallen, especially in the redder states and in the states surrounding us here in the south, people have realized what\u8217\'20\'19s at stake,\u8221\'20\'1D Ratcliffe said. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are scared, and people are worried, which is why they\u8217\'20\'19re turning out and they\u8217\'20\'19re voting on this issue.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That fear and anger was palpable among the Democratic attendees of the Orange rally, several of whom cited protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as their top priority for November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople younger than me now don\u8217\'20\'19t have the same freedoms I had when I was a woman of childbearing age, and I feel that\u8217\'20\'19s not fair,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lynn Meyers, a 78-year-old from Locust Grove.\par \par \uc2 Bill Maiden, a 58-year-old voter from Culpepper, added, \u8220\'20\'1CEven my friends that are Republican, they are concerned about [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access] too. They don\u8217\'20\'19t agree with Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s stance on this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions contributed to Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 disappointing performance in the 2022 midterms, and the issue could now threaten the party\u8217\'20\'19s hopes in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Despite the potential political liability of a 15-week ban, Democrats fear that Youngkin will not hesitate to approve an even more severe policy if Republicans flip the senate. They often remind voters that, shortly after Roe was overturned last year, Youngkin said, \u8220\'20\'1CAny bill that comes to my desk I will sign happily and gleefully in order to protect life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Youngkin bets big on 2023\par \par \uc2 Three years ago, most Virginians had never heard the name \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 . A former co-CEO of the private equity firm the \uc2 Carlyle Group\uc2 , Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s 2021 gubernatorial bid marked his first foray into electoral politics.\par \par \uc2 He defeated six primary opponents by carefully toeing the line between endorsing many of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s policy positions on culture war issues without completely alienating the independent voters who eventually fueled his victory in the general election. Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s win over the former Democratic governor \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  was all the more surprising given that it came just one year after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  beat Trump in Virginia by 10 points.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe guy never ran for office before, but he just had really good instincts [on] how to strike that balance and get the support of the [\u8216\'20\'18Make America Great Again\u8217\'20\'19] Republicans while not seeming threatening at all to swing voters at the same time,\u8221\'20\'1D Rozell said. \u8220\'20\'1CNot many Republicans these days can do that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Those instincts appear to have served Youngkin well in office so far. The VCU survey conducted in July showed Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s approval rating stood at +18, as 49% of Virginians approved of the governor\u8217\'20\'19s job performance and 31% disapproved. Biden trailed far behind Youngkin at -15, with 39% of Virginians approving of his job performance and 54% disapproving.\par \par \uc2 Those approval numbers have made Knotts more optimistic about Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 prospects in November. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a very popular governor who is really getting in and doing some programs that are good for Democrats, Republicans [and] independents,\u8221\'20\'1D Knotts said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo I think this could be a really good year for us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s popularity has also spurred talk of a potential presidential bid, and the Fox News owner, \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/12/us/politics/desantis-fox-news-rupert-murdoch.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  told allies that he would like to see the Virginia governor jump into the 2024 Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s success or failure in November could decide his fate on the national stage, and he has dedicated substantial political capital to bringing home a win for Republicans. Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s state Pac, Spirit of Virginia, raised an impressive $5.9m during the second quarter of the year to promote Republican campaigns.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a unifying agenda that is resonating across the Commonwealth,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Dave Rexrode\uc2 , chair of the Spirit of Virginia. \u8220\'20\'1CVirginians want leaders in the general assembly who will work with Governor Youngkin to move our commonwealth forward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Beyond Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s own political future, the outcome in Virginia this November could provide a roadmap for both parties in 2024, as they battle it out for the White House.\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 When Barack Obama won Virginia in the 2008 presidential race, he became the first Democrat to carry the state since 1964, and the victory marked a sea change for his party. Democrats have won Virginia in every presidential election since Obama\u8217\'20\'19s landmark victory, and the party now holds both of Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 US Senate\uc2  seats.\par \par \uc2 Their wins have led some Democrats to downplay Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s status as a battleground state, but Swecker warns that would be a dangerous oversight.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople got very excited about the successes we had and took for granted that we were a blue state,\u8221\'20\'1D Swecker said. \u8220\'20\'1CThose of us who are in the trenches know better.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s victory in 2021 demonstrated how Republicans can still play statewide in Virginia, and the governor hopes that the legislative races this year will prove his win was no fluke.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am curious whether the Republicans are able to carry that momentum into this election cycle and what it might suggest about the Republican base and how deeply engaged it is and activated going into the 2024 elections,\u8221\'20\'1D Rozell said.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of the outcome in Virginia, the November results will be closely scrutinized by both parties to spot potential strengths and vulnerabilities heading into 2024, when the White House and Congress will be up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 As someone who has closely studied Virginia politics since the 1980s, Rozell has endured much talk about the state being a guarantee for one party or the other over the years, but he insists that Virginia remains, as ever, a battleground.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t assume that Virginia is a Democratic lock any more,\u8221\'20\'1D Rozell said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd those 13 electoral votes can be really important next year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230909ej9900132\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
