\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Death Star law\u8217\'20\'19 to  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 : the new rightwing laws taking effect in Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed Pilkington \par \uc2 1,344 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2023\par 13:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hundreds of new state laws come into effect on 1 September, including attacking trans rights, further limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushing back climate efforts\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Republican-controlled legislature in Texas will dump an avalanche of virulent new laws on the state\u8217\'20\'19s 30 million residents, cementing its reputation as a hotbed of the far-right turmoil that is sweeping America.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of laws will come into effect as the product of the legislative session that Texas holds every two years. Among them are a number of highly controversial targeted bills that will have a seismic impact on some of the most vulnerable Texan communities.\par \par \uc2 They include measures attacking the rights and healthcare of transgender Texans, undermining the autonomy of progressive cities such as Houston, extending the state\u8217\'20\'19s prohibition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing back on efforts to combat rising heat amid the climate crisis, and more.\par \par \uc2 The Republican group that controls both houses of the Texas legislature has been emboldened by favourable election results in 2022 that showed little or no backlash to their radical rightwing agenda of a year before. The introduction of an almost total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jun/24/overturning-roe-story-is-women-unfreedom"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade was overturned}}}\uc2  also appears to have inflicted minimal damage on the party\u8217\'20\'19s standing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Republican party is unbridled in Texas,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mark Jones, a political science professor at \uc2 Rice University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the most striking new laws that take effect on Friday, which Jones likened to a \u8220\'20\'1Csmorgasbord of red meat for the Republican base\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 Anti-LGBTQ+ measures\par \par \uc2 Several of the most significant bills that come into effect on Friday involve attacks on the LGBTQ+ community. The most contentious is SB 14.\par \par \uc2 When the new law goes into effect on Friday, after the state supreme court gave the go-ahead, Texas will become the most populous state to ban gender-affirming care for transgender children. The legislation bars under-18 trans Texans from gaining access to medical treatments including hormone therapies, as well as surgeries that are rarely performed on children.\par \par \uc2 Under the measure, trans kids will be \u8220\'20\'1Cweaned off\u8221\'20\'1D any medical care they receive.\par \par \uc2 Johnathan Gooch of Equality Texas, the largest LGBTQ+ advocacy group in the state, said the new law had had a dramatic chilling effect. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen a lot of healthcare providers either close or move out of state, which has left not only trans youth but many trans adults without the medication they need.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Twisting the knife in the anti-trans wound, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/05/17/texas-transgender-college-athletes-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SB 15 prohibits}}}\uc2  trans men from competing in men\u8217\'20\'19s college sports teams, and trans women from taking part in women\u8217\'20\'19s college athletics teams. The move is an extension of a similar ban that was introduced for grade school children in the 2021 legislative session.\par \par \uc2 Then there is SB 12, the latest in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jan/21/anti-drag-show-laws-bans-republican-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rash of state laws}}}\uc2  cracking down on drag shows performed in front of children. Though lawmakers removed \u8220\'20\'1Cdrag\u8221\'20\'1D from the language of the bill, their target is crystal clear \u8211\'20\'13 they specifically demonise show props that \u8220\'20\'1Cexaggerate male or female sexual characteristics\u8221\'20\'1D. The law was temporarily halted by a federal judge on Thursday, pending a legal challenge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is tragic,\u8221\'20\'1D Gooch said. \u8220\'20\'1CTexas has a long history of drag performances stretching back to 1930s San Antonio and Houston. It astounds me that almost 100 years later, lawmakers have made drag performances illegal in many cases.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Texas is also echoing book bans that have been introduced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/19/clay-county-florida-book-bans-moms-for-liberty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in Florida}}}\uc2  and other Republican-controlled states. The measure, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/history.aspx?LegSess=88R&Bill=HB900,"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HB 900}}}\uc2 , orders school libraries to remove or segregate any books carrying \u8220\'20\'1Csexually explicit\u8221\'20\'1D material, raising fears that it will lead to blanket censorship of titles addressing LGBTQ+ themes.\par \par \uc2 Taking all this together, Gooch said that the LGBTQ+ community in Texas was feeling \u8220\'20\'1Cexhausted and defeated. Those who can afford to leave have been leaving, especially families with trans kids but also trans adults who feel that their healthcare will be next on the chopping block.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Assault on local democracy\par \par \uc2 While Republicans enjoy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sos.state.tx.us/elections/voter/elected.shtml"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lock-tight control}}}\uc2  over statewide elected offices in Texas, they still have to contend with a number of left-leaning major cities who see \u8211\'20\'13 and do \u8211\'20\'13 things very differently. The Republican solution is to strip cities of their local democratic power.\par \par \uc2 New laws out on Friday amount to the most aggressive attempt yet by the Republican-controlled legislature to erode the power of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s progressive and Democratic-led big cities. That includes Houston, Dallas, San Antonio, El Paso and other urban areas.\par \par \uc2 The most far-reaching provision is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=88R&Bill=HB2127"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HB 2127}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 dubbed by opponents in the Lone Star state the \u8220\'20\'1CDeath Star law\u8221\'20\'1D. It wrenches away from cities and counties whole areas of policymaking, from labour relations to agriculture and the environment, in effect {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/06/07/texas-republicans-cities-local-control/#:~:text=A%20yearslong%20war%20waged%20by,all%20kinds%20of%20local%20ordinances."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 transferring control }}}\uc2  from progressive urban areas to the hard-right caucus in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Death Star law covers entire swaths of code. It\u8217\'20\'19s so murky we have no idea what it means,\u8221\'20\'1D said Abbie Kamin, a Democratic city council member in Houston. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are literally denying the right of local control for progressive major cities and harming our ability to represent and provide services to our millions of residents.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A Texas judge has ruled the Death Star law unconstitutional, but the state is appealing and the future of the measure still hangs in the balance.\par \par \uc2 Houston, the largest city in Texas, has been singled out. Two new laws, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/88R/billtext/pdf/SB01750F.pdf#navpanes=0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SB 1750}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/06/01/harris-county-elections-texas-oversight-law/#:~:text=Senate%20Bill%201933%2C%20authored%20by,which%20means%20just%20Harris%20County"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SB 1933}}}\uc2 , respectively unseat the elected Democratic election administrator in Houston who has successfully expanded the city\u8217\'20\'19s electorate, and allow for the Republican legislature to take over future election counts if there are complaints of \u8220\'20\'1Cirregularities\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an act of revenge against our efforts to expand access to voting in Houston,\u8221\'20\'1D Kamin said. \u8220\'20\'1CMore than that, it\u8217\'20\'19s interference in the integrity of our elections.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As a further blow to local democracy, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/05/28/texas-legislature-prosecutors-removal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HB 17}}}\uc2  allows the removal of progressive locally elected prosecutors who choose not to pursue regressive laws such as the criminalisation of marijuana possession or abortions.\par \par \uc2 Abortions\par \par \uc2 Texas already has one of the toughest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country, allowing for only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/28/texas-limited-access-abortion-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rare exceptions}}}\uc2  and threatening doctors accused of violating the prohibition with possible life in prison. SB 24 uses state funds to create a statewide network of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics misleadingly known as \u8220\'20\'1Ccrisis pregnancy centers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Under the provision, state funds are also barred from being used to support family planning services including birth control.\par \par \uc2 Diversity, equity and inclusion\par \par \uc2 SB 17 eradicates DEI offices and training programs from all public university campuses.\par \par \uc2 Heat and the climate crisis\par \par \uc2 Texas has more worker deaths from heat than any other state, according to federal data, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/06/16/texas-heat-wave-water-break-construction-workers/#:~:text=At%20least%2042%20workers%20died,different%20primary%20cause%20of%20injury."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas Tribune}}}\uc2  reports. Yet Austin and Dallas will no longer be allowed to require mandatory 10-minute water breaks for outdoor construction workers in extreme heat.\par \par \uc2 The state is currently in the grip of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news4sanantonio.com/news/local/texas-heatwave-prompts-ercots-urgent-call-for-energy-conservation-from-6-9-pm-today-san-antonio-texas-austin-new-braunfels-county-city"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record-breaking heatwave}}}\uc2 , with temperatures reaching 119F (48C).\par \par \uc2 The searing heat is being felt especially keenly in Texas prisons which have seen a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tpr.org/criminal-justice/2023-08-09/a-spike-in-deaths-hits-texas-prisons-as-study-challenges-states-claim-of-no-heat-related-deaths"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spike in deaths}}}\uc2  this summer. Yet the state legislature chose to spend {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/31/texas-prison-heatwave-temperatures-cells"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not a penny}}}\uc2  of its $33bn surplus on introducing air conditioning to its crumbling penal institutions.\par \par \uc2 Despite Texas already suffering from the impact of the climate crisis, including strengthening hurricanes and brutal heat, environmentalists were dismayed that from Friday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/05/18/climate-change-texas/#:~:text=Hurricanes%20that%20hit%20the%20Texas,on%20land%2C%20scientists%20have%20found."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new levies}}}\uc2  on electric vehicles will be imposed. Anyone buying an electric car will have to pay $400, on top of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/08/21/texas-new-law-electric-vehicle-fee/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $200 annual fee}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other red meat laws\par \par \uc2 Republicans have thrown in some other eye-catching new laws that are certain to play well with the base. As Covid cases begin to rise again, with some states encouraging renewed use of masks, Texas is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kens5.com/article/news/health/coronavirus/texas-law-banning-covid-related-mandates-by-local-governments-takes-effect/273-812706c4-c36b-48e2-9551-deaba0be4092"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 permanently banning}}}\uc2  mandates for mask wearing and vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 Hitting another hot-button issue, HB 6 defines overdoses from fentanyl as \u8220\'20\'1Cpoisonings\u8221\'20\'1D. That will allow prosecutors to charge anyone who supplies a fatal dose of the opioid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/05/16/texas-fentanyl-murder-legislature/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 with murder}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230901ej91001bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
