\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  providers on two years of Texas ban: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re living in a devastating reality\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Mary Tuma \par \uc2 2,832 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2023\par 16:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Senate Bill 8 wiped out almost all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the second-most populous state in the US, and served as a harbinger of what was to come over the rest of the country\par \par \uc2 Nearly a year before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  eviscerated Roe v Wade, the court allowed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unprecedented \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  to take effect in Texas, serving as a harbinger of what was to sweep over the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 The most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law at the time, with no exception for rape, incest, or lethal fetal abnormality, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/19/texas-abortion-ban-law-greg-abbott"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senate Bill 8}}}\uc2  barred care after six weeks of pregnancy, and carried a private enforcement provision that empowered anyone to sue a provider or someone who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/oct/10/texas-abortion-law-jonathan-f-mitchell-profile"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Caids or abets\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The move successfully wiped out {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/03/texas-abortion-ban-six-months-grave-warning"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost all \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  care}}}\uc2  in the second-most populous state in the US. When Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization hit, the state doubled down, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/25/us-abortion-trigger-bans-laws-tennessee-texas-idaho"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criminally banning}}}\uc2  all care and solidifying itself as the largest state in the US to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the two years since, Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers \u8211\'20\'13 some of the first in the US to experience a nearly post-Roe world \u8211\'20\'13 reflect on the devastating and lasting effect of the severe law, the trauma they felt denying patients care, and the struggle they faced when deciding whether or not to flee the state or stay put.\par \par \uc2 Dr\par \par \uc2 Jessica Rubino: \u8216\'20\'18The law forced me to be a bad doctor\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 When Senate Bill 8 took effect, Dr Rubino felt like she was on a \u8220\'20\'1Csinking ship\u8221\'20\'1D. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider and family medicine specialist was forced to turn away dozens of patients at Austin Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Center \u8211\'20\'13 including one who was experiencing kidney failure. At the same time, patients below the six-week mark were rushing to choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care before it was too late, leaving thoughtful decision-making behind.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI had to tell people there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing I can legally do for you, unless you\u8217\'20\'19re on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jul/13/texas-abortion-ban-maternal-health-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 death\u8217\'20\'19s doorstep}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rubino. \u8220\'20\'1CThe law forced me to be a bad doctor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was heartbreaking and soul-crushing,\u8221\'20\'1D she continued. \u8220\'20\'1CI was watching a healthcare disaster play out in real time, knowing that this law not only affects our state but is causing a ripple effect in every other state. With SB 8 \u8211\'20\'13 and even years before the law \u8211\'20\'13 we saw the writing on the wall with Roe and tried to warn everyone, but I\u8217\'20\'19m not sure who was listening.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rubino also recalled a conversation she had six months prior to SB 8 with colleagues across the state who appeared united, vowing to continue providing care despite the law\u8217\'20\'19s consequences. People are going to die, she told them, we should take the \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonal hit\u8221\'20\'1D. However, that wave of defiance never materialized. Rubino lacked critical mass.\par \par \uc2 She soon fell into an \u8220\'20\'1Cextreme\u8221\'20\'1D depression; it was difficult to get out of bed each day and she eventually sought mental health therapy and antidepressants. Her brain felt \u8220\'20\'1Cbroken\u8221\'20\'1D, she said. After Dobbs, she stopped performing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for nearly a year, exacerbating her gloom.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHaving to deny patients the healthcare you are trained \u8211\'20\'13 and able \u8211\'20\'13 to give them is something you never get over. It\u8217\'20\'19s not only medically unethical, it\u8217\'20\'19s morally wrong,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rubino. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was traumatizing, and it still haunts me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 SB 8, she said, was the tipping point for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas like her who have been forced to navigate onerous laws over the years that compromise the care they give, including a mandatory sonogram and 24-hour waiting period that incorporates relaying {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thedailybeast.com/all-of-texass-state-mandated-lies-about-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 erroneous medical information}}}\uc2 , bans on insurance coverage for care, restrictions on minors\u8217\'20\'19 access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and more.\par \par \uc2 In May, under the advice of attorneys and those closest to her, Rubino and her family left Texas with no plans to return. She worked at a clinic in Bristol, Virginia, where she largely served patients in banned southern states, before moving to DC in late August to help expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services at a reproductive health clinic there.\par \par \uc2 Rubino still struggles with the decision to flee Texas, while also acknowledging the legal inability to continue her calling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a sense of guilt, of letting down the community I serve. Sometimes I feel like I gave up on these people,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 She also worries that a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban could once again pull her away from the community she now treats. She considers one day working in the UK or New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 Rubino feels deeply anxious about the fate of the patients she has left behind and mentioned a recurring patient, a victim of domestic violence, whose partner blocked her access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe\u8217\'20\'19s going to call and I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to be there,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rubino. \u8220\'20\'1CShe\u8217\'20\'19s not in a safe situation and we know staying pregnant can lead to more abuse, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenation.com/article/society/texas-abortion-gun-laws-killing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 even death by an abusive partner}}}\uc2 . The safest thing for her would be to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but now she\u8217\'20\'19s not going to have that choice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dr\par \par \uc2 Ghazaleh Moayedi: \u8216\'20\'18Inhumane and illogical\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Testifying before Congress three separate times to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.house.gov/meetings/JU/JU00/20211104/114209/HHRG-117-JU00-Wstate-MoayediG-20211104.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 oppose \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2  and uplift the right to access, Dr Ghazaleh Moayedi has made her mark as an outspoken and passionate reproductive justice advocate for Texans.\par \par \uc2 But the road wasn\u8217\'20\'19t always clear for the doctor: unsure of what to do after graduating college, Moayedi\u8217\'20\'19s friend recommended she take a nanny job. Her boss was \uc2 Amy Hagstrom-Miller\uc2 , the head of a network of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/meet-the-woman-behind-the-supreme-courts-high-stakes-abortion-case-240642/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 then major figure}}}\uc2  in Texas reproductive rights who would go on to lead several legal challenges against the state, including a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2017/04/27/whole-womans-health-texas-trump/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2016 US supreme court victory}}}\uc2 . Moayedi began working in Miller\u8217\'20\'19s clinic, where she saw her interests collide.\par \par \uc2 As a \u8220\'20\'1Cbrown, Muslim\u8221\'20\'1D n Iranian American woman who grew up in Texas, Moayedi quickly realized the majority of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors \u8211\'20\'13 largely white men \u8211\'20\'13 did not reflect the diversity of the patients they treated, and vowed to fix that.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI could feel a palpable racial and cultural divide,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CNone of the doctors looked like the people we take care of. I wanted to be a provider that helped represent the communities we serve. I decided to go to medical school with that goal as a driving force.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Moayedi has worked in Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care since 2014, weathering the roller coaster of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, including a 2020 order to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/apr/30/us-states-ban-abortions-coronavirus-leave-women-desperate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  under the pretense of the Covid emergency, which, at the time, upended her plans to start her own practice.\par \par \uc2 After SB 8, she transitioned her care to Oklahoma. When Oklahoma\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law took effect, she switched gears, providing ultrasounds in Texas to those traveling to and from out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Moayedi then became uncertain if she could safely venture to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still legal, as the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/sites/default/files/images/executive-management/Post-Roe%20Advisory%20(updated%20draft%2006.21.2022)%20(1).pdf?utm_content=&utm_medium=email&utm_name=&utm_source=govdelivery&utm_term="}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 encouraged}}}\uc2  local prosecutors to go after providers shortly after Roe fell. She and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.classaction.org/media/fund-texas-choice-et-al-v-paxton-et-al.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued the state}}}\uc2  for legal protection, and paused their services in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 After securing a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/06/texas-abortion-funds-resume-service-legal-reprieve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court victory}}}\uc2 , Moayedi has worked to build an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and miscarriage telemedicine practice, still in the process of getting off the ground. She is now licensed in 20 states \u8211\'20\'13 but only half allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  telemed. She also travels to Kansas, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution#:~:text=The%20state%20will%20remain%20a,a%20statement%20welcoming%20the%20result."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 safe haven state}}}\uc2 , to provide care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve had to really pivot quite a bit. It\u8217\'20\'19s been absolutely wild,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CMy practice doesn\u8217\'20\'19t look anything like I thought it would. For now, my goal is to stay in Texas but we\u8217\'20\'19ll see what happens.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Moayedi says the law\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cinhumane and illogical\u8221\'20\'1D impact is especially pronounced when she is treating a patient in another state only to discover they\u8217\'20\'19re from not just the same city as her, but the same neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHere we both are, hundreds of miles away from our home and support system, just to receive healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CMoments like those just hit you in the gut.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As a complex family planning specialist, Moayedi constantly worries for patients with \u8220\'20\'1Cpotentially catastrophic\u8221\'20\'1D high-risk pregnancies, especially as the Texas law offers only vague medical emergency exceptions, leading patients to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/20/texas-women-pregnancy-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 near-death experiences}}}\uc2 . She receives calls from colleagues wondering if pregnant patients with complications, like C-section scar ectopic pregnancies, can receive care in Texas. She often refers them out of state to be safe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI really don\u8217\'20\'19t have words to describe the deep, deep pain I feel,\u8221\'20\'1D said Moayedi. \u8220\'20\'1CThese laws are insulting, disgusting, cruel, and absolutely pointless.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The provider and advocate expresses disappointment with the federal administration, who she feels has failed to meaningfully protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and patients since SB 8 took effect.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s response has been a limp handshake,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to see tangible, bold action to restore or at least prevent the further erosion of reproductive rights. We need unwavering support \u8211\'20\'13 not a leader who can barely say the word \u8216\'20\'18\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kathy Kleinfeld: \u8216\'20\'18SB 8 was meant to be a fear tactic that paralyzed care\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Kathy Kleinfeld will never forget the desperation that swept over Houston Women\u8217\'20\'19s Reproductive Services after SB 8 took effect. Anxious patients begged her and her staff to perform \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care past the six-week mark, even offering money under the table and other favors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey were crying and pleading with us, saying \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19ll do whatever you want,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Kleinfeld. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was so heartbreaking, there was nothing we could do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Patients \u8211\'20\'13 as well as clinic staff \u8211\'20\'13 held their breath during each ultrasound, hoping the pregnancy would fall under the state-mandated time frame. For those past the mark, Kleinfeld and colleagues became \u8220\'20\'1Cdystopian travel agents\u8221\'20\'1D connecting patients with out-of-state care.\par \par \uc2 After 30 years of providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Houston, Kleinfeld had never experienced anything so chaotic and devastating. Then Dobbs hit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt felt like everything we experienced with SB 8 was magnified \u8211\'20\'13 it was like SB 8 on steroids,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kleinfeld. \u8220\'20\'1CThe intensity, the confusion, the chaos all became so overwhelming.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While she was forced to halt \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, Kleinfeld did not want to leave her patients behind. One month after the fall of Roe, she regrouped, considerably downsizing her 5,000 sq-ft clinic and cutting her staff by more than half. She now provides pre- and post- \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ultrasounds for those traveling out of state, as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic referrals. Her clinic is only one of two former independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas \u8211\'20\'13 and just a handful across the US \u8211\'20\'13 that have not closed or moved away.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe did not want to completely abandon pregnant people in Houston,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kleinfeld. \u8220\'20\'1CWe felt it was still really important to adapt and provide this necessary service. It feels absolutely awful to not be able to offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, but at the same time, we feel grateful to be able to still help patients in whatever way we can.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her clinic received around 1,200 visits this year, with most traveling to and from New Mexico, Colorado and Kansas.\par \par \uc2 The fear unleashed by SB 8 two years ago still lingers today: Patients are scared to disclose that they want or have had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; they are fearful to bring a partner or family member with them to a procedure out-of-state or even to the ultrasound at Kleinfeld\u8217\'20\'19s clinic, worrying that a loved one may be in legal trouble for \u8220\'20\'1Caiding or abetting\u8221\'20\'1D care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe still have to explain to patients all the time that it is not illegal to help someone obtain a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kleinfeld. \u8220\'20\'1CSB 8 was meant to be a fear tactic that paralyzed care and instilled anxiety in patients, and even after Dobbs, we are still seeing its impact.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dr Alan Braid and Andrea Gallegos: \u8216\'20\'18Waving our hands hands on top of a burning building\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 As a medical resident in 1972, Dr Alan Braid will never forget treating a 15-year-old girl in a San Antonio emergency room who was suffering from sepsis \u8211\'20\'13 a life-threatening blood infection \u8211\'20\'13 after a botched and illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , her vaginal cavity packed with rags. Braid and doctors did everything they could but the infection was so severe, she died a few days later from massive organ failure. That year, he saw another two teenagers die from illegal abortions.\par \par \uc2 It was then that Braid realized that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care was vital and medically necessary, an inextricable component of overall healthcare. One year later, Roe would help solidify and protect Braid\u8217\'20\'19s mission.\par \par \uc2 For the next 45 years, he provided ob-gyn and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Texas. When Senate Bill 8 hit, it felt like 1972 all over again, he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo repeat history and expect a different outcome is insanity. Women will be injured and women will die \u8211\'20\'13 again \u8211\'20\'13 without access to healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Braid.\par \par \uc2 With a passion for reproductive rights, Andrea Gallegos joined her father\u8217\'20\'19s practice as manager of Alamo Women\u8217\'20\'19s Reproductive Services a few years ago. She describes the impact of SB 8 as \u8220\'20\'1Cdevastating\u8221\'20\'1D to patients, many of whom were saddled with multiple barriers to care. Even when staff would offer to pay for travel or the procedure itself, patients \u8211\'20\'13 still bound by the inability to find child care or time off work \u8211\'20\'13 couldn\u8217\'20\'19t make the journey out of state.\par \par \uc2 Braid felt like he had to fight back. In an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/09/18/texas-abortion-provider-alan-braid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 act of overt defiance}}}\uc2 , the provider performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a patient beyond the six-week limit. He was not only acting out of medical duty but hoped to invoke a legal challenge that would eventually halt SB 8.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think any of us really thought SB 8 would last \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s so blatantly unconstitutional and just crazy, we figured the courts \u8211\'20\'13 even a court as conservative as the fifth circuit \u8211\'20\'13 would recognize the law needs to be stopped,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gallegos.\par \par \uc2 While Braid\u8217\'20\'19s intentional act of resistance attracted an outpouring of nationwide support, the lawsuits against him {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/12/08/texas-abortion-provider-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ultimately failed}}}\uc2  to halt SB 8, leaving the provider feeling largely defeated.\par \par \uc2 He and his team continued to navigate the draconian law, routinely sending patients to their Tulsa, Oklahoma, clinic, where the caseload tripled within the first couple of months, placing a strain on the out-of-state provider.\par \par \uc2 When Oklahoma\u8217\'20\'19s governor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/05/26/1101428347/oklahoma-governor-signs-the-nations-strictest-abortion-ban#:~:text=Sue%20Ogrocki%2FAP-,Oklahoma%20Gov.,%2C%202022%2C%20in%20Oklahoma%20City."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed into law}}}\uc2  an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban \u8211\'20\'13 modeled after Texas\u8217\'20\'19s SB 8 \u8211\'20\'13 in April 2022, Braid was forced to shutter the critical pipeline for Texans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt felt like we were waving our hands on top of a burning building, trying to warn everyone else that this is what it\u8217\'20\'19s going to look like for the rest of the country soon,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gallegos. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we see the lack of access, the forced travel, the domino effect on surrounding clinics now everyday post-Dobbs, in Texas we were experiencing it first.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Following Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, Braid was forced to close the doors to his San Antonio clinic and stopped practicing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Texas after nearly five decades. In May, he officially moved to Albuquerque, New Mexico, where he has set up a clinic in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-guns-new-mexico-f4c51200f0a4e3f2ce1dc250401c9b11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 safe haven}}}\uc2  state.\par \par \uc2 Gallegos relocated to Carbondale, Illinois, in July, a spot nestled between {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/maps/abortion-laws-by-state/?state=IL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -hostile states}}}\uc2 , to oversee a new clinic there.\par \par \uc2 Leaving Texas \u8211\'20\'13 and friends and family behind \u8211\'20\'13 is deeply \u8220\'20\'1Cbittersweet\u8221\'20\'1D for the father-daughter duo: there is a sense of \u8220\'20\'1Cabandonment\u8221\'20\'1D but also a recognition that the move was necessary.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not easy to completely start over but I know this is where I\u8217\'20\'19m supposed to be,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gallegos.\par \par \uc2 For the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, it\u8217\'20\'19s also a painful reminder of the growing inequity of reproductive healthcare across the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt hits me hard knowing geography has played such a significant role in privilege to access to what I consider basic healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gallegos. \u8220\'20\'1CGeography should not determine if you can have a safe or dangerous pregnancy. We are living in a devastating reality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Braid, now in his late 70s, describes working in New Mexico as \u8220\'20\'1Crefreshing\u8221\'20\'1D, as he can \u8220\'20\'1Cjust be a doctor\u8221\'20\'1D and not \u8220\'20\'1Chave to call attorneys\u8221\'20\'1D for guidance every step of the way, as he did in Texas.\par \par \uc2 However, he has left his home state \u8211\'20\'13 and the place where he learned to be a physician so many years ago \u8211\'20\'13 with a tinge of regret, wishing he not only provided one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in violation of SB 8, but several more, convinced that the act of rebellion would have eventually led to a successful court battle that brought down the law. His daughter seeks to allay his remorse.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI remind my dad that the law was so unprecedented, so hard to predict and navigate, none of us knew what would happen,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gallegos. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the end, the whole point of SB 8 was to elicit fear in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and sadly, that\u8217\'20\'19s exactly what it did.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230831ej8v001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
