\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Agonising delays for women as Dobbs decision worsens OB-GYN shortage\b0\par\par\uc2 Robin Buller \par \uc2 1,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2023\par 10:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Supreme court ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has led to specialists leaving restrictive states, with 36% of counties maternity care deserts\par \par \uc2 When Caslin Gilroy learned she was pregnant in May, she immediately contacted her doctor\u8217\'20\'19s office to book prenatal care. She\u8217\'20\'19d visited the same clinic for years \u8211\'20\'13 including for her first pregnancy in 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 and thought booking an appointment would be straightforward. But Gilroy was told her doctor had no availability until August, when she would be close to 12 weeks along.\par \par \uc2 Unable to get a pregnancy confirmation appointment, which ideally happens between eight and 10 weeks, Gilroy grew anxious, and even struggled to convince herself that the pregnancy was real.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith my first pregnancy, I felt like I was a priority. I never had trouble getting an appointment,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 These setbacks are widespread. One woman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://community.babycenter.com/post/a77813794/frustrated-unable-to-get-mfm-appointment-soon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 waited more than five weeks}}}\uc2  to connect with a specialist for a complication; another had yet to receive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reddit.com/r/TwoXChromosomes/comments/11x311f/second_trimester_of_pregnancy_and_still_no/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  care at 20 weeks}}}\uc2  because of cancellations and insurance issues.\par \par \uc2 Delays can be traumatizing when something goes wrong. At an ultrasound last fall, Kelsey Stolfer of Pittsburgh learned that there was no longer a heartbeat and needed to undergo dilation and curettage \u8211\'20\'13 a surgical procedure to remove fetal tissue. The earliest slot available wasn\u8217\'20\'19t for another full week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just felt numb,\u8221\'20\'1D said Stolfer, describing the mental toll of waiting for the miscarriage procedure. \u8220\'20\'1CAt that point, you\u8217\'20\'19ve already spent a week and a half with a dead baby inside of you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Experts say these delays are just one symptom of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://stateline.org/2016/08/15/a-shortage-in-the-nations-maternal-health-care/?amp=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long-predicted}}}\uc2  nationwide OB-GYN shortage that has intensified in the wake of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, and it\u8217\'20\'19s affecting women\u8217\'20\'19s health across the country.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 In conservative states, care delays largely stem from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.yahoo.com/iowa-shortage-ob-gyn-doctors-103102103.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fallout of the Dobbs decision}}}\uc2  last summer when the supreme court revoked the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Facing possible lawsuits for providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, OB-GYNs in Texas, Florida, Idaho and elsewhere are choosing to relocate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wesh.com/article/florida-obgyn-doctors-shortage/44392270"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  to more liberal states}}}\uc2 , while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ob-gyn-shortage-roe-v-wade-abortion-bans/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medical students are opting for other fields}}}\uc2  entirely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFlorida has become a place where it is unsustainable to be an OB-GYN,\u8221\'20\'1D said Stephanie Ros, a maternal fetal medicine specialist at the \uc2 University of South Florida\uc2  who, until recently, ran the school\u8217\'20\'19s OB-GYN residency program.\par \par \uc2 For years, the program was among the nation\u8217\'20\'19s top schools for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training, and was found on the prestigious {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ryanprogram.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ryan}}}\uc2  list of the best family planning residency programs. But after the state banned most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, the designation was revoked. Applicant numbers subsequently declined dramatically, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://msmagazine.com/2023/08/10/ob-gyn-residency-abortion-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 skilled candidates took their talents elsewhere}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.lww.com/greenjournal/Abstract/2023/05001/The_Dobbs_Decision_and_Its_Geographical_Effect_on.343.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national survey}}}\uc2 , 60% of medical students said they were unlikely to apply for residency in a restrictive state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStates with stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws won\u8217\'20\'19t align with my goals as a future OB-GYN,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rohini Kousalya Siva, president of the \uc2 American Medical Student Association\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIf young doctors want to \u8230\'20\'26 get the skills they need, then they have to go to states where they can access [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] training.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Verda J Hicks, president of the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , described the trend as cause for concern about the \u8220\'20\'1Cnext generation of OB-GYNs\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Notably, fewer residents means fewer doctors on the floor. To remedy that vacuum, USF hired locum doctors, who Ros described as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe substitute teachers of the doctor world\u8221\'20\'1D. Still, routine care appointments at USF are significantly pushed back. In August, the earliest a new patient could book a prenatal visit was November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have people who \u8230\'20\'26 don\u8217\'20\'19t get their first ultrasound until 30 weeks, because they just can\u8217\'20\'19t get in,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Delays in prenatal care represent more than inconveniences. These scheduling issues can be dangerous. If a patient doesn\u8217\'20\'19t receive first trimester care, they might miss windows for early genetic screening tests, or fail to safely treat diabetes or hypertension at a critical time in fetal development.\par \par \uc2 When chronic diseases are not managed as well, the overall risk of any pregnancy goes up, explained Erika Werner, chair of obstetrics and gynecology at \uc2 Tufts\uc2  medical center in Boston. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you don\u8217\'20\'19t have your first visit until 14 weeks, you don\u8217\'20\'19t have the same access to prenatal testing. You may not have an early ultrasound that reveals a major structural problem,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Romeo Galang, an OB-GYN and medical officer in CDC\u8217\'20\'19s division of reproductive health, echoed the importance of early treatment, noting that \u8220\'20\'1Cquick and accurate treatment at the first sign of a serious pregnancy-related complication can save lives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/988823?form=fpf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Even parts of the country}}}\uc2  without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are struggling to keep up with care needs. Christina Han, the director of maternal fetal medicine at UCLA in California, pointed to the influx of out-of-state patients seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and how it stretches the workforce thin.\par \par \uc2 Han specializes in complex procedures like multifetal reduction \u8211\'20\'13 an operation that must be completed early in pregnancy. That time crunch means that if a patient travels to LA for urgent reproductive care, which Han says is happening with increased frequency, the hospital has to defer local patients\u8217\'20\'19 scheduled operations, including terminations, that are not as time sensitive.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to tell our patient who is struggling with a miscarriage \u8230\'20\'26 that we just can\u8217\'20\'19t get them in. And that is an emotional, physical burden for these patients,\u8221\'20\'1D said Han.\par \par \uc2 Werner, who also chairs the \uc2 Society for Maternal Fetal Medicine\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s health policy and advocacy committee, explained that this displacement effect is especially pronounced in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://states.guttmacher.org/policies/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states that directly border those with restrictive policies}}}\uc2 , and are now receiving the lion\u8217\'20\'19s share of out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, which borders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/14/west-virginia-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 West Virginia}}}\uc2 , Stolfer remembers thinking about women in other states as she awaited care for her miscarriage. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was one way that I was trying to make peace with how long it was taking,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Dobbs is not to blame for all of these stressors, however. A nationwide OB-GYN shortage has been on the horizon since well before the supreme court struck down Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s always existed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ros in Florida, contending that workforce strains have been a topic of discussion since she was in medical school in the early 2000s.\par \par \uc2 Kousalya Siva describes the shortage as \u8220\'20\'1Ca significant driving force\u8221\'20\'1D behind her choice of field.\par \par \uc2 For years, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bhw.hrsa.gov/sites/default/files/bureau-health-workforce/data-research/projections-supply-demand-2018-2030.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 studies have predicted}}}\uc2  that population rise paired with an ageing workforce would lead to gaps in women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare. Ruth Crystal of \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  also points to the high risk for medical malpractice litigation and demands for long and irregular hours as factors that steer people away from the field.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COB care is a 24-hour-a-day job,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CBabies don\u8217\'20\'19t come only between banking hours.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As in other sectors of the economy, awareness of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.njspotlightnews.org/video/major-ob-gyn-shortage-predicted-for-nj-rest-of-us/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 burnout}}}\uc2  (which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/news/news-articles/2019/10/why-ob-gyns-are-burning-out"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 OB-GYNs suffer in high numbers}}}\uc2  ) and desire for greater work-life balance have prompted doctors to limit their working hours in ways they previously did not.\par \par \uc2 Taken together, this confluence of factors means physicians across the country have fewer hours to devote to patients for routine care.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Constricted access hits some populations more than others.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, 36% of counties are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marchofdimes.org/peristats/data?top=23"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 maternity care deserts}}}\uc2 , meaning they lack any obstetric care facilities or providers, explains Amanda Williams, clinical innovations adviser at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s California Quality of Care Collaborative. The affected populations are also disproportionately people of color, people with lower incomes, and people in rural areas \u8211\'20\'13 groups that already face care inequities.\par \par \uc2 In the US, Black women are experiencing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/23/america-black-maternal-mortality-crisis#:~:text=For%20Black%20women%2C%20these%20maternal,in%20the%20last%20two%20decades."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 soaring maternal mortality rates}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of these things compound for poor maternal health outcomes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Williams. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen patients give birth in these maternity deserts, they have higher rates of preterm births and maternal deaths.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Resolving the crisis won\u8217\'20\'19t be easy, said Crystal, underscoring the need for increased funding, especially in underserved regions, and student loan forgiveness, most notably for doctors who choose to practice in care deserts.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, Ros also points to government-mandated limits on the number of federally funded residency positions in each field, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aamc.org/news/medical-school-enrollments-grow-residency-slots-haven-t-kept-pace"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have not been raised since 1996}}}\uc2  despite population growth. \u8220\'20\'1CThey need to undo these caps,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Werner believes the most critical fix is abolishing restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and creating \u8220\'20\'1Cparity across all states\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s only when it comes to OB-GYN care that what you can get in one state is different than what you can get in another,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat just forever means that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have unequal care in different states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230822ej8m001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
