\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Ohio Republicans bet voters would dilute their own power. They lost\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levine in New York \par \uc2 939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 August 2023\par 10:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Referendum shows concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access remain a powerful force in American politics\par \par \uc2 When Ohio Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/23/ohio-abortion-rights-republican-ballot-super-majority"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decided to rush}}}\uc2  a constitutional amendment on to the statewide ballot this year, they made a big bet.\par \par \uc2 They bet that turnout would be low in an election held in the middle of the summer (it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ohiosos.gov/elections/election-results-and-data/historical-election-comparisons/voter-turnout-in-primary-elections-even/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just 8%}}}\uc2  in last year\u8217\'20\'19s August contest). They bet that voters in the state, which voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016 and 2020, would willingly dilute their own political power, raising the threshold to pass constitutional amendments from a simple majority to 60%. And they bet that voters would embrace such an idea knowing that a constitutional amendment on the ballot this fall would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 All three of those gambles backfired on Tuesday. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/08/ohio-issue-1-rejected-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio voters overwhelmingly rejected}}}\uc2  the proposal, called Issue 1. Unofficial results show that more than 3 million people voted in the election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/politics/elections/state/2023/08/09/issue-1-aug-8-voter-turnout-over-38-why-was-turnout-so-high/70558321007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 38% turnout}}}\uc2  that exceeded expectations.\par \par \uc2 But beyond reproductive rights, the Ohio contest also signals the growing awareness of and backlash to efforts to change voting rules to benefit politicians. Protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is extremely popular in the United States. Efforts that would make it harder to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights can help voters understand how Republican politicians are trying to tilt elections to thwart majority rule.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose two issues are now conflated. They\u8217\'20\'19re going to remain conflated,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sarah Walker, the policy and legal advocacy director at the Ballot Initiative Strategy Center, a left-leaning group that works on ballot initiatives. \u8220\'20\'1CIt looks like a power grab from politicians. It looks like a consolidation of power and an inability to listen to the majority-will of the voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The election result is a firm data point showing how reproductive rights continue to motivate voters in the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision last year overturning Roe v Wade. Since the decision, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have been on the ballot in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/ohio-issue-one-reject-loss-abortion-rights-ballot-measure-rcna98842"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seven states}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the pro-choice position has prevailed in all of them.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/04/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats won}}}\uc2  a critical state supreme court seat earlier this year by building a campaign around protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fighting gerrymandering. And {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/07/michigan-proposal-2-voting-access-overturning-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in Michigan }}}\uc2  last year, voters overwhelmingly approved separate ballot initiatives supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and voting rights while flipping the state legislature to Democratic control.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen over and over again now since the Dobbs decision, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wins elections,\u8221\'20\'1D said Amanda Litman, the co-founder of Run for Something, a non-profit organization that focuses on down-ballot races. \u8220\'20\'1CThere were points during the campaign for Issue 1 where the Republicans were trying to make the case this was about something other than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, the impending election in November. People smelled the bullshit. And they made it quite clear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, the victory was especially significant given that Republicans in the state legislature have openly rebuffed the will of the voters recently. Last year, they ignored a constitutional amendment passed by voters in 2015 to end extreme partisan gerrymandering in the state legislature. They kept districts distorted to preserve their supermajority in the state legislature, and then used that majority to send Issue 1 to the ballot. Voters issued them a rebuke on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Jen Miller, the president of the Ohio chapter of the \uc2 League of Women Voters\uc2 , said that the campaign against the initiative didn\u8217\'20\'19t lean on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to convince voters. Instead, she said, organizers focused on how the stakes extended beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and emphasized neighbor-to-neighbor canvassing to educate people about significant changes in Ohio that had come through constitutional amendments.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were not really talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the field,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe League\u8217\'20\'19s message was very clear that this was bigger than one issue, bigger than one party, bigger than one election. And people understood that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2 , the CEO of TargetSmart, a Democratic data firm, said early vote totals in Ohio offered a glimpse of who was turning out to vote (he was still waiting for full results to get a comprehensive picture of the electorate). Women accounted for 60% of those who voted early this year but did not vote in 2022 and there were also signs of increased turnout in the state among Black women and young voters, he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you can get 3.1 million people out to vote in an August election in Ohio, on something that isn\u8217\'20\'19t even directly voting on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but is tangentially, it clearly speaks to the organizing and mobilizing power of the issue,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The result in Ohio is unlikely to stop Republicans in the state or elsewhere from trying to make it harder to amend state constitutions. Republicans in Missouri {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.stltoday.com/news/local/government-politics/despite-ohio-vote-missouri-gop-still-pushing-to-make-it-harder-to-change-state-constitution/article_e10d4fe8-36c6-11ee-81a6-b36d15567527.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are pushing}}}\uc2  a similar measure to raise the threshold for passing a constitutional amendment ahead of an expected referendum to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights there. \u8220\'20\'1CI think you\u8217\'20\'19ll probably see the question coming back,\u8221\'20\'1D Matt Huffman, the Republican president of the Ohio senate, said on Tuesday evening, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/open/2023/08/state-issue-1-is-probably-coming-back-in-the-future-ohio-senate-president-says.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to Cleveland.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Miller predicted that another effort to raise the amendment threshold in Ohio would not be successful.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI sincerely hope that last night\u8217\'20\'19s victory is a cautionary tale to lawmakers and special interests who may consider other brazen power grabs in the future,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily see an appetite to revisit that in any way. But if they do, they need to know that Issue 1 created a pan-partisan movement to defend voter rights in our democracy here in Ohio. And we\u8217\'20\'19re ready.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230810ej8a001jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
