\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Ohio saw through the con\u8217\'20\'19:  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights advocates celebrate Issue 1 result\b0\par\par\uc2 Ava Sasani \par \uc2 1,015 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2023\par 20:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists vow to \u8216\'20\'18continue the fight\u8217\'20\'19, supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  retain hope for November election\par \par \uc2 In a resounding victory for supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Ohio voters on Tuesday overwhelmingly rejected Issue 1, a GOP-backed ballot measure that would have made it considerably harder to amend the state constitution.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates celebrated the defeat of Issue 1, which means Ohio keeps its longstanding rule on citizen-led state constitutional amendments: a simple majority of state voters can change the constitution through a ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 The vote sets the stage for a battle over reproductive rights in November, when Ohioans will decide if the state constitution should guarantee a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COhioans understood that if we didn\u8217\'20\'19t stop this amendment, we\u8217\'20\'19d never be able to directly go to the voters again, on any issue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kellie Copeland, executive director of Pro-Choice Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Republicans billed this week\u8217\'20\'19s election, and Issue 1, as a means of protecting Ohio from out-of-state special interests. But the state GOP previously eliminated August special elections because of the high cost to taxpayers and comparatively low voter turnout. The decision to revive special elections, which cost Ohio roughly $20m, appeared to be a transparent attempt to thwart the passage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment this November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Lord hates a hypocrite, and so do voters,\u8221\'20\'1D Copeland said.\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters rejected an effort by Republican lawmakers and special interests to change the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional amendment process.\par \par \uc2 This measure was a blatant attempt to weaken voters\u8217\'20\'19 voices. Ohioans spoke loud and clear, and democracy won.\u8212\'20\'14 Joe Biden (@JoeBiden) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JoeBiden/status/1689313592032600077?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 9, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Millions of Ohio voters cast ballots in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s election, with an estimated 57% choosing to reject Issue 1, according to the Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur opponents made a real tactical error by bringing this up in August in a way that\u8217\'20\'19s so intellectually offensive to Ohioans, creating a special election right after you ban special elections,\u8221\'20\'1D Copeland said.\par \par \uc2 The record-high turnout signaled the enduring power of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as an issue that will energize voters, even in a hastily-planned summer election. The Ohio results mirror last year\u8217\'20\'19s midterms, when voters in Michigan, Kentucky, Kansas and California all opted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Despite Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s setback, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents in Ohio vowed to continue the fight against November\u8217\'20\'19s ballot initiative.\par \par \uc2 Peter Range, Ohio Right to Life\u8217\'20\'19s CEO, said in a statement on Wednesday that \u8220\'20\'1Cthis result will not deter us from continuing to fight the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s extreme agenda this November\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access celebrated Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s vote, welcoming the results as a hint of what to expect in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COhioans made it clear in the conversations we had canvassing that they do not want our rights, voting rights or bodily autonomy, to be stripped from us,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jordyn Close, deputy director of the Ohio Women\u8217\'20\'19s Alliance.\par \par \uc2 We know Ohio voters don\u8217\'20\'19t want this initiative to go through, but turnout for special elections is always a toss up\par \par \uc2 Jordyn Close\par \par \uc2 She remained cautiously optimistic on Tuesday evening.\par \par \uc2 As the night wore on and results started rolling in, Close and a team of volunteers with the Ohio Women\u8217\'20\'19s Alliance prepared care packages for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients across the state. They kept one eye on the news while packing goodie bags with tea, snacks, warm socks and menstrual pads.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know Ohio voters don\u8217\'20\'19t want this initiative to go through, but turnout for special elections is always a toss up,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Close breathed a sigh of relief when the results were announced on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Many opponents of Issue 1 worried that Ohio voters could be swayed by outside interests. Richard Uihlein, a GOP megadonor based in Illinois, spent millions trying to get the measure passed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am just so incredibly relieved that Ohio voters saw through the con,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maria Phillis, an OhioOB-GYN who is training to specialize in high-risk pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Following the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade last summer, a law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 before most people know they are pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 took effect in Ohio. That six-week ban was then put on hold by an Ohio judge in October 2022, restoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state up to 22 weeks of pregnancy until further notice.\par \par \uc2 When Republican lawmakers approved Issue 1 last spring, Phillis became nervous.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey could try to bring back the six-week ban, or something even stricter,\u8221\'20\'1D she told the Guardian weeks before the August special election.\par \par \uc2 The Lord hates a hypocrite, and so do voters\par \par \uc2 Kellie Copeland\par \par \uc2 Phillis and other high-risk OB-GYNs sometimes have to terminate a pregnancy to save the life of the mother. The six-week ban created a narrow \u8220\'20\'1Cexception\u8221\'20\'1D for cases involving a medical emergency, but the vague language sowed widespread confusion and fear among doctors across Ohio.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe way the exception was written, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t specify when you can legally intervene to help a pregnant patient,\u8221\'20\'1D Phillis said. \u8220\'20\'1CDo you have to wait for them to become septic or sick?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Phillis feared that, without a state constitutional amendment to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Ohio Republicans would reinstate penalties for doctors who perform the procedure.\par \par \uc2 If the six-week ban returns to Ohio, Phillis would \u8220\'20\'1Chave to consider\u8221\'20\'1D relocating to a state that is less hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When the law was in effect, Phillis and her colleagues became consumed by anxiety.\par \par \uc2 If one overzealous prosecutor decided that Phillis misinterpreted the six-week ban\u8217\'20\'19s language on medical exceptions, her life and professional career as a doctor could be upended.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut to see these results, where we won, and not by a little bit, it really feels like the people of Ohio have our backs,\u8221\'20\'1D said Phillis. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like I can plan for a life here without worrying that I might have to uproot and move in the next year or two.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230809ej89002xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
