\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Win for reproductive rights as Ohio voters reject effort to make it harder to amend state constitution\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levine \par \uc2 746 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2023\par 17:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Proposal would have made it considerably harder to amend state constitution as voters give verdict on Issue 1\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters on Tuesday rejected a proposal that would have made it considerably harder to amend the state constitution in a major win for reproductive rights and democracy advocates in the state.\par \par \uc2 The result means that Ohio will keep its current process for amending the state constitution in place. The procedure first requires voters to collect a certain number of signatures from at least 44 of the state\u8217\'20\'19s 88 counties to send an amendment proposal to the ballot and then a simple majority to pass it.\par \par \uc2 Issue 1, the proposal under consideration, would have made both of those steps harder. It would have required voters to collect signatures in all 88 counties and then required a supermajority of 60% to pass it.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden said of the result: \u8220\'20\'1CDemocracy won.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It was the first time since 1926 that Ohio voters cast ballots on a proposed constitutional amendment in August. Republicans, who control the state legislature, rushed the measure to the ballot in an effort to make it harder to pass a referendum to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that\u8217\'20\'19s set for this fall.\par \par \uc2 With a little over half of the vote counted, the \u8220\'20\'1Cno\u8221\'20\'1D vote led 59% to 41%. A majority vote would have been needed to approve the amendment, which was not possible given votes left to be counted, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/ohio-abortion-rights-constitutional-amendment-special-election-227cde039f8d51723612878525164f1a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Associated Press projected}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday is a huge victory for the people of Ohio. Majority rule still stands in Ohio. The people\u8217\'20\'19s power is preserved because people like you showed up and overwhelmingly defeated Issue 1,\u8221\'20\'1D Dennis Willard, a spokesman for One Person One Vote, the coalition that opposed Issue 1, said at an election night rally, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AndrewJTobias/status/1689075438394425344"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to cleveland.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COhioans\u8217\'20\'19 support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and reproductive freedom was never in question. From defeating Issue 1 tonight to submitting nearly twice the amount of signatures needed to get a measure protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access on the ballot in November, Ohio voters have made clear that they will settle for nothing less than reproductive freedom for all,\u8221\'20\'1D the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group \uc2 Naral Pro-Choice America\uc2  said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans should be ashamed of their efforts to subvert the will of voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , a Democrat facing what is expected to be one of the toughest re-election bids next year, also praised the defeat of the amendment.\par \par \uc2 By rejecting Issue 1, Ohioans rejected special interests and demanded that democracy remain where it belongs \u8212\'20\'14 in the hands of voters, not the rich and powerful.\par \par \uc2 That is what has always guided me. I am proud to stand with Ohioans in this fight.\u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  (@SherrodBrown) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SherrodBrown/status/1689071920132599808?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 9, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 The issue underscores the continued political salience of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade last year. Campaigns linked to protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in politically competitive states, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 like a referendum in Kansas}}}\uc2  and a supreme court race in Wisconsin, have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/04/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 incredibly successful}}}\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have prevailed in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/ohio-issue-one-reject-loss-abortion-rights-ballot-measure-rcna98842"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all seven states }}}\uc2  where they have been on the ballot since last year.\par \par \uc2 The rejection also comes as activists are drafting a constitutional amendment that would strip lawmakers of their power to draw district lines and turn it over to a citizen-led commission instead. Last year, Ohio Republicans ignored repeated orders from the supreme court to draw maps that were not severely distorted to their advantage.\par \par \uc2 The rejection is a major win for the One Person, One Vote campaign, a broad coalition of good-government, labor and environmental groups that aggressively campaigned across the state urging people to reject the amendment. The contest was expected to be a low-turnout midsummer affair, but turnout actually surged during the election. The result is also a blow to Richard Uihlein, a GOP megadonor, who spent millions trying to get the amendment to pass.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen they forced Issue 1 on to the ballot, they awakened a sleeping giant and unleashed a movement. And that movement isn\u8217\'20\'19t going away tomorrow. It will continue to build and grow and to carry us through to victories in November and beyond. We thank Ohio voters for doing their homework and for going out to vote \u8216\'20\'18no\u8217\'20\'19 on Issue 1,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachael Belz, CEO of Ohio Citizen Action, which worked to reject the amendment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230809ej890008h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
