\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Republicans in Ohio are about to vote \u8230\'20\'26 to curtail the power of voting\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2023\par 14:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The sole question posed to voters in the special election is a direct attempt to stop the legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through democratic means\par \par \uc2 Technically, August special elections are supposed to be illegal in Ohio. Late last year, a Republican-backed bill passed the state house {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ohiosos.gov/media-center/press-releases/2021/2021-12-09/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prohibiting}}}\uc2  most special elections in August, reasoning that timing an election in the dog days of late summer depressed turnout, and cost too much money. But those same Republicans changed their tune in May, when it became clear that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters in Ohio would be able to put a ballot measure to voters securing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state in the November 2023 election. Ohio has a six-week ban on the books, but it is currently blocked by a court, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal up to 22 weeks of pregnancy. The measure, if passed, would help keep it that way, amending the Ohio state constitution to grant individuals a right to \u8220\'20\'1Cmake and carry out one\u8217\'20\'19s own reproductive decisions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ballot measures have been extremely successful tools of the pro-choice movement since the supreme court abolished the federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right last year in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health: pro-choice ballot initiatives passed by surprisingly large margins even in Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s heavily Republican neighbor state, Kentucky, as well as the similarly deep-red Kansas. Since Dobbs, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/08/07/opinion/abortion-democracy-ohio.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 every single time}}}\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have been put to the voters, they have prevailed. And so suddenly, the Ohio GOP felt that it was important that a vote be held in August: a vote, that is, to curtail the power of voting.\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters head to the polls on Tuesday to vote on Issue 1, the Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 response to the November constitutional amendment. The sole question posed to voters in the August special election is a direct attempt to stop the legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through democratic means: if passed, Issue 1 would make it more difficult for a ballot initiative to be brought to Ohio voters, and more difficult to pass one that was. The rule change would require advocates to collect signatures in all Ohio counties before a proposal could be placed on the ballot \u8211\'20\'13 a procedure that would give disproportionate power to rural, conservative parts of the state \u8211\'20\'13 and raise the threshold for passage from 50% to 60%. Currently, the pro-choice ballot initiative slated to go before Ohio voters in November polls at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/4115739-almost-60-percent-of-ohio-voters-back-abortion-rights-amendment-poll/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 58% approval}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and Issue 1 in Ohio has become a proxy for the broader fight many Republicans are waging across the states: when voters don\u8217\'20\'19t like the party\u8217\'20\'19s proposed policies \u8211\'20\'13 and overwhelmingly, voters do not like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8211\'20\'13 then instead of changing their platforms or setting out to persuade the electorate to change their minds, Republicans simply change the rules, so that the voters\u8217\'20\'19 wishes don\u8217\'20\'19t get in the way of their preferred policy outcomes. Don\u8217\'20\'19t want to vote for the Republican party line? Then state Republicans will make sure that your vote doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter.\par \par \uc2 The Issue 1 special election is just the latest in a string of efforts by state Republican parties to curtail access to ballot measures. In Missouri, a court ruled that a ballot initiative seeking to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be presented on the 2024 ballot, even though the Republican attorney general there, Andrew Bailey, had tried to stonewall the effort by falsely claiming that the vote would cost the state a gargantuan amount of money. But state Republicans there had already pushed another measure {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://missouriindependent.com/2023/05/09/major-overhaul-of-initiative-process-one-step-from-missouri-ballot/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 through the state house}}}\uc2 , requiring ballot initiatives to receive at least 57% of the vote to pass. Like in Ohio, Missouri was unable to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure off the ballot. But just as Ohio Republicans are doing, the Missouri GOP tried to rig the process, explicitly to lessen the pro-choice side\u8217\'20\'19s chances. The measure failed in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/ballot-initiative-petition-voter-missouri-republican-8e4d5b8293eb864235cf347410d5d7f5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri state senate}}}\uc2 , but Republicans there have vowed to try again. Republicans in at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/ohio-issue-1-abortion-ballot-measures/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nine other states}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 Arizona, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lwv.org/legal-center/king-v-thurston"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arkansas}}}\uc2 , Florida, Maine, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-initiative-process-mississippi-ballot-election-b138244fb4e20c6d974392812a3a3ddc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mississippi}}}\uc2 , North Dakota, Oklahoma, South Dakota and Utah \u8211\'20\'13 have also tried to make it harder for ballot initiatives to pass, at least when those initiatives support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the only issue where Republicans have sought to curtail access to direct democracy in order to protect their policy goals. In South Dakota, an effort last year to raise the ballot initiative passage threshold to 60% was aimed mostly at stopping Medicaid expansion in the state. (It failed.)\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been the issue around which America\u8217\'20\'19s anti-democratic forces are most determined and inventive. In Texas, for instance, Republican politicians have responded to local prosecutors in large, Democratic-leaning cities like Houston who say they will not prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/05/19/senate-prosecutors-abortion-voter-fraud/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passing a bill}}}\uc2  allowing those prosecutors to be removed for \u8220\'20\'1Cmisconduct\u8221\'20\'1D. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2023/02/12/republicans-target-abortion-local-prosecutors-00082386"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Similar bills}}}\uc2  aiming to curtail the authority of elected district attorneys over whether or not to enforce criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have also been brought forward by Republicans in Georgia, Indiana and South Carolina. Like the limits on ballot initiatives, the limits on the discretion of local DA\u8217\'20\'19s also aim to end the ability of public opinion to influence policy outcome. If you don\u8217\'20\'19t want to vote for the Republican policy, the Republicans will make sure your vote doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter; and if you vote in an official who will pursue a different policy, the Republicans will make sure that official loses the authority to do her job.\par \par \uc2 Maybe it\u8217\'20\'19s appropriate that Republicans have made the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crusade the focus of so much of their anti-democracy efforts. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, after all, are substantively anti-democratic. They are unpopular, yes, imposed by the unelected supreme court. But more importantly they are an insult to citizenship, depriving half of Americans the ability to live their lives with freedom, dignity, bodily integrity and self-determination \u8211\'20\'13 preconditions to any meaningful, equal status as citizens. It makes sense that Republicans would embark on sneaky, procedural efforts to undermine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in pursuit of this same project. They don\u8217\'20\'19t want to allow women to live as full, equal citizens. But really, they don\u8217\'20\'19t especially want that for anyone else, either. In justifying his decision to overturn Roe v Wade, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , who wrote the majority opinion, wrote that if women didn\u8217\'20\'19t like what he was doing to them, they could just vote. \u8220\'20\'1CWomen are not without political power,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote. At least, the Republican ones aren\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230808ej88001p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
