\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans pushed a special election in Ohio \u8211\'20\'13 what does it mean for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights?\b0\par\par\uc2 Ava Sasani \par \uc2 794 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2023\par 14:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 Issue 1 proposal raises threshold for a change in state constitution from a simple majority to 60% of votersUS politics live \u8211\'20\'13 latest updates\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Ohio will hold a special election that could make it harder for the state\u8217\'20\'19s voters to amend their own constitution.\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers in May approved \u8220\'20\'1CIssue 1,\u8221\'20\'1D a proposal that would raise the threshold required to pass any future ballot measure amending Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s constitution from a simple majority to 60% of voters.\par \par \uc2 Voting \u8220\'20\'1Cyes\u8221\'20\'1D on Issue 1 would make it more difficult for Ohio citizens to amend the state constitution, raising the threshold to 60% of all voters. The change would be effective immediately.\par \par \uc2 Voting \u8220\'20\'1Cno\u8221\'20\'1D on Issue 1 would keep the current method of creating and passing a citizen-led state constitutional amendment. Since 1912, Ohio has allowed a simple majority of voters to approve constitutional amendments through ballot questions.\par \par \uc2 Why did Republicans create Issue 1?\par \par \uc2 Issue 1 marks a bizarre reversal for Ohio Republicans, who earlier this year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.legislature.ohio.gov/legislation/134/hb458"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enacted a law}}}\uc2  that eliminates almost all August special elections. Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s election will cost Ohio an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.legislature.ohio.gov/download?key=20641&format=pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated $20m}}}\uc2 , a price tag Republicans previously said was too high for a day that traditionally sees low voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 But the GOP backtracked earlier this year, when a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates in Ohio prepared to bring a ballot initiative on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to voters in November 2023.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe hypocrisy is just incredible, the way they are terrified of this very democratic process,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kellie Copeland, executive director of Pro-Choice Ohio and a member of Ohioans for Reproductive Freedom.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/religious-landscape-study/state/ohio/views-about-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio Republicans}}}\uc2  tend to be less supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights than independent voters and Democrats, but a recent poll from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2023/07/24/ohioans-back-abortion-rights-amendment-a-test-case-for-other-states/70413318007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 USA Today Network/Suffolk University}}}\uc2  showed rising support for a state constitutional amendment protecting the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politician strategy is backfiring,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jordyn Close, a lifelong Ohioan and deputy director of the Ohio Women\u8217\'20\'19s Alliance. \u8220\'20\'1COhioans are seeing that the Republicans are willing to absolutely decimate democracy, they are willing to change the way any ballot initiative works, just to limit people\u8217\'20\'19s bodily autonomy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Ohio lawmakers who championed this year\u8217\'20\'19s August special election {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ohiosos.gov/globalassets/elections/2023/spec/issuereport.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said Issue 1}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwill protect Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s Constitution similar to the way the US Constitution has been protected since our country\u8217\'20\'19s founding\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 How will Issue 1 change the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 Last month, Ohio officials approved the November ballot initiative on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after Close and her colleagues gathered almost double the amount of signatures needed to pose a state constitutional question to voters.\par \par \uc2 The November ballot will ask if the Ohio constitution should guarantee a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If Issue 1 passes in August, it would take immediate effect, meaning it will be harder \u8211\'20\'13 though not impossible \u8211\'20\'13 for Ohio voters to reach the number of votes required to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Following the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade last summer, a 2019 law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 before most people know they are pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 took effect in Ohio. That six-week ban was then put on hold by an Ohio judge in October 2022, restoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state up {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/21/ohio-abortion-referendum-paperwork-filed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to 22 weeks of pregnancy until further notice.}}}\par \par \uc2 Ohio has since become a critical destination for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients in the surrounding region, as neighboring states like Indiana, Kentucky and West Virginia enacted sweeping bans on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But the Ohio six-week ban could soon be reinstated by the state supreme court, which is currently reviewing the lower court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to block the law from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think everyone understands that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Ohio is really hanging on by a thread right now,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jessie Hill, a Cleveland-based law professor who helped lead the lawsuit challenging Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Hill said the Ohio supreme court will \u8220\'20\'1Calmost definitely\u8221\'20\'1D wait to release their ruling on the six-week ban until after voters weigh in on the constitutional question in November.\par \par \uc2 If November\u8217\'20\'19s ballot initiative on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates worry that the conservative majority on the state\u8217\'20\'19s high court could be emboldened to reinstate the six-week ban, which Hill said would \u8220\'20\'1Cdevastate\u8221\'20\'1D reproductive healthcare access across the midwest.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the seventh most populous state in the country, keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  available is really important here, just looking at the sheer number of people impacted by a potential ban,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CA win in November, with people proactively voting to affirm the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that would be very, very important.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230808ej880018k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
