\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The election that could determine the future of democracy in Ohio\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levine in Westlake, Ohio \par \uc2 2,083 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2023\par 11:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ohio will be voting on a measure that makes it harder to amend the constitution in a high-stakes election \u8211\'20\'13 and the Republican proposal is \u8216\'20\'18minority rule\u8217\'20\'19, experts say\par \par \uc2 An under-the-radar election in Ohio on Tuesday has quietly emerged as one of the most high-stakes stress tests for American democracy in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The question Ohio voters will decide on 8 August is simple: how easy should it be to amend the state constitution? Like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/Initiated_constitutional_amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 17}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/Initiated_constitutional_amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other states}}}\uc2 , Ohio allows citizens to place constitutional amendments on the statewide ballot if they get a certain number of signatures and more than 50% of the statewide vote. The process has been in place for more than a century in Ohio, and in November, voters will use it to decide whether to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In May, Republicans who control the state legislature {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/23/ohio-abortion-rights-republican-ballot-super-majority"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abruptly sent a proposal}}}\uc2  to the ballot called Issue 1 that would make it much harder to change the constitution. If approved, a constitutional amendment would need 60% of the vote to pass instead of a simple majority. It would also make it significantly harder for citizens to even propose a constitutional amendment, requiring signatures from 5% of the voters in all of Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s 88 counties (the state currently requires organizers to get signatures in 44).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt absolutely is minority rule,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/12/ohio-republican-judge-blocking-party-from-distorting-electoral-districts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maureen O\u8217\'20\'19Connor}}}\uc2 , a Republican who served on the Ohio supreme court for nearly two decades and stepped down as chief justice at the end of last year, and opposes Issue 1, said in a telephone interview. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you get 59.9% of a vote that says yes, 40.1% can say no. This is the way it\u8217\'20\'19s gonna be. We can thwart the effort of the majority of Ohioans that vote. And that\u8217\'20\'19s not American.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The change in signature gathering would make it nearly impossible to get something on the ballot, which is already difficult, and only allow deep-pocketed groups to do so, said Jen Miller, the president of the Ohio chapter of the \uc2 League of Women Voters\uc2 , which opposes the amendment.\par \par \uc2 The campaign to raise the threshold has been largely funded by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/23/foundation-government-accountability-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Richard Uihlein}}}\uc2 , an Illinois billionaire and GOP mega-donor who has spent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/politics/2023/07/27/ohio-issue-1-supporters-critics-funded-by-out-of-state-donors/70383823007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $5m}}}\uc2  on the effort so far. A conservative non-profit backed by Uihlein, the Foundation for Government Accountability, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/23/foundation-government-accountability-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has been involved}}}\uc2  in efforts to raise the threshold for constitutional amendments across the country.\par \par \uc2 We can thwart the effort of the majority of Ohioans that vote. And that\u8217\'20\'19s not American\par \par \uc2 Maureen O\u8217\'20\'19Connor\par \par \uc2 Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AndrewJTobias/status/1603130384744534016?s=20&t=VwO_MBEHrPVV-CIASKq2UA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have made little secret}}}\uc2  of why {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.statenews.org/government-politics/2023-06-06/larose-says-issue-1-is-100-about-stopping-possible-abortion-amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they\u8217\'20\'19re in a rush}}}\uc2  to change the rules: this fall, Ohioans are set to vote on an amendment that would enshrine the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is 100% about keeping a radical pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment out of our constitution. The left wants to jam it in there this coming November,\u8221\'20\'1D the Ohio secretary of state, \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.statenews.org/government-politics/2023-06-06/larose-says-issue-1-is-100-about-stopping-possible-abortion-amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Republican}}}\uc2  running for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and one of the most prominent supporters of the amendment, said last month.\par \par \uc2 Similar measures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access have been extremely popular in other states and passed by wide margins after the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade last year.\par \par \uc2 Beyond reproductive rights, the August election has far-reaching implications for democracy in Ohio. Republicans hold a supermajority in the Ohio legislature after they manipulated district lines to their advantage last year, brazenly ignoring several rebukes from the state supreme court. Activists are already working to draft a constitutional amendment that would strip lawmakers of their redistricting authority entirely. But making it harder to change the constitution would essentially allow Republicans to keep their distorted advantage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COhioans would no longer have a tool accessible to them to keep the Ohio government accountable when they are not acting in the interest of our communities and our families,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jen Miller, the president of the Ohio chapter of the \uc2 League of Women Voters\uc2 , which opposes Issue 1.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about is that a small minority would be able to block the will of the majority of Ohioans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A very dirty trick\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Opponents of Issue 1 have assembled a wide-ranging coalition that includes civic action groups, unions, and environmental groups. The campaign, One Person One Vote, has received considerable funding from out-of-state progressive groups, and have aggressively canvassed across the state, sent mailings to voters, and held weekly community meetings throughout the summer.\par \par \uc2 One hot evening in July, Sarah Strinka, a 26-year-old canvasser with Ohio Citizen Action, one of the main groups opposing Issue 1, crisscrossed lawns in Westlake, a Cleveland suburb, in tie-dye sandals, making sure that people knew the election was happening on 8 August and trying to persuade them to vote against it. Nearly all of the voters who came to their doors that evening said they planned to vote against the change.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems like a very dirty trick to try and not get a major issue like this pushed through in an August election,\u8221\'20\'1D said Matt Jackson-McCabe, one of the voters who Strinka talked to. \u8220\'20\'1C I was like, \u8216\'20\'18This is bullshit.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like pulling the wool over the people\u8217\'20\'19s eyes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Daniel Hayden, 76, another voter in Westlake. \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans \u8211\'20\'13 they want to take away the control, to control everything. They want to take control away from the people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not everyone Strinka spoke with was entirely convinced. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s my understanding, because I\u8217\'20\'19m Republican, that this is a Democratic-led issue. Correct?\u8221\'20\'1D one man said in his driveway. Strinka pointed out that there was actually bipartisan opposition to the amendment. \u8220\'20\'1CIt impacts every single issue moving forward. I know a lot of people are talking about issues for November, but this could impact everything,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The man said he would do more research.\par \par \uc2 Republicans \u8216\'20\'18can kind of do what they want\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Ohioans have voted on hundreds of constitutional amendments over the last century, but they haven\u8217\'20\'19t been asked to vote on one in an August election since 1926. Last year, the turnout in August was so anaemic, just 8%, that Republicans decided to cancel August elections altogether.\par \par \uc2 But in May, they abruptly reversed that decision, saying that the cost of the election was worth it ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we save 30,000 lives as a result of spending $20 million, I think that\u8217\'20\'19s a great thing,\u8221\'20\'1D Matt Huffman, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republican who serves as the senate president, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/ohio-abortion-rights-fight-highlights-republican-electoral-vulnerabilities-2023-05-10/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told reporters}}}\uc2  earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 The rush seems to be a cynical calculation that the election will have low turnout and those who do cast a ballot will be highly motivated \u8216\'20\'18yes\u8217\'20\'19 voters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s a mistake to revive August elections for the sole purpose of passing an issue of such consequence,\u8221\'20\'1D said Robert Taft, a Republican who served as governor of Ohio from 1999 to 2007 and opposes Issue 1. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it were to pass, it\u8217\'20\'19d either have, possibly have 11 or 12% of eligible voters in Ohio deciding that the constitution shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be amended unless 60% agree.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And that calculation may be backfiring. Turnout during early voting has been strong \u8211\'20\'13 more than 533,000 people have cast their ballots so far. That far exceeds the 288,700 votes cast early in the May 2022 primaries. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is gubernatorial-level turnout,\u8221\'20\'1D an election official {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-ohio-constitutional-amendments-voters-turnout-election-65381bfc596ab5e34c830f67cef05fc2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Associated Press}}}\uc2 , which also reported some offices throughout the state were struggling to staff the massive turnout.\par \par \uc2 Polling has been limited and it\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to predict the results of a ballot referendum. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/politics/2023/07/19/ohio-issue-1-poll-finds-most-ohio-voters-oppose-august-ballot-issue/70383816007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 USA Today/Suffolk University pol}}}\uc2  l in July showed 57% of voters opposed Issue 1, while 26% supported it. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.onu.edu/northern-poll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 different July poll}}}\uc2  by \uc2 Ohio Northern University\uc2  showed voters were more evenly divided.\par \par \uc2 Those who support Issue 1 argue that it\u8217\'20\'19s already too easy for citizens to amend the constitution. That isn\u8217\'20\'19t supported by data; since 1912 citizens have sent 71 constitutional amendments to the ballot and just 19 have passed, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2023/07/20/author-of-book-on-ohio-constitution-warns-against-issue-1-changing-111-years-of-ohio-voter-power/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to Steven Steinglass}}}\uc2 , a professor emeritus at the College of Law of Cleveland State University, who has studied the constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think there should be that high of a threshold,\u8221\'20\'1D said Heidi M, a lawyer in Cleveland who declined to give her full name. \u8220\'20\'1CIs it going to be a higher burden to go out and get signatures for an initiative? Yes, but I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that\u8217\'20\'19s a bad thing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe constitution is supposed to be hard to change. But the constitution is not for making laws,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tomie Patton, the president of the Republican club in Avon Lake, just west of Cleveland. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you don\u8217\'20\'19t like the legislation then change the legislators like we have to everywhere else.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But in Ohio, changing the legislature isn\u8217\'20\'19t so easy. In 2015, voters overwhelmingly approved a constitutional amendment that required lawmakers to draw legislative districts that reflected the political balance of Ohio over the prior decade. When it came time to draw new districts in 2021, they should have drawn ones that reflected the 54-46 percent advantage Republicans have had in the state.\par \par \uc2 Instead, lawmakers drew maps that enabled them to keep a supermajority. The Ohio supreme court rejected the maps five different times, but Republicans were able to run out the clock and eventually adopt a map in their favor. Republicans used that supermajority to refer the measure to the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are well-aware of how critical that supermajority is. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can kind of do what we want,\u8221\'20\'1D Matt Huffman, the Republican senate president, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/2022/05/19/meet-matt-huffman-the-lima-republican-who-runs-ohio/7269099001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said last year}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Connor served as the key swing vote in each of the Ohio supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decisions until she stepped down from the court last year because she had reached the mandatory retirement age.\par \par \uc2 Now, she said, she and other activists are drafting a constitutional amendment that would take redistricting power away from the legislature entirely, putting it in the hands of a panel of citizens.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll have a constitutional amendment, hopefully, and that will change the entire playing field,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it\u8217\'20\'19s nothing but a good thing unless you\u8217\'20\'19re a member of the Republican supermajority.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Vote no\u8217\'20\'19 campaign presses on\par \par \uc2 With the election edging closer, the \u8220\'20\'1Cvote no\u8221\'20\'1D campaign has been emphasizing what they say is the fundamental unfairness of what it would mean to raise the threshold to 60%.\par \par \uc2 The same evening that Strinka knocked on doors in Westlake, a group of volunteers with the \uc2 League of Women Voters\uc2  sat under the pavilion at the local library and, over pizza, wrote postcards to voters urging them to cast their ballots against the amendment. One of them was Donna McGreal, 68, who said she hadn\u8217\'20\'19t really been politically active since the Vietnam war. Each of the postcards she wrote essentially had some version of the same message.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s simple,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote in neat cursive on the postcards. \u8220\'20\'1CIssue 1 tries to change it so 59% of the vote will lose to 41%. Keep majority rule in Ohio.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re hoping that message will resonate with voters like Sue Kuderca, 66, a retired nurse in Youngstown, who said she was leaning towards supporting the amendment because of her views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy mind is not 100% made up. It took me a while to even understand what yes or not meant. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  thing, I\u8217\'20\'19m pretty adamant about that. An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when ready to deliver at 9 months. That\u8217\'20\'19s murder,\u8221\'20\'1D she told Ron Gay, a canvasser with the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  who approached her in her driveway and urged her to vote no. (The proposed amendment in November would allow the state to continue to prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal viability, generally at 22-23 weeks. Many states, even while Roe v Wade was fully intact, did not allow abortions at nine months.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI totally understand. People feel that way about that. But that\u8217\'20\'19s in November. They\u8217\'20\'19re going to take away our right to change anything by majority vote in August,\u8221\'20\'1D Gay replied.\par \par \uc2 Kuderca said she would think about it.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 7 August 2023. The images in the article are of opponents of Issue 1, not supporters as well as earlier captions stated.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230806ej86000mc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
