\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Why Maryland became a haven for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  care after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fall\b0\par\par\uc2 Ava Sasani \par \uc2 1,064 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2023\par 18:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state has emerged as an example of how Democratic lawmakers can take proactive steps to bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  upended the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  enshrined in Roe v Wade, the immediate task before Democrats seemed simple: keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal in as many states as possible.\par \par \uc2 But over a year since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, some leaders in the reproductive rights movements worry that Democrats have tunnel vision, focusing their messaging and resources entirely on the legal tug-of-war over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the midwest and south.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVoters want to understand: what are you going to do to make things better? They don\u8217\'20\'19t want to just hear, oh, we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That\u8217\'20\'19s important, but that\u8217\'20\'19s not good enough,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andrea Miller, president of the National Institute for Reproductive Health, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Miller said the fight to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal is just one frontier in the larger battle for reproductive healthcare. She spent the past year pushing blue states to pass legislation that makes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  easily accessible, affordable and without stigma for patients and providers.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, Maryland has emerged as an example of how Democratic lawmakers can take proactive steps to bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, even in states where the procedure is and will probably remain legal.\par \par \uc2 Maryland is one of just eight states that require private insurers to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care with no cost-sharing.\par \par \uc2 In April 2022, in anticipation of the supreme court ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Maryland Democrats passed a bill that allows nurse practitioners, physician\u8217\'20\'19s assistants, and other medical health professionals to supervise abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBefore, we would only allow doctors to do it, even though nurse practitioners, midwives, all these other medical professionals are able to handle the similar things like miscarriages and deliveries,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Maryland house delegate Joseline Pe\u241\'00\'F1a-Melnyk, one of the bill\u8217\'20\'19s sponsors.\par \par \uc2 The law also directs the state to invest $3.5m a year for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/11/maryland-abortion-republican-governor-larry-hogan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -care training.}}}\par \par \uc2 If Roe fell, Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s solidly Democratic legislature could ward off any attempts at criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients or providers. But keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal was not good enough. Pe\u241\'00\'F1a-Melnyk and her colleagues wanted to ensure that Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics would have the capacity to deal with an inevitable surge of out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis case ending Roe didn\u8217\'20\'19t come out of nowhere, we saw it coming, so when the legislative session started in 2022, we started preparing,\u8221\'20\'1D Pe\u241\'00\'F1a-Melnyk said.\par \par \uc2 She said Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s investment in reproductive healthcare helped prepare the state for the coming months, when West Virginia, Ohio and other neighbors enacted a dizzying web of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The push to bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  resources in Maryland is part of what attracted Dr Anne Banfield to the state.\par \par \uc2 Dr Banfield spent over a decade of her career as an OB-GYN at a rural hospital in West Virginia. She relocated to Maryland in spring 2022, just after a leaked draft opinion revealed that the supreme court was poised to upend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I was in West Virginia, there was this constant hum in the background of, oh, what are the politicians going to do next,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t realize how much that negatively impacts your overall outlook until the pressure is gone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One year after Banfield moved, Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s governor, Wes Moore, signed a new set of protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and patients, including a measure that shields the state\u8217\'20\'19s doctors from legal liability if they provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CListen, we are a rural hospital, it is a place that typically has challenges recruiting providers,\u8221\'20\'1D Banfield said.\par \par \uc2 But in the past few months, Banfield said she has been able to hire recent graduates from top residency programs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can look at these candidates and say, this is a place where you can come and practice full-spectrum reproductive healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CHere is a place where you will be protected.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 States without affirmative protections for patients and providers could see increased \u8220\'20\'1Cbrain drain\u8221\'20\'1D, with doctors like Banfield relocating to places like Maryland. Reproductive health workers are increasingly wary of states that do not proactively push to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more affordable and freely accessible, beyond basic questions about legality.\par \par \uc2 Hanan Jabril, a full-spectrum doula and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organizer in Wisconsin, is preparing to apply to medical school in hopes of becoming an OB-GYN.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere I\u8217\'20\'19m applying, where I want to end up, it\u8217\'20\'19s something I\u8217\'20\'19ve been thinking about for years, and the political climate of different states is a huge part of that decision,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jabril. \u8220\'20\'1CStates like Wisconsin are just hemorrhaging residents in OB-GYN especially, because the work is being criminalized by these bans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When Roe was overturned last year, an 1849 law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  went back into effect in Wisconsin. The 19th-century ban is currently facing a legal challenge that is expected to land before the Wisconsin supreme court.\par \par \uc2 Jabril was part of a coalition of progressive organizers that helped elect a liberal justice to the state supreme court in April. With a liberal majority on the court, Wisconsin is poised to overturn the state\u8217\'20\'19s 1849 law.\par \par \uc2 But Jabril said that overturning the ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn\u8217\'20\'19t good enough.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s important, but I think people forget that this fight didn\u8217\'20\'19t start last year,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 Jabril said there were cost barriers to accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have long predated the overturn of Roe. Because of the Hyde amendment, passed in 1977, states are banned from using federal Medicaid dollars to pay for abortions, with very narrow exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Of the 32 states following the Hyde amendment, just four states help pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases where a pregnancy could cause long-lasting damage to a patient\u8217\'20\'19s physical health.\par \par \uc2 Jabril wants to train and practice medicine in a state like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/state-policy/explore/state-funding-abortion-under-medicaid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maryland or Illinois}}}\uc2 , which has voluntarily opted to use state dollars to cover all or most medically necessary \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople don\u8217\'20\'19t realize how much more there is to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access than just, the law says it\u8217\'20\'19s OK,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jabril. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you can\u8217\'20\'19t pay for it, or if there\u8217\'20\'19s no provider within driving distance, then \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still not accessible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230722ej7m000xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
