\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Group using \u8216\'20\'18shield laws\u8217\'20\'19 to provide  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  care in states that ban it\b0\par\par\uc2 Rebecca Grant \par \uc2 1,281 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 July 2023\par 16:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Aid Access ships medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to all 50 states under the protection provided to clinicians serving patients in banned states\par \par \uc2 Dr Linda Prine is providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to people in all 50 states, even those that have banned it. That might seem like an admission to be discreet about in post-Roe America, but Prine and her colleagues at Aid Access, a telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  service, are doing it openly and in a way they believe is on firm legal ground.\par \par \uc2 On 14 July, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aidaccess.org/en/page/3767036/usa-abortion-providers-aid-access-started-serving-all-usa-states.?fbclid=IwAR1gxcYKuU-H-uUqlPBa3AeGUfRmtjsX8ImP7xYcUt9mt81kQ20nAfPGoGk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Aid Access announced}}}\uc2  that over the past month, a team of seven doctors, midwives and nurse practitioners have mailed medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 3,500 people under the protection of \u8220\'20\'1Cshield laws\u8221\'20\'1D, which protect clinicians who serve patients in states where providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal. As soon as she learned about shield laws, Prine knew it represented an opportunity to go on the offensive, for those bold enough to try it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt made me think, OK, we need to fight back,\u8221\'20\'1D Prine said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t just take this lying down. We\u8217\'20\'19ve got to do something. And this was what we can do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 From its origins, Aid Access has always been willing to test legal boundaries. It was started in 2018 by the Dutch physician Dr Rebecca Gomperts. At the time, \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulations prevented licensed US providers from mailing mifepristone, one of the two drugs in the medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, so Aid Access was structured like Gomperts\u8217\'20\'19 other telemedicine service, Women on Web. That process involved \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers filling out an online consultation, and if eligible, Gomperts wrote a prescription from Europe and the pills were dispatched by a pharmaceutical partner in India.\par \par \uc2 Then, in 2020, Covid hit. And {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/health/federal-judge-rules-women-can-get-abortion-pill-without-doctor-visits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a federal judge suspended}}}\uc2  the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s in-person dispensing requirement for mifepristone. For the first time, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://undark.org/2021/04/05/digital-abortion-access/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legally prescribed medication \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  could be put in the mail}}}\uc2 . Aid Access used this opportunity to implement a hybrid model: In states where telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal, US clinicians handled the prescriptions, while in states where it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t, the pills continued to be mailed from India.\par \par \uc2 One drawback of shipping from India was the packages could take weeks to arrive. In addition to the stress and uncertainty involved in waiting, the time lag could push people past the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://srhr.org/abortioncare/chapter-3/abortion-3-4/medical-management-of-induced-abortion-recommendations-27-30-3-4-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12-week limit}}}\uc2  recommended by the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/23755658/abortion-pill-second-trimester-mifepristone-misoprostol"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 although there is some emerging research that \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills can safely be taken later}}}\uc2 .) Covid also created concerns about shipping delays, and there was always the chance that customs could seize the packages.\par \par \uc2 The experience of wanting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then needing to wait three or four weeks to get it to happen \u8230\'20\'26 that\u8217\'20\'19s just so hard\par \par \uc2 Dr Linda Prine\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe whole experience of wanting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then needing to wait three or four weeks to get it to happen, and not even be sure if those pills are ever going to come, that\u8217\'20\'19s just so hard,\u8221\'20\'1D said Prine, who started working with Aid Access in 2021. \u8220\'20\'1CWho wants to do that? Nobody.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In March 2022, Prine read an op-ed by three legal scholars \u8211\'20\'13 David S Cohen of \uc2 Drexel University\uc2 , Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9 of \uc2 Temple University\uc2 , and Greer Donley of the \uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 that introduced her to the idea of shield laws. The trio had published a paper titled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://columbialawreview.org/content/the-new-abortion-battleground/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The New \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Battleground}}}\uc2  in the Columbia Law Review, which outlined the ways that shield laws could protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who treated patients in banned states if Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCertainly, it\u8217\'20\'19s not a surprise that post-Dobbs, there are going to be medical care providers who want to push the limits and care for as many people as they can, including people in other states,\u8221\'20\'1D Cohen said in an interview. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are going to do this, so we were thinking about what can the states where they live do to help them the most?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Inspired by their work, a wave of states started passing shield laws. The first, in Connecticut, passed in May 2022. Massachusetts, the fifth state to pass a shield law in July 2022, was the first to include a telemedicine provision, meaning the state pledged to protect a provider licensed there who prescribed and mailed medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, via telemedicine, to a patient in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned \u8211\'20\'13 like Texas or Alabama. Currently, 15 states have shield laws in place, and five \u8211\'20\'13 Massachusetts, Washington, Vermont, Colorado and New York \u8211\'20\'13 have specific telemedicine protections.\par \par \uc2 Post-Dobbs, there are going to be medical providers who want to push the limits and care for as many people as they can\par \par \uc2 David S Cohen\par \par \uc2 Before Aid Access, no US providers had publicly tested them. Then, on 18 June, the organization started serving patients nationwide with providers licensed in those five states. Up to 13 weeks, they offer prescriptions for $150 with a sliding scale that asks people to pay whatever they can afford, with a shipping time of two-five days. (In a physical clinic, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2021.01528"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the median cost of medication \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is over $500}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow Aid Access is completely US-provider led,\u8221\'20\'1D Lauren Jacobsen, a nurse practitioner licensed in Massachusetts who joined Aid Access in February 2023, told the Guardian this month. \u8220\'20\'1CI think this is important because it sends the broader message that this is an American issue, a US problem, and taking advantage of the shield laws means we are returning this to an at-home solution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In addition to enabling faster shipping times, Jacobsen said some people also feel more secure knowing that the pills are coming from licensed clinicians through an \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved pipeline. This is part of what distinguishes Aid Access from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill suppliers that operate through unofficial channels, such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://msmagazine.com/2023/07/18/free-cheap-abortion-pills/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unregulated online pharmacies and clandestine community networks}}}\uc2 . While the nonprofit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plancpills.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Plan C}}}\uc2  has found those medications to be as advertised, and reliably safe and effective (and also, in the case of community networks, free), they don\u8217\'20\'19t offer interaction with a licensed clinician, and some people want that support as part of the process.\par \par \uc2 Right now, each of the Aid Access providers is sending approximately 50 packages a day. Prine said all the packing and postage and shipping tasks are a \u8220\'20\'1Cbig pain in the rear\u8221\'20\'1D, but it\u8217\'20\'19s manageable. They are prepared to scale, both in terms of infrastructure and in terms of the legal challenges their actions could invite.\par \par \uc2 Cohen suggests there will be a \u8220\'20\'1Ccoming battle\u8221\'20\'1D as shield laws get tested, and emphasized that providers have the greatest amount of protection while they are in shield law states. Jacobsen and Prine are not overly concerned about legal repercussions, but that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean they\u8217\'20\'19re not taking precautions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf it happens, it happens, and we are prepared,\u8221\'20\'1D Prine said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I\u8217\'20\'19m definitely not taking any vacations in Texas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Because shield laws are designed to protect providers, patient risk is a separate factor \u8211\'20\'13 one that\u8217\'20\'19s particularly acute for people from communities that face heightened surveillance from law enforcement. A state doesn\u8217\'20\'19t need to have an explicit law criminalizing people who have abortions to prosecute them, often under unrelated statutes, like the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/20/us/celeste-burgess-abortion-pill-nebraska.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 illegal concealment of human remains}}}\uc2 . Even before Dobbs, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ifwhenhow.org/resources/self-care-criminalized-preliminary-findings/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 people were arrested for self-managing abortions}}}\uc2 . The risk is real, but in a moment where people have too few options and time is of the essence, Prine said, every option counts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI do consults all the time, and people are not saying, \u8216\'20\'18What about the legality of this?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Prine said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is not their concern. Their question is, \u8216\'20\'18How soon will the pills arrive?\u8217\'20\'19 That is the number one question.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230723ej7n000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
