\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas women give harrowing testimony on impact of extreme  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 1,076 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2023\par 20:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women tell court about experience of carrying life-threatening pregnancies because of restrictive law with few exemptions\par \par \uc2 Texas women who were denied abortions took the stand and delivered harrowing testimonies of their experiences of carrying life-threatening pregnancies as a result of the state\u8217\'20\'19s high restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 The women are part of a lawsuit filed in March by the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , which is seeking clarification on which situations fall under the \u8220\'20\'1Cmedical emergency\u8221\'20\'1D exception in Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. The lawsuit appears to be the first of its kind in which women who were denied abortions are suing a state since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade last year.\par \par \uc2 In addition to losing their medical licenses, doctors who perform abortions in Texas can face fines of up to $100,000 as well as life in prison.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven if they provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that they believe complies with the ban\u8217\'20\'19s narrow exceptions, they still risk the laws being enforced against them,\u8221\'20\'1D the plaintiffs argued in court filings.\par \par \uc2 During Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing, plaintiff Samantha Casiano delivered an emotional testimony about being forced to give birth to a baby who died four hours later as a result of a fatal birth defect.\par \par \uc2 Twenty weeks into her pregnancy, Casiano discovered that her baby had anencephaly, a rare condition in which parts of the baby\u8217\'20\'19s skull and brain are missing. Casiano said that doctors informed her that they were unable to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state and that she was instead given funeral home information.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt like I was abandoned,\u8221\'20\'1D Casiano said. \u8220\'20\'1CI had this funeral home paper and this is just supposed to be a scan day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Casiano went on to describe her thought processes upon learning about her baby\u8217\'20\'19s condition, telling the court that she considered getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of state but was also afraid of losing her job or being jailed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt like I was imprisoned in my own body,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 At one point, as Casiano read aloud a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s note about her high-risk pregnancy, she became overwhelmed and vomited during the hearing. Following a recess, Casiano returned to the stand and said that recalling the events \u8220\'20\'1Cjust makes my body remember, and it just reacts\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI now have a psychiatrist \u8230\'20\'26 I now vomit a lot more. I\u8217\'20\'19ve never vomited before like that, ever, before my pregnancy. My body\u8217\'20\'19s never reacted that way,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 Another plaintiff, Amanda Zurawski, recounted her experiences of developing sepsis after she was refused an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when her water broke at only 18 weeks. Zurawski said her doctor told her that a miscarriage was inevitable but because her fetus still had a heartbeat, they could not induce labor.\par \par \uc2 Zurawski, who wanted to seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of state, said her situation was so severe doctors said she had to stay within 15 minutes of a hospital.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI had to listen to her heartbeat, simultaneously wanting to hear it and not wanting to hear it at the same time \u8230\'20\'26 If it stopped, they would be able to intervene,\u8221\'20\'1D Zurawski {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/07/19/texas-women-testify-abortion-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 testified}}}\uc2 , according to the Texas Tribune.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, when Zurawski started developing septic shock, she was rushed to the hospital where doctors finally agreed to induce labor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI went from feeling physically OK to shaking uncontrollably. I was freezing cold even though it was 110F out. My teeth were chattering violently. I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get a sentence out. My husband Josh asked me how I was feeling on a scale from one to 10. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t know the difference between one and 10 \u8211\'20\'13 which one was higher,\u8221\'20\'1D she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/women-suing-texas-abortion-bans-court-testify/story?id=101487004"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said, according to ABC News.}}}\par \par \uc2 Zurawski, who ended up delivering a deceased baby, said that as a result of the whole ordeal, she has been left with scar tissue and a fallopian tube which has permanently closed.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Brandt was another plaintiff who delivered emotional testimony. Brandt said she was forced to leave Texas and go to Colorado for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after one of the twins she was carrying was diagnosed with acrania, a rare and fatal condition in which the fetal skull is absent.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I had not gone out of state and had just done what was legal in Texas, my daughter Marley would most likely be in the NICU [neonatal intensive care unit] because she would have been born before 37 weeks. All my ultrasounds up to labor I would have had to watch twin A deteriorate more and more every week,\u8221\'20\'1D Brandt {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/women-suing-texas-abortion-bans-court-testify/story?id=101487004"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 , per ABC.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI would have had to give birth to an identical version of my daughter without a skull and without a brain and hold her until she died. Then I would have had to submit a death certificate and plan a funeral and decide if I wanted to bury her or cremate her. It just would have been heartbreaking. But instead I got to just give birth to my healthy daughter,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 Brand said that she does not \u8220\'20\'1Cfeel safe to have children in Texas any more\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know that it was very clear that my health didn\u8217\'20\'19t really matter, that my daughter\u8217\'20\'19s health didn\u8217\'20\'19t really matter, which was really heartbreaking,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys of the state have pushed back against the lawsuit, which includes more than a dozen women from Texas.\par \par \uc2 Amy Pletscher, an attorney for the state, told Associated Press the lawsuit was brought by women and doctors who \u8220\'20\'1Csimply do not like Texas\u8217\'20\'19s restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She claimed that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe purpose of this court is not to legislate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sixteen states, including Texas, do not allow abortions when a fatal fetal anomaly is detected, while six do not allow exceptions for the mother\u8217\'20\'19s health, according to an analysis by KFF, a health research organization.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit in Texas comes as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions elsewhere in the US continue to face challenges. On Monday, an Iowa judge temporarily blocked the state\u8217\'20\'19s new ban on most abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy, just days after Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  signed the measure into law.\par \par \uc2 The majority of US adults, including those living in states with the strictest limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , want it to be legal at least through the initial stages of pregnancy, according to a poll released in late June by the Associated Press-NORC Center for Public Affairs Research.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230720ej7k002jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
