\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Idaho sued over law banning adults from helping minors get abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Ava Sasani \par \uc2 909 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2023\par 16:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawsuit challenges statute that under-18s must get permission from parent or guardian before leaving state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates sued the Idaho government on Tuesday, claiming a state law that prohibits adults from helping minors get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Idaho has one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the nation, forcing patients to seek care in neighboring states such as Oregon and Washington, where the procedure is legal. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/06/idaho-abortion-trafficking-law-governor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in April}}}\uc2 , Idaho lawmakers passed legislation requiring any person under 18 to get permission from a parent or guardian before traveling out of state to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The controversial law marks the first major push since the fall of Roe v Wade in 2022 to explicitly block people from traveling across state lines to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is a new level of government intrusion on what it means to be an American,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wendy Heipt, a lawyer at Legal Voice, an advocacy group representing the plaintiffs challenging the Idaho statute.\par \par \uc2 The new lawsuit said the Idaho statute violates multiple constitutional protections, including the right to free speech, due process, and the ability to travel between states. Under the law, anyone who helps a minor get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be sentenced to two or five years in prison, but adults are also prohibited from \u8220\'20\'1Crecruiting\u8221\'20\'1D a pregnant minor, a term that reproductive justice advocates in the state said is confusing and vague.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDoes \u8216\'20\'18recruit\u8217\'20\'19 mean handing someone a brochure? Does that mean a conversation about options?\u8221\'20\'1D said Heipt. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is incredibly hard for the average person to know what they can and can\u8217\'20\'19t legally do under this law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lourdes Matsumoto, the only individual plaintiff on the case, is an Idaho attorney who works with survivors of domestic and sexual violence, many of whom are minors.\par \par \uc2 Before the law\u8217\'20\'19s passage in April, Matsumoto said she encountered cases where a child is impregnated through rape \u8220\'20\'1Croughly once per month\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She still hears stories of young assault survivors on a near-daily basis, but Matsumoto said she has stopped hearing about cases involving pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not that the problem has gone away, people are just more hesitant to reach out for help,\u8221\'20\'1D Matsumoto said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe law empowers abusers, if you don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to information, it is much, much harder to get out of a dangerous situation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There is particular confusion over what constitutes \u8220\'20\'1Crecruiting\u8221\'20\'1D a minor in the context of the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNobody knows what is illegal, which puts everyone at risk,\u8221\'20\'1D Matsumoto said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat if an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver just picks somebody up and drives them to the border, are they liable?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.idaho.gov/wp-content/uploads/sessioninfo/2023/standingcommittees/230327_ssta_0800AM-Minutes.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In March,}}}\uc2  the Idaho state senator Todd Lakey, a Republican who championed the law, conceded that the language is ambiguous.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRecruiting, harboring and transporting, those are descriptive words, I guess the court would have to decide if the conduct constitutes one of those three things,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lakey, roughly one week before the language was signed into law by Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs suing Idaho said the law was hastily cobbled together by Lakey and his fellow conservative legislators under the assumption that the state court system would rule against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Earlier this year, the Idaho supreme court upheld the state\u8217\'20\'19s sweeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, adding that the Idaho constitution does not protect access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But conservative judges, even those hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, might be skeptical of the new Idaho law. When the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled that there is no federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  wrote in his concurring opinion that people could still travel out of state to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing the constitutional right to interstate travel.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know travel bans are unpopular and maybe unconstitutional,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ziegler said Idaho was \u8220\'20\'1Crepackaging\u8221\'20\'1D the law as a kind of parental rights issue, not a travel ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIdaho\u8217\'20\'19s position is \u8216\'20\'18this isn\u8217\'20\'19t a ban on travel, the actions that are criminalized by this law are supposed to be taking place in Idaho,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 She said the law is designed to sidestep the constitutional boundary set by Justice Kavanaugh to instead focus on the preparations that an adult might make within Idaho. For example, helping a minor make an appointment at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Washington or Oregon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIdaho is a testing ground for many conservative causes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ziegler, pointing to the state\u8217\'20\'19s first-in-the-nation law restricting transgender children\u8217\'20\'19s ability to participate in sports.\par \par \uc2 If the court upholds the Idaho law, Ziegler warns that other conservative state legislatures might be emboldened to pass copycat laws. She also warned that Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s focus on minors might be the \u8220\'20\'1Ctip of the iceberg\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re [in] the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, you want this law upheld by the courts, but that isn\u8217\'20\'19t the endgame,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou want to leverage that ruling to go after the rights of adults.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The anti-trans legislation of today could foreshadow the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. For years, conservative lawmakers have restricted transgender minors\u8217\'20\'19 access to healthcare. In April, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/apr/26/trans-healthcare-conservative-republican-states-missouri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri}}}\uc2  became the first state to restrict adult patients\u8217\'20\'19 access to gender-affirming care.\par \par \uc2 Ziegler said the ultimate goal of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents who support the Idaho law is to \u8220\'20\'1Cstop everybody from traveling out of state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230712ej7c002bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
