\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Iowa Republicans consider six-week  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban during special session\b0\par\par\uc2 Nick Robins-Early \par \uc2 994 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2023\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bill faces few hurdles from being passed as state\u8217\'20\'19s house, senate and governor\u8217\'20\'19s office are all Republican-controlled\par \par \uc2 Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s state legislature held a special session on Tuesday ahead of voting on a bill the same day that would ban most abortions at about six weeks of pregnancy, when most people don\u8217\'20\'19t yet know they are pregnant. The state is the latest in the country to vote on legislation restricting reproductive rights after the overturning of Roe v Wade last year, which ended the nationwide constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , called for the special session last week, vowing to \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinue to fight against the inhumanity of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and calling the \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D movement against reproductive rights \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most important human rights cause of our time\u8221\'20\'1D. Lawmakers in the GOP-controlled legislature will debate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.legis.iowa.gov/legislation/BillBook?ba=HSB255&ga=90"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 house study bill 255}}}\uc2 , which was released on Friday and seeks to prohibit abortions at the first sign of cardiac activity except in certain cases such as rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s house, senate and governor\u8217\'20\'19s office are all Republican-controlled, and the bill faces few hurdles to being passed.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in state legislatures nationwide have rolled back reproductive rights after the end of Roe, and have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/us/abortion-laws-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banned or severely limited abortions}}}\uc2  in more than a dozen states. A number of those states, such as Texas, Alabama and Mississippi, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/us/abortion-laws-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed full \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2  with no exceptions for cases of rape or incest. The bans, which powerful Christian conservative groups have supported, are in conflict with the views of the majority of Americans who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2022/06/13/about-six-in-ten-americans-say-abortion-should-be-legal-in-all-or-most-cases-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 numerous}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/04/26/1171863775/poll-americans-want-abortion-restrictions-but-not-as-far-as-red-states-are-going"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polls}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-health-congress-government-and-politics-fc8c68d1c4dd29ef0a364050013b8cf4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 show}}}\uc2  are generally supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. A Des Moines Register/Mediacom Iowa {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/iowa-poll/2022/10/23/iowa-poll-abortion-laws-fetal-heartbeat-ban-roe-v-wade/69563125007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey from last year}}}\uc2  showed that about 61% of Iowans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 Heated protests and a public hearing\par \par \uc2 Before Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s legislature debated the bill, lawmakers held a public hearing on Tuesday that gave advocates and opponents of the legislation a chance to deliver brief statements. Each side was given equal time, alternating between speakers for over two hours.\par \par \uc2 Multiple medical professionals spoke in opposition to the bill, warning that passing it would endanger patients and prevent physicians from issuing care. Other reproductive rights advocates spoke on how the bill would take away people\u8217\'20\'19s autonomy and oppose the will of the majority of Iowans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat a privileged life you lead if you can only see pregnancy as a blessing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Amy Bingaman, an obstetrician and gynecologist. \u8220\'20\'1CYou would be forcing a woman to a lifelong obligation which affects her education, career, family and community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDo you know the intricacies and nuances and the pain and the joy and fear and everything that comes with having a uterus and looking for basic healthcare? Do you?\u8221\'20\'1D Naya Thomas, a birth doula and employee at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , told lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 A number of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  speakers backing the legislation came from Christian advocacy groups and framed their position as a human rights issue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe talk about women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, but we don\u8217\'20\'19t want to give those rights to girls in the womb,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maggie DeWitte, the executive director of Pulse Life Advocates, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group which also states on its website that \u8220\'20\'1Ccontraception kills babies\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A pastor from Des Moines who backed the legislation described how he stood praying outside a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  location once a week for seven years, and warned that \u8220\'20\'1Ceveryone in this room is going to stand before a holy god and give an account, and woe to the one who says, \u8216\'20\'18I arbitrarily chose death.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Another speaker in support of the bill, who identified himself as a medical student and president of his university\u8217\'20\'19s Christian medical association, compared denying rights to fetuses with slavery and the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 As the public hearing took place inside the chambers, a crowd of protesters from both sides waved signs and shouted slogans in the capitol rotunda. The Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , who had been campaigning in Iowa, also arrived at the capitol on Tuesday and was met with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/tjbarton83/status/1678781335337578497?"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights demonstrators chanting}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cfascist\u8221\'20\'1D at him.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/tjbarton83/status/1678842456509480960/video/1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 booed and shouted}}}\uc2  at Republican lawmakers after members of a subcommittee voted to advance the bill to a general house vote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re killing women!\u8221\'20\'1D one protester yelled, as state troopers escorted the demonstrators away from the chambers.\par \par \uc2 A split court decision forces vote\par \par \uc2 Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s bill is similar to a 2018 state law, then the most restrictive in the nation, that banned abortions at about six weeks. The Iowa supreme court later deemed that law unconstitutional in 2019 and blocked it from coming into effect. The court decided last year, however, that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-health-iowa-supreme-court-government-and-politics-0074e14defa4a6699e65d921f0edbc58"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is not a constitutionally protected right in Iowa}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a ruling that opened the door for lawmakers to restrict reproductive rights in the state.\par \par \uc2 The special session on Tuesday is taking place after Reynolds {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2023/06/16/sharply-divided-court-maintains-ban-on-iowas-fetal-heartbeat-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failed in her attempt}}}\uc2  to have the Iowa supreme court remove the injunction against her 2018 six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and allow it to be enforced. The Iowa supreme court justice Thomas Waterman called the state\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to enact the 2018 six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban \u8220\'20\'1Can unprecedented effort to judicially revive a statute that was declared unconstitutional\u8221\'20\'1D, and in his opinion last month referenced a previous ruling that made it illegal for police to search a suspect\u8217\'20\'19s garbage without a warrant.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be ironic and troubling for our court to become the first state supreme court in the nation to hold that trash set out in a garbage can for collection is entitled to more constitutional protection than a woman\u8217\'20\'19s interest in autonomy and dominion over her own body,\u8221\'20\'1D Waterman wrote.\par \par \uc2 The court was split 3-3 in its decision last month on reinstating the 2018 bill, resulting in a deadlock that meant a lower court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling blocking the ban remained in place.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230711ej7b001jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
