\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The sex ed class you wish you\u8217\'20\'19d had\u8217\'20\'19: the influencer doctors teaching Americans the basics\b0\par\par\uc2 Alaina Demopoulos \par \uc2 1,753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2023\par 11:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 With schools failing American students, OB-GYNs use TikTok to tackle questions and dispel myths\par \par \uc2 Some of TikTok\u8217\'20\'19s biggest stars aren\u8217\'20\'19t teen influencers or adorable pets \u8211\'20\'13 they are OB-GYNs posting sex education videos.\par \par \uc2 Need to know if you can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@busymomofmany/video/7236909778889428266?lang=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continue to take}}}\uc2  antidepressants while pregnant? Dr Keith L Riggs, a Houston-based OB-GYN, has got you covered. Want to see how an IUD is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@txshay/video/6853204578880605446?lang=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inserted into the uterus}}}\uc2  ? Check out a demo on the Dallas physician assistant Shay Blue\u8217\'20\'19s page. Have questions on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@alirodmd/video/6883964746350021893?lang=en&q=%23obgyn&t=1688581214796"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 what sex position}}}\uc2  is most likely to get you pregnant? Dr Ali Rodriguez \u8211\'20\'13 aka the Latina Doc \u8211\'20\'13 made a video for that. (Spoiler: it\u8217\'20\'19s whatever position you like the most \u8211\'20\'13 no method has emerged as a scientifically proven best choice.)\par \par \uc2 All kinds of doctors have joined TikTok. There are plastic surgeons and dermatologists who gleefully post videos hypothesizing what work an actor has had done. Dentists film videos \u8211\'20\'13 equal parts terrifying and mesmerizing \u8211\'20\'13 showing what plaque looks like as it\u8217\'20\'19s scraped from teeth. If you really want to see footage from a colonoscopy, hit up the urology corner of #healthtok.\par \par \uc2 But those who practice #OBGYN \u8211\'20\'13 a hashtag that has over 5bn views on the app \u8211\'20\'13 enjoy a particular kind of virality. And some of the most popular have parlayed their online fame into other ventures.\par \par \uc2 Dr Jennifer Lincoln, who has 2.8 million followers and claims to offer \u8220\'20\'1Cthe health class you wish you had in high school\u8221\'20\'1D, published a book on reproductive health in 2021 and hosts a podcast where she answers listeners\u8217\'20\'19 questions about all things sex. (Recent episodes include A Summer Period Survival Guide and Myth-Busting the Morning-After Pill.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s just a lot of people out there who do not know how to access things,\u8221\'20\'1D Lincoln, who lives in Portland, said. \u8220\'20\'1CCommenters have asked about anything from birth control to a pregnancy test. These are basic things we would have hoped to have been covered in sex ed, but that\u8217\'20\'19s not the case in the majority of states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Americans have been receiving inadequate sex education for decades \u8211\'20\'13 but in the last year, things have become even worse. The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s reversal of Roe v Wade has led to a flood of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  misinformation online, and Florida\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdon\u8217\'20\'19t say gay\u8221\'20\'1D law means that teachers can no longer lead classroom discussions on gender identity or sexuality. As LGBTQ+ students continue to be marginalized across the country, they lack information that can help them understand their bodies and cultivate a sense of autonomy.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago, people with concerns about their reproductive health might hit up anonymous Reddit boards for help \u8211\'20\'13 now, they can take their pick of TikTok experts to follow.\par \par \uc2 Dr Danielle Jones, who goes by @mamadoctorjones on TikTok, said she had joined the platform because that\u8217\'20\'19s where the kids are. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a good venue to do some sex education and dispel myths about things that impact people who are younger,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that if we can get into their heads early and dispel misinformation before they encounter it, it can keep them from falling down the rabbit hole.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And there are a lot of myths to dispel. Though \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  reports that the vast majority of parents support having sex education taught in middle and high school, the US is pretty terrible at teaching it. Only 30 states and the district of Columbia require sex education classes in schools, and those that do may stress harmful abstinence-only narratives or spread medically inaccurate information.\par \par \uc2 Since the fall of Roe, Lincoln\u8217\'20\'19s teen viewers have reached out to her after applying to college in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have been gutted, such as Texas, Florida, or Oklahoma. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re really scared, and they\u8217\'20\'19re not sure if they\u8217\'20\'19ll be able to access contraception,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CParents will also message me saying, \u8216\'20\'18My daughter is going to college, she has her heart set on the University of Texas, but I\u8217\'20\'19m scared for her. What should I do?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lincoln\u8217\'20\'19s answer: \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s talk about birth control and get Plan B and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills ahead of time, just in case. You may not think this is a conversation you have to have with your daughter, but in 2023, you do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jones, who practiced obstetrics in Texas before her family moved to New Zealand in 2021, said many of her followers reach out to her with questions they do not want to ask their primary care physicians.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn states like Texas, people are concerned about who they can safely ask about contraception,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you don\u8217\'20\'19t know how your healthcare provider feels about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you don\u8217\'20\'19t know if you can trust them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tiffany Connolly, a 26-year-old from Grand Rapids, Michigan, has learned helpful information from OB-GYNs on TikTok. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a useful source when it can be difficult to pinpoint certain things within my body,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t always just call up a doctor or make an appointment right away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Connolly, who does not want children, plans to get a tubal ligation after her IUD expires next year. Young people who seek sterilizations often have to visit multiple doctors before finding one who will agree to provide it, but Connolly found a spreadsheet posted by Dr Franziska Haydanek, a Rochester, New York, gynecologist with more than 300,000 followers, that lists the names of doctors across the country who are known to safely and responsibly perform the procedure on unmarried and childless patients.\par \par \uc2 Haydanek posted the spreadsheet last summer, right as the reversal of Roe v Wade pushed more women to consider the procedure as a means of permanent birth control. Since then, the video has been viewed over 50,000 times.\par \par \uc2 Krysten Stein, a PhD candidate in media studies, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ijoc.org/index.php/ijoc/article/view/18175/3702"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 written}}}\uc2  about TikTok gynecologists for a communications journal. \u8220\'20\'1CI wanted to know why these videos were getting so much traction,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen people seek these kinds of resources online, it\u8217\'20\'19s often because they don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to health insurance or doctors.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stein has polycystic ovary syndrome, which can cause irregular periods and pelvic pain, but often goes undiagnosed by doctors who downplay its symptoms as normal period side effects.\par \par \uc2 Years ago, Stein found refuge in online forums like Reddit, where she finally engaged with people who took her pain seriously. She suspects that people on TikTok form a similar kind of community on the app. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a platform where you can see other people who might be experiencing the same thing as you are,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Samantha Broxton lives in southern California and frequents OB-GYN TikTok, where, the 35-year-old mom said, she had learned things she wished her own doctors had told her years ago. It\u8217\'20\'19s been a resource for her, but she also wonders what type of care TikTok OB-GYNs provide their patients offline.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf they\u8217\'20\'19re talking about inequalities in medicine on TikTok, I want to know if they\u8217\'20\'19re vocal about it too in the workplace,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAre they working to improve the system, or is it just easy to talk about doing that online?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The American College of Gynecology and Obstetrics does not give doctors specific rules on how to use TikTok, but some hospitals and institutions have social media policies. For the most part, Stein said, doctors are on their own when it comes to deciding what information is appropriate to include in a TikTok.\par \par \uc2 They don\u8217\'20\'19t always get it right. Last year, four obstetrics nurses were fired from an Atlanta hospital for making a video mocking expectant mothers. Emory hospital, which employed the nurses, later released a statement saying the video was \u8220\'20\'1Cdisrespectful and unprofessional\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Should OB-GYN influencers take money from brands? When Stein interviewed some for her paper, there was no general consensus. Certain TikTok OB-GYNs said they would only accept deals with brands that felt aligned with their values \u8211\'20\'13 they were not just taking cash from anyone. Others were less judicious.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome of them said, \u8216\'20\'18I want to be a content creator full time,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Stein said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere were a lot of moral questions that came up around that. There are no rules, and right now it\u8217\'20\'19s based upon the specific person\u8217\'20\'19s moral compass.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And how do you know someone is actually a doctor, and not just playing one on TikTok? Lincoln noted that some creators are misleading in their credentials, calling themselves \u8220\'20\'1Chormone experts\u8221\'20\'1D in their bio. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a term some people use after reading a book or taking a weekend \u8216\'20\'18course\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 so, meaningless,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 There are also chiropractors, anesthesiologists, and generalists who are not reproductive health experts dispensing advice on the subject. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really confusing to people, because they see MD in the handle and think they\u8217\'20\'19re experts, though they\u8217\'20\'19re not experts in the field,\u8221\'20\'1D Lincoln said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis harms the OB-GYN TikTok space because these grifting experts often throw our field under the bus.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Actual gynecologists worth a 30-second watch, Lincoln says, are ones who cite their sources or at least let their viewers know when something is their opinion rather than a studied fact. \u8220\'20\'1CAs a rule, when I\u8217\'20\'19m explaining something medical, I always give references,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to be transparent about what we know and what we don\u8217\'20\'19t.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jones believes the most urgent part of her job right now is spreading accurate information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. She grew up in rural Texas and described herself as pro-life until going to medical school changed her mind. Now, she hopes to help others come to the same conclusion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve had people reach out and say that I\u8217\'20\'19ve helped them see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from a different perspective,\u8221\'20\'1D Jones said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s one of the most meaningful things I can hear: \u8216\'20\'18Two weeks ago I would have called you a murderer, but now I support the right to choose.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Still, she knows the limitations of TikTok activism. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat I do online is valuable, and it\u8217\'20\'19s a great supplement, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not going to fully replace sex education,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CYoung people need that, and we know the outcomes are not going to be good when they don\u8217\'20\'19t receive it in schools.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230707ej77001gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
