\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biden says supreme court \u8216\'20\'18misinterpreted the constitution\u8217\'20\'19 as he announces new student debt relief plan \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 L\u233\'00\'E9onie Chao-Fong (now) and Chris Stein (earlier) \par \uc2 10,956 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2023\par 22:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18new path\u8217\'20\'19 will rely on different law than one blocked by court to provide debt forgiveness to \u8216\'20\'18as many borrowers as possible\u8217\'20\'19Sign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Summary of the day\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a quick recap of today\u8217\'20\'19s developments:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  dealt a major blow to LGBTQ+ rights, ruling that a Colorado civil rights law which compels businesses and organizations to treat same-sex couples equally is in violation of the right to free speech. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/us-supreme-court-ruling-lgbtq-rights-colorado"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The decision, }}}\uc2  reached by a six to three vote of the conservative supermajority, could open the door for businesses to refuse service to some couples based on their sexuality \u8211\'20\'13 but could also have the same effect on willingness of service based on a customer\u8217\'20\'19s race or religion, gender or disability. The ruling prompted an excoriating dissent from the three liberal justices.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  expressed his fears that the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives may fuel discrimination against gay, lesbian and transgender Americans nationwide by striking down a Colorado law prohibiting businesses from turning away LGBTQ+ people. Biden vowed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649efee28f08d551cf54b3ca#block-649efee28f08d551cf54b3ca"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in a statement}}}\uc2  that his administration \u8220\'20\'1Cwill also work with states across the country to fight back against attempts to roll back civil rights protections that could follow this ruling.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said the court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling against the Colorado law \u8220\'20\'1Cthreatens future progress\u8221\'20\'1D. The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision \u8220\'20\'1Cdeparts from decades of jurisprudence by creating an exception to protections against discrimination in public accommodations\u8221\'20\'1D, she said in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649f484e8f08ff8b60151785#block-649f484e8f08ff8b60151785"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a statement. }}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The supreme court also ruled against the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s $430bn student debt forgiveness plan in a blow to up to 40 million borrowers in the US. In a 6-3 decision, the conservative-leaning supermajority of justices ruled that the 2003 Heroes Act does not authorize Biden\u8217\'20\'19s debt forgiveness plan. Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s loan forgiveness plan, announced last August, $20,000 per person of federal student debt would have been forgiven for borrowers who were Pell grant recipients and up to $10,000 of debt would have been forgiven for other borrowers.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  vowed the \u8220\'20\'1Cfight was not over\u8221\'20\'1D after the court ruled against his landmark student debt forgiveness plan. \u8220\'20\'1CI think the court misinterpreted the constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said, announcing his intention to pivot to another law to find another path forward. Biden promised to now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/30/joe-biden-new-student-debt-relief-plan-scotus-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  turn to the Higher Education Act of 1965}}}\uc2  to restore student debt relief. He also plans to enact a 12-month repayment program that would help people with student debt avoid defaulting on their loans if they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t pay and avoid years of bad credit ratings.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The education secretary, \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2 , said he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649f4b448f08d551cf54b673#block-649f4b448f08d551cf54b673"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly disagreed}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D with the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision, adding that the court had \u8220\'20\'1Cruled against more than 40 million working families\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re talking about low and middle income families recovering from the worst pandemic in a century,\u8221\'20\'1D Cardona said at a White House briefing.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The supreme court turned away a case challenging Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s rules around voting rights for people with felony convictions,{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/us-supreme-court-mississippi-voting-rights-case-black-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  leaving intact}}}\uc2  a policy implemented more than a century ago with the explicit goal of preventing Black people from voting. Those convicted of any one of 23 specific felonies in Mississippi permanently lose the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The supreme court agreed to next term take up a case that could give the conservatives a chance to further erode gun control laws. The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649f011a8f08ff8b60151511#block-649f011a8f08ff8b60151511"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will consider }}}\uc2  whether a federal law that takes guns away from people who are under a domestic violence restraining order is constitutional.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Indiana\u8217\'20\'19s highest court has allowed its near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to go into effect, a year after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling overturning Roe v Wade. Indiana was the first state in the nation to pass an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after the end of Roe v Wade, but its supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/12/indiana-supreme-court-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked the measure}}}\uc2  passed by the Republican-dominated legislature later in 2022.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Much of North Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s 12-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban will take effect on Saturday, after a federal judge temporarily blocked just one provision included in the law. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649f51a28f08ff8b601517bc#block-649f51a28f08ff8b601517bc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The move }}}\uc2  deals a blow to one of the last bastions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the south.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The state department released report critical of its handling of the chaotic US withdrawal from Afghanistan. America\u8217\'20\'19s diplomatic arm \u8220\'20\'1Cfailed to establish a broader task force as the situation in Afghanistan deteriorated\u8221\'20\'1D, and \u8220\'20\'1Cit was unclear who in the Department had the lead,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649f29e98f08d551cf54b59a#block-649f29e98f08d551cf54b59a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the report}}}\uc2  determined.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors said they have turned over more than 80,000 pages of materials to congressman George Santos\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers in the federal fraud and money laundering case against him. Santos {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649f19428f08d64edbf1950c#block-649f19428f08d64edbf1950c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pleaded not guilty }}}\uc2  last month to charges that he duped donors, stole from his campaign, collected fraudulent unemployment benefits and lied to Congress about being a millionaire. The New York Republican, known for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/21/george-santos-piers-morgan-terrible-liar"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fabricating}}}\uc2  key parts his life story, is free {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/22/george-santos-bail-person-to-be-revealed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on bond}}}\uc2 , awaiting trial.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Much of North Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s 12-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban will take effect tomorrow, after a federal judge temporarily blocked just one provision included in the law.\par \par \uc2 US district court judge Catherine Eagles issued a narrow temporary restraining order putting on hold a requirement for doctors to document the location of a pregnancy when prescribing a medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bulk of the law will be allowed to take effect on 1 July.\par \par \uc2 In May, the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican-controlled general assembly successfully overrode the Democratic governor\u8217\'20\'19s veto of the 12-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. The move dealt a blow to one of the last bastions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the south, which has been significantly curtailed after the supreme court overturned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 In his speech at the White House, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he would use the Higher Education Act of 1965 to allow the education department to release and waive loans.\par \par \uc2 The education department filed a notice to begin the regulatory process of using the 1965 Act to cancel student debt, which does not require relying on a national emergency.\par \par \uc2 Politico\u8217\'20\'19s Michael Stratford writes that the regulatory notice was only finalized by the department last night.\par \par \uc2 enltr?? The regulatory notice for Biden's Higher Ed Act fallback plan for student debt relief was finalized by Ed Dept last night per the PDF metadata. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/AZxsHa8hL7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/AZxsHa8hL7}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Michael Stratford (@mstratford) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mstratford/status/1674885753816379392?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 The New Republic\u8217\'20\'19s Grace Segers writes that the Biden administration appears to have been more prepared for the court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling than during the Dobbs ruling last year.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe Biden administration appears to be more prepared for this ruling in contrast to their immediate reaction to the Dobbs decision, which despite also being expected, almost seemed to catch them flat-footed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/W9Ybq4gyaA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/W9Ybq4gyaA}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Grace Segers (@Grace_Segers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Grace_Segers/status/1674881041364926467?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Speaking from the White House briefing room, \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2 , the secretary of education, said he \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly disagreed\u8221\'20\'1D with the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision and vowed to \u8220\'20\'1Copen up an alternative path to debt relief for as many borrowers as possible, as quickly as possible\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In ruling against the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s landmark student debt forgiveness plan, the court had \u8220\'20\'1Cruled against more than 40 million working families\u8221\'20\'1D, Cardona said.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re not talking about the millionaires who benefited from the billions in tax giveaway a few years ago. We\u8217\'20\'19re talking about low and middle income families recovering from the worst pandemic in a century.\par \par \uc2 He said it was \u8220\'20\'1Coutrageous\u8221\'20\'1D that Republican members of Congress had \u8220\'20\'1Cfought so hard against the program that would have helped millions of their own constituents\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Cardona added:\par \par \uc2 Today, I want to assure our students, our borrowers and families across America \u8211\'20\'13 our fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 Miguel Cardona\par \par \uc2 Education Secretary \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  speaks during the daily briefing at the White House. Photograph: Susan Walsh/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Court's ruling against Colorado law protecting LGBTQ+ rights 'threatens future progress', says Harris\par \par \uc2 Vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has spoken out against the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling today {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/us-supreme-court-ruling-lgbtq-rights-colorado"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 striking down}}}\uc2  a Colorado civil rights law which compels businesses and organizations to treat same-sex couples equally.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision \u8220\'20\'1Cdeparts from decades of jurisprudence by creating an exception to protections against discrimination in public accommodations\u8221\'20\'1D, a statement from Harris reads.\par \par \uc2 On the last day of Pride Month, the Supreme Court has paved the way for businesses across our nation to discriminate in the name of \u8220\'20\'1Cfree expression\u8221\'20\'1D\u8212\'20\'14against the LGBTQI+ community, racial and religious minorities, the disability community, and women.\par \par \uc2 At a time when we celebrate hard-won advancements in LGBTQI+ rights, this decision threatens future progress.\par \par \uc2 She added that she and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinue to rigorously enforce federal anti-discrimination protections and fight for the right of all people to participate equally in our society\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 We have a clip from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s speech where he announced a \u8220\'20\'1Cnew path\u8221\'20\'1D on student loan relief that will rely on a different law than the one that the supreme court today said his administration could not use to relieve some $430bn in federal student debt.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Once a person loses their right to vote in Mississippi it is essentially impossible to get it back.\par \par \uc2 To do so, a disenfranchised person must get the legislature to approve an individualized bill on their behalf by a supermajority in both chambers and then have the governor approve the bill. There are no online instructions or applications and lawmakers can reject or deny an application for any reason.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bfeldman89.com/projects/suffrage-bills/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hardly anyone}}}\uc2  successfully makes it through the process. Between 1997 and 2022, an average of seven people successfully made it through the process each year, according to Blake Feldman, a criminal justice researcher in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court did not say on Friday why {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/us-supreme-court-mississippi-voting-rights-case-black-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it was rejecting the case}}}\uc2  (it takes four votes on the court to grant review) and Justices \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  were the only two justices who noted their dissent from the denial. Jackson wrote an opinion saying the fifth circuit had committed \u8220\'20\'1Ctwo egregious analytical errors that ought to be corrected\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 First, she wrote, even though Mississippi voters removed a crime in 1950 and added two more in 1968, the substance of many of the original crimes from 1890 remained intact. That means that the list is still discriminatory, she wrote in a dissent that was joined by Sotomayor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \u8220\'20\'1Cremaining crimes\u8221\'20\'1D from [the list of crimes] pernicious origin still work the very harm the 1890 Convention intended \u8211\'20\'13 denying Black Mississippians the vote,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/063023zor_b07d.pdf#page=24"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 turned away}}}\uc2  a case on Friday challenging {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/08/us-1890-law-black-americans-voting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s rules}}}\uc2  around voting rights for people with felony convictions, leaving intact a policy implemented more than a century ago with the explicit goal of preventing Black people from voting.\par \par \uc2 Those convicted of any {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msvotes.org/policy-and-research/rights-restoration/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of 23 specific felonies}}}\uc2  in Mississippi permanently lose the right to vote. The list is rooted in the state\u8217\'20\'19s 1890 constitutional convention, where delegates chose disenfranchising crimes that they believed Black people were more likely to commit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe came here to exclude the negro. Nothing short of this will answer,\u8221\'20\'1D the president of the convention said at the time. The crimes, which include bribery, theft, carjacking, bigamy and timber larceny, have remained largely the same since then; Mississippi voters amended it remove burglary in 1950 and added murder and rape in 1968.\par \par \uc2 It continued to have a staggering effect in Mississippi. Sixteen percent of the Black voting-age population remains blocked from casting a ballot, as well as 10% of the overall voting age population, according to an estimate by The Sentencing Project, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sentencingproject.org/reports/locked-out-2022-estimates-of-people-denied-voting-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a criminal justice non-profit}}}\uc2 . The state is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/MS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 38% Black}}}\uc2 , but Black people make up more than half of Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s disenfranchised population.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/us-supreme-court-mississippi-voting-rights-case-black-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Read the full story by my colleague Sam Levine here.}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/us-supreme-court-mississippi-voting-rights-case-black-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme court leaves intact Mississippi law disenfranchising Black voters}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 In his speech at the White House, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  repeated his criticism of the Republicans who led the successful effort to block his plan to cancel some federal student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Biden called out those Republican members who received \u8220\'20\'1Chundreds of thousands for themselves\u8221\'20\'1D in Paycheck Protection Program (PPP) loans that were later forgiven, but who had strongly opposed his student debt plan.\par \par \uc2 The hypocrisy is stunning.\par \par \uc2 enltr\u8220\'20\'1CThe hypocrisy is stunning.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden slams members of Congress who opposed student loan debt forgiveness but had their PPP loans forgiven {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RdMb486gVz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/RdMb486gVz}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1674875743677947904?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 The new student debt relief plan will be implemented under the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s rulemaking process, the White House said, and it seems like it will take months to get the program up and running.\par \par \uc2 The education department today issued a notice announcing the plan, will hold a virtual public hearing on 18 July and \u8220\'20\'1Cfinalize the issues to be addressed through rulemaking and begin the negotiated rulemaking sessions this fall. The Department will complete this rulemaking as quickly as possible,\u8221\'20\'1D according to the White House.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the White House said the education department will institute \u8220\'20\'1Ca 12-month \u8216\'20\'18on-ramp\u8217\'20\'19 to repayment, running from 1 October 2023 to 30 September 2024, so that financially vulnerable borrowers who miss monthly payments during this period are not considered delinquent, reported to credit bureaus, placed in default, or referred to debt collection agencies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Federal student loan payments have been paused since Covid-19 broke out in March 2020, and were set to restart this October. The Biden administration said the \u8220\'20\'1Con-ramp\u8221\'20\'1D is intended to provide relief to financially struggling borrowers who can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to start making payments again right away.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/leonie-chao-fong"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 L\u233\'00\'E9onie Chao-Fong}}}\uc2  is taking the blog over now to keep you posted on this developing story.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Court 'misinterpreted constitution' - Biden\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  directed blame for the apparent demise of his student debt relief program both at the Republicans who sued over the plan, and at the supreme court justices who ruled against it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the court misinterpreted the constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. Asked whether he gave Americans \u8220\'20\'1Cfalse hope\u8221\'20\'1D by promising $430bn in total debt relief only for it to be blocked in court, he replied, \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t give any false hope. The question was whether or not I would do even more than was requested. What I did I felt was appropriate and was able to be done and would get done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut the Republican snatched away the hope that they were given,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis new path is legally sound,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in announcing his new attempt at student loan relief.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to take longer, but in my view it\u8217\'20\'19s the best path that remains to providing for as many borrowers as possible. I\u8217\'20\'19m directing my team to move as quickly as possible on law,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden announces 'new path' on student debt relief\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he will announce a \u8220\'20\'1Cnew path\u8221\'20\'1D on student loan relief that will rely on a different law than the one that the supreme court today his administration could not use to relieve some $430bn in federal student debt.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m announcing today a new path consistent with today\u8217\'20\'19s ruling to provide student debt relief to as many borrowers as possible as quickly as possible. We will ground this new approach in a different law than my original plan, the so-called Higher Education Act. That will allow (education secretary \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  ) \u8230\'20\'26 to compromise, waive, or release loans under certain circumstances,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has started his speech by criticizing the Republicans who successfully sued to block his student loan forgiveness program.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe money was literally about to go out the door, and then Republican elected officials and special interests stepped in. They said no, no, literally snatching from the hands of millions of Americans thousands of dollars of student debt relief that was about to change their lives,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou know, these Republican officials just couldn\u8217\'20\'19t bear the thought of providing relief for working-class, middle-class Americans. Republican state officials sued my administration, attempting to block relief, including to millions of their own constituents.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 After a delay of 30 minutes, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is finally speaking about the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s block of his student debt relief program.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s listen in.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lorie Smith is the web designer whose refusal to create a website for a same-sex wedding that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/29/supreme-court-lgbtq-document-veracity-colorado"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 may or may not have actually existed}}}\uc2  led to the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives today striking down a Colorado law intended to prevent discrimination against the LGBTQ+ community.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Fox News, Smith, whose case {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/30/christian-hate-group-funding-us-anti-lgbtq-anti-abortion-organizations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was championed by the Christian legal non-profit Alliance Defending Freedom}}}\uc2 , cheered the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in the case:\par \par \uc2 enltr\u8220\'20\'1CI am incredibly grateful for the court's decision that affirms that the government cannot force anyone to say something they don't believe.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Christian web designer Lorie Smith praises SCOTUS ruling in her favor after she refused to work on same-sex weddings {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ja1fHlvS4i"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ja1fHlvS4i}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1674866520663416848?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden set to address supreme court's ruling against student debt relief program\par \par \uc2 In a speech at the White House scheduled to start soon, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is expected to announce his next moves after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/student-loan-forgiveness-supreme-court-ruling-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stopped him}}}\uc2  from canceling $430bn in federal student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Follow this blog as we cover the speech live.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Government report faults state department's handling of Afghanistan withdrawal\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the Friday afternoon before the Independence Day holiday weekend, and the state department just happened to choose this moment to release a report critical of its handling of the chaotic US withdrawal from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s diplomatic arm \u8220\'20\'1Cfailed to establish a broader task force as the situation in Afghanistan deteriorated\u8221\'20\'1D, and \u8220\'20\'1Cit was unclear who in the Department had the lead,\u8221\'20\'1D the report determined, according to the Associated Press. The document was completed in March 2022, but the Biden administration only decided to release it now, though many parts of it remain withheld from the public.\par \par \uc2 Fortunately for the people of the United States and Afghanistan alike, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/afghanistan-state-department-withdrawal-c4004657b7390a952d2a74c74fd720f2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AP has it covered extensively}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The State Department failed to do enough planning before the collapse of the U.S.-backed government in Afghanistan, according to a Biden administration review of the department\u8217\'20\'19s performance during the chaotic evacuation of Americans and Afghan allies.\par \par \uc2 The review repeatedly blames the administrations of both former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for their efforts before and after the August 2021 departure of U.S. forces from Kabul. The U.S. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/afghanistan-immigration-travel-lifestyle-kim-kardashian-west-346e0959989079e5fba109247967573c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 evacuated}}}\uc2  an estimated 124,000 Afghans from the country.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have in turn accused Biden of not taking responsibility for intelligence failures leading up to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s seizure of the country and for the scenes of chaos at Kabul\u8217\'20\'19s airport, where 13 Marines died in a suicide bombing.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration released sections of the long-awaited State report, which was completed in March 2022, on the Friday before the July 4 holiday weekend, though it withheld most of the report from public release. It {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/joe-biden-afghanistan-withdrawal-congress-war-5ff87c14ffd4f7daaa6675e52d3bba1c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had released}}}\uc2  a National Security Council review of the withdrawal on the day before Good Friday and the Easter weekend but declined to issue internal Pentagon and State Department assessments.\par \par \uc2 A State Department task force helped bring out nearly 2,000 Afghan citizens in July and early August 2021, weeks before the Aug. 31, 2021, deadline the U.S. set for withdrawal. They were eligible for processing under a special U.S. visa program for Afghans.\par \par \uc2 But State \u8220\'20\'1Cfailed to establish a broader task force as the situation in Afghanistan deteriorated,\u8221\'20\'1D the report says.\par \par \uc2 And as the military planned for an evacuation of American civilians and Afghan allies, \u8220\'20\'1Cit was unclear who in the Department had the lead,\u8221\'20\'1D it says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe decisions of both President Trump and President Biden to end the U.S. military mission in Afghanistan had serious consequences for the viability of the Afghan government and its security,\u8221\'20\'1D the report says. \u8220\'20\'1CThose decisions are beyond the scope of this review, but the (review) team found that during both administrations there was insufficient senior-level consideration of worst-case scenarios and how quickly those might follow.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 After a rough week for Democratic policies at the supreme court, this seven-year-old post from Hillary Clinton is making the rounds on progressive \uc2 Twitter\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 enltrA Republican president could stack the Supreme Court with justices who will rip away all of the progress we\u8217\'20\'19ve made. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/peZv2sXdQO"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/peZv2sXdQO}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  (@HillaryClinton) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HillaryClinton/status/687050404971069442?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 12, 2016}}}\par \par \uc2 The three supreme court justices \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 all supported the court\u8217\'20\'19s decisions this week overturning affirmative action and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student debt forgiveness program, as well as curbing laws that protect LGBTQ+ people from discrimination.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Students protest outside the supreme court after its conservative justices struck down \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s debt relief program today. Photograph: Allison Bailey/NurPhoto/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 Several dozen demonstrators gathered outside the supreme court on Friday following its decision to strike down the Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration\u8217\'20\'19s student loan forgiveness plan and block millions of borrowers from receiving debt relief.\par \par \uc2 Tori Wanzer of Maryland was visibly disappointed by the conservative justices\u8217\'20\'19 decision as she stood outside the court with a group representing the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2  (\uc2 AFT\uc2 ). \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s absolutely ridiculous,\u8221\'20\'1D she told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CThis ruling negatively impacts so many different groups of people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wanzer questioned the court\u8217\'20\'19s motivation \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t seem like it\u8217\'20\'19s for the greater good\u8221\'20\'1D. Comparing the decision with Florida governor and Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/education/2023/feb/13/ap-african-american-studies-florida-ron-desantis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejection of an Advanced Placement class on African American studies}}}\uc2 , she said Friday\u8217\'20\'19s ruling felt like it was \u8220\'20\'1Cjust one thing after another\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s 2023 and we\u8217\'20\'19ve gotten more progressive, with certain movements and groups of people who can be loud and proud with their rights. [The court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling] is a way to stop that. It\u8217\'20\'19s a way for them to take control over who gets assistance and acceptance into certain institutions and who doesn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8211\'20\'13 who\u8217\'20\'19s forced to suffer and who is helped to greatness,\u8221\'20\'1D Wanzer said.\par \par \uc2 Bryanna Mitchell, 24, from Arizona, said she was \u8220\'20\'1Cshocked\u8217\'20\'19 when she heard the court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling today, and that she decided to attend the protest on behalf of the \uc2 AFT\uc2  because it was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery necessary to show up for all the people who have student debt\u8221\'20\'1D and to call on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cfollow through with what he promised us\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Shanna Hayes of New York described the \u8220\'20\'1Cshame\u8221\'20\'1D she used to feel about carrying her student loan. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just me,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hayes, 34, who is an economic justice fellow at the Student Debt Crisis Center (SDCC). \u8220\'20\'1CMany of us have heard the rhetoric \u8211\'20\'13 that I\u8217\'20\'19ve made poor decisions, and that I need to pay my bills\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut it\u8217\'20\'19s not just people who are in their twenties and thirties. It\u8217\'20\'19s people who are grandparents who are struggling. It\u8217\'20\'19s people who can\u8217\'20\'19t retire because all their retirement funds are going to be taken by the government because they\u8217\'20\'19ve defaulted on their student loans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 SDCC managing director Sabrina Calazans said the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling felt like a \u8220\'20\'1Cgut punch\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re just heartbroken by this decision\u8221\'20\'1D, the 26-year-old said.\par \par \uc2 Calazans said she regularly spoke with people about how they were being personally impacted by student debt. \u8220\'20\'1CWe hear from people who can\u8217\'20\'19t retire, who can\u8217\'20\'19t get married, who can\u8217\'20\'19t start a family, and who can\u8217\'20\'19t invest in their futures\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to do something about it. It\u8217\'20\'19s poor, working-class folks, middle-class folks, Black and brown Americans who are disproportionately impacted by student debt.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Progressives demand Biden invoke 'Plan B' to cancel student debt\par \par \uc2 The supreme court has the kibosh on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s plan to cancel some federal student debt, but progressives insist the president still has options.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday, I am urging the Biden administration to implement a Plan B immediately to cancel student debt for tens of millions of Americans who are struggling to pay the rent, put food on the table, and pay for the basic necessities of life,\u8221\'20\'1D said independent senator Bernie Sanders, a longtime proponent of student debt cancellation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDespite this legally unsound supreme court decision, the president has the clear authority under the Higher Education Act of 1965 to cancel student debt. He must use this authority immediately. If Republicans could provide trillions of dollars in tax breaks to the top one percent and profitable corporations, if they could cancel hundreds of billions in loans for wealthy business owners during the pandemic when Trump was president and if they could vote to spend $886bn on the Pentagon, please don\u8217\'20\'19t tell me that we cannot afford to cancel student debt for working families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 His call was echoed by Democratic congressman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , who agreed Biden has the legal authority to relieve debt:\par \par \uc2 enltrIt is very important to note this \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  ruling does NOT remove Biden\u8217\'20\'19s ability to pursue student loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 The Biden Admin can use the HEA (Higher Ed Act) - our position from the start - to continue loan forgiveness before payments resume. They should do so ASAP. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/inV3yWsDwB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/inV3yWsDwB}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  (@AOC) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AOC/status/1674801593336864768?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrJust last year, DOE forgave $6 BILLION for defrauded students. Trump completely wiped out debt for disabled veterans. Neither program was challenged nor overturned.\par \par \uc2 We still have the power to cancel and must use it, or we\u8217\'20\'19re looking at an economic crisis for millions of people.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  (@AOC) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AOC/status/1674802644219752448?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors said today that they have turned over more than 80,000 pages of materials to congressman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/george-santos"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 George Santos}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s lawyers in the federal fraud and money laundering {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/10/george-santos-court-federal-charges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 case against him}}}\uc2 , the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 The documents weren\u8217\'20\'19t publicly released, as is common during this stage of a case. The barely five-minute hearing at a Long Island courthouse focused on the case schedule, with the next court date set for 7 September.\par \par \uc2 Santos didn\u8217\'20\'19t speak in court, nor ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/10/george-santos-court-federal-charges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unlike last time}}}\uc2  ) to journalists waiting outside. The New York Republican, known for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/21/george-santos-piers-morgan-terrible-liar"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fabricating}}}\uc2  key parts his life story, is free {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/22/george-santos-bail-person-to-be-revealed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on bond}}}\uc2 , awaiting trial.\par \par \uc2 Santos pleaded not guilty last month to charges that he duped donors, stole from his campaign, collected fraudulent unemployment benefits and lied to Congress about being a millionaire.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors have charged Santos with 13 counts of wire fraud, money laundering, theft of public funds and making false statements to Congress. He faces up to 20 years in prison if convicted.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile ABC reports that someone who did speak outside the court was the military veteran {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/18/george-santos-republicans-committee-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Richard Osthoff}}}\uc2 , who has accused Santos of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/18/george-santos-republicans-committee-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filching money}}}\uc2  raised to save his dog.\par \par \uc2 enltrNavy vet Osthoff who accused Santos of stealing money raised for his dying dog shouted \u8220\'20\'1Cyou killed my dog!\u8221\'20\'1D at the Congressman as he left court.\par \par \uc2 He told ABC News he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want people to forget \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat a piece of work he is\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/YJzMnWthh3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/YJzMnWthh3}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/i0tJDndAFh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/i0tJDndAFh}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Will Steakin (@wsteaks) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/wsteaks/status/1674838610615447554?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 The progressive congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  of California argued the Biden administration still has options to offer relief to student borrowers, even after the supreme court struck down the president\u8217\'20\'19s debt forgiveness program.\par \par \uc2 As of now, student loan payments are set to resume in September, after a three-year pause precipitated by the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve got to figure out how to at least pause the student loan repayment in September, until we make good on our promise to forgive student loans,\u8221\'20\'1D Khanna said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t forsake young people who are relying on the president\u8217\'20\'19s policy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Khanna went on to say that Biden should consider invoking the Higher Education Act of 1965 to cancel some student debt. The Biden administration relied on the Heroes Act of 2003 to defend its debt cancelation program, but the court rejected the legal basis for that argument.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether the White House seems open to those proposals, Khanna replied, \u8220\'20\'1CThey have engaged in a constructive conversation with those of us in the progressive caucus, but we\u8217\'20\'19ve made it clear: there is no other option. The students who have been promised this forgiveness cannot be repaying student loans in September. That would be a punch to the gut of millions of young Americans across this country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, demonstrators have gathered in front of the supreme court building in Washington to protest the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision. The Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/leonie-chao-fong"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 L\u233\'00\'E9onie Chao-Fong}}}\uc2  is there and will send a dispatch.\par \par \uc2 enltrProtest outside the Supreme Court following the court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling against the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s student debt forgiveness plan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/NOdl1ytZyT"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/NOdl1ytZyT}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 L\u233\'00\'E9onie Chao-Fong (@leonie_chaofong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/leonie_chaofong/status/1674821017150128129?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 For the second time in two days, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will make remarks at the White House in response to decisions by the conservative-controlled \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The US president is due to step up to the mike at the White House at 3.30pm ET and make comments on this morning\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/student-loan-forgiveness-supreme-court-ruling-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court ruling against}}}\uc2  the legality of his $430bn program to offer student debt relief to up to 40 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 The White House will speak in the Roosevelt Room. He is expected to talk about options for taking alternative action to relief the loan burden.\par \par \uc2 These are likely to be brief remarks as, scheduled for 3.45pm is the press briefing by the White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , who will be accompanied by the secretary of education, \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2 , and the deputy director of the National Economic Council, \uc2 Bharat Ramamurti\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, speaking from the White House to slam the court\u8217\'20\'19s striking down of affirmative action in US higher education, Biden answered one question called out as he left, following brief remarks. In response to being asked if the justices now represented a \u8220\'20\'1Crogue court\u8221\'20\'1D he responded: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not a normal court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  prepares to leave after speaking on the supreme court ruling on affirmative action in college admissions in the Roosevelt Room of the White House, 29 June. Photograph: Evan Vucci/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Progressive congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  of California denounced the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to scrap \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s student debt forgiveness plan, in an interview with the Guardian this morning.\par \par \uc2 Khanna pointed to the decision as another example of how the \u8220\'20\'1Cout of touch\u8221\'20\'1D court is worsening Americans\u8217\'20\'19 everyday lives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a court that is regressive, that is rolling back women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, rolling back rights about racial equality, rolling back environmental protections, rolling back now assistance for young people,\u8221\'20\'1D Khanna said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it\u8217\'20\'19s exactly why we need term limits on these supreme court justices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Khanna has previously demanded term limits for the justices, who serve lifelong terms, but he said the most recent series of hard-right decisions from the court underscore the urgent need to enact the policy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany of these people couldn\u8217\'20\'19t win elections for dogcatcher,\u8221\'20\'1D Khanna said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why we need term limits.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 US Representative \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  (D-CA) arrives for an official State Dinner in honor of India's Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2 , at the White House in Washington, last week. Photograph: Stefani Reynolds/AFP/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 The day so far\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649ee8b18f08ff8b60151343#block-649ee8b18f08ff8b60151343"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked}}}\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s plan to relieve some federal student loan debt from borrowers, and also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649ee0478f08ff8b601512a5#block-649ee0478f08ff8b601512a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled against}}}\uc2  a Colorado law the protected LGBTQ+ people from discrimination by businesses. Democrats have decried the twin rulings, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649ef2d48f08ff8b60151417#block-649ef2d48f08ff8b60151417"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 saying}}}\uc2  they are further proof that the court has been captured by rightwing Republicans. While many in the GOP are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649eee828f08d64edbf1925c#block-649eee828f08d64edbf1925c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hailing}}}\uc2  the apparent end of the president\u8217\'20\'19s plan to cancel hundreds of billions of dollars in federal student loans, they are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649efc368f08d551cf54b396#block-649efc368f08d551cf54b396"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comparatively quiet}}}\uc2  about the blow struck by the six conservative justices against LGBTQ+ protections. As for Biden, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649f07fc8f08ff8b60151583#block-649f07fc8f08ff8b60151583"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 we\u8217\'20\'19ll hear more from him}}}\uc2  at a to-be-announced time this afternoon, where he says he will speak about the ruling against the loan program.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else has happened so far today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-649f011a8f08ff8b60151511#block-649f011a8f08ff8b60151511"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed}}}\uc2  to next term take up a case that could give the conservatives a chance to further erode gun control laws.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Indiana\u8217\'20\'19s highest court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649efa398f08ff8b60151489#block-649efa398f08ff8b60151489"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has allowed}}}\uc2  its near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to go into effect, a year after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling overturning Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Together with its decision yesterday ending affirmative action, the just-concluded term {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/30/supreme-court-rulings-latest-student-debt-relief-lgbtq-rights-news-biden-trump?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-649eeb6b8f08ff8b60151381#block-649eeb6b8f08ff8b60151381"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was another}}}\uc2  where the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives flexed their muscles.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden says Republican 'hypocrisy' is 'stunning' after debt relief struck down\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is accusing the Republicans who led the successful effort to block his plan to cancel some federal student loan debt of \u8220\'20\'1Cstunning\u8221\'20\'1D hypocrisy, and vowed to keep fighting to help more Americans afford college degrees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe hypocrisy of Republican elected officials is stunning,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said in a newly released statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey had no problem with billions in pandemic-related loans to businesses \u8211\'20\'13 including hundreds of thousands and in some cases millions of dollars for their own businesses. And those loans were forgiven. But when it came to providing relief to millions of hard-working Americans, they did everything in their power to stop it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI believe that the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to strike down our student debt relief plan is wrong. But I will stop at nothing to find other ways to deliver relief to hard-working middle-class families. My administration will continue to work to bring the promise of higher education to every American,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said.\par \par \uc2 Promising \u8220\'20\'1Cthe fight is not over,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said: \u8220\'20\'1CLater today, I will provide more detail on all that my administration has done to help students and the next steps my administration will take.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The president\u8217\'20\'19s full statement is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/06/30/statement-from-president-joe-biden-on-supreme-court-decision-on-student-loan-debt-relief/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Student loan decision 'unthinkable', Biden says, plans afternoon address\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has called the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling against his student loan cancellation plan \u8220\'20\'1Cunthinkable\u8221\'20\'1D, and announced he will speak on the issue this afternoon:\par \par \uc2 enltrUnthinkable.\par \par \uc2 This fight isn\u8217\'20\'19t over. I\u8217\'20\'19ll have more to announce when I address the nation this afternoon. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wGBuwBySB7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/wGBuwBySB7}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1674820972224958468?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court conservatives could further erode gun control in next term\par \par \uc2 After releasing the final decisions of its term today, the supreme court also announced some of the cases it would consider in its next term, including one with significant implications for gun control nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The court will consider whether a federal law that takes guns away from people who are under a domestic violence restraining order is constitutional. While they are always capable of surprises, recall that last year the six-justice conservative majority {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/23/us-supreme-court-new-york-law-gun-control"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 handed down a decision in New York State Rifle & Pistol Association v Bruen}}}\uc2  that greatly expanded the ability of Americans to carry concealed weapons.\par \par \uc2 As Eric Ruben, an assistant professor at \uc2 Southern Methodist University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s law school writes, the new case, United States v Rahimi, gives the conservatives an opportunity to strike another blow against gun control:\par \par \uc2 enltr??BREAKING: \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  will decide whether disarming people subject to a domestic violence restraining order (DVRO) is constitutional under the Second Amendment. This is a MAJOR stress test of the unprecedented historical-analogical standard created in Bruen. ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/GoQ2DNePF3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/GoQ2DNePF3}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Eric Ruben (@ericmruben) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ericmruben/status/1674810476742492160?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe case is United States v. Rahimi, an appeal from the Fifth Cir., which struck down the federal law prohibiting those subject to a DVRO from possessing a gun, declaring it an \u8220\'20\'1Coutlier[] that our ancestors would never have accepted.\u8221\'20\'1D Every court prior to Bruen had upheld it. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/MutyEhpi8H"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/MutyEhpi8H}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Eric Ruben (@ericmruben) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ericmruben/status/1674810478881636352?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrWhy wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t our ancestors have \u8220\'20\'1Caccepted\u8221\'20\'1D DVROs? Partly bc the the Founding gen undervalued women\u8217\'20\'19s lives. Rather than focus on protecting women in abusive relationships, historically the law protected the husband\u8217\'20\'19s prerogative of marital chastisement. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/u3gSSrCWfZ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/u3gSSrCWfZ}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Eric Ruben (@ericmruben) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ericmruben/status/1674810481239027714?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden 'deeply concerned' court's decision could fuel LGTBQ+ discrimination\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has released a statement expressing his fears that the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives may fuel discrimination against gay, lesbian and transgender Americans nationwide by striking down a Colorado law prohibiting businesses from turning away LGBTQ+ people.\par \par \uc2 The president said:\par \par \uc2 In America, no person should face discrimination simply because of who they are or who they love. The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s disappointing decision in 303 Creative LLC v Elenis undermines that basic truth, and painfully it comes during Pride month when millions of Americans across the country join together to celebrate the contributions, resilience, and strength of the LGBTQI+ community. While the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision only addresses expressive original designs, I\u8217\'20\'19m deeply concerned that the decision could invite more discrimination against LGBTQI+ Americans. More broadly, today\u8217\'20\'19s decision weakens long-standing laws that protect all Americans against discrimination in public accommodations \u8211\'20\'13 including people of color, people with disabilities, people of faith, and women.\par \par \uc2 Biden vowed that his administration \u8220\'20\'1Cwill also work with states across the country to fight back against attempts to roll back civil rights protections that could follow this ruling.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 You can read the full statement {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/06/30/statement-from-president-joe-biden-on-supreme-court-decision-in-303-creative-llc-v-elenis/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Top House Democrat condemns decision against LGBTQ+ protection, GOP keeps quiet\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a tough day for Democrats and their policies at the supreme court, and they\u8217\'20\'19re letting their voters know it, with the party\u8217\'20\'19s leaders and rank-and-file lawmakers alike speaking out against the justices\u8217\'20\'19 decisions curbing LGBTQ+ protections, and stopping \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from relieving some student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 But as CNN points out, while plenty of Republicans are cheering the student loan decisions, but few have anything to say about the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision against a Colorado law intended to stop businesses from discriminating against gay people:\par \par \uc2 enltrOne interesting thing this morning: TONS of Republicans have been quick to weigh in on student loan decision. Fewer (including key GOP leaders) are weighing in on case involving gay rights, a sign of how much the politics surrounding the issue have changed in the last decade.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Lauren Fox (@FoxReports) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FoxReports/status/1674805796045979650?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Democrats have no such qualms. In a statement, House minority leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  described the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in the Colorado case as a step backwards:\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s extreme, rightwing supreme court ruling invites a return to a time when businesses were empowered to turn people away simply because of their sexual orientation or gender identity. The overwhelming majority of Americans believe that when a business opens its doors, those doors should be open to all. Unfortunately, the extremists on the supreme court are more interested in jamming their rightwing ideology down the throats of the American people than promoting fundamental fairness.\par \par \uc2 No person or business should be given a license to discriminate. That is exactly what today\u8217\'20\'19s wrongheaded supreme court decision will unleash. Every individual, no matter who they are or who they love, deserves the freedom to go about their daily lives and access the goods and services they need without fear or discrimination. House Democrats will continue to stand with the LGBTQ+ community until liberty and justice for all prevails and the Equality Act is the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Indiana court allows state's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to go into effect\par \par \uc2 A year after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade and allowed states to completely prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Indiana\u8217\'20\'19s highest court has allowed its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to go into effect, Indiana Public Broadcasting reports:\par \par \uc2 enltrI've deleted my previous tweet, as I have more up-to-date info on when the ban takes effect.\par \par \uc2 The Indiana Supreme Court rules that the state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban does NOT violate the Indiana Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The ban is now set to take effect within the next month or so. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/8TXiTaA0y9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/8TXiTaA0y9}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Brandon J. Smith (@brandonjsmith5) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/brandonjsmith5/status/1674808625775226881?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Indiana was the first state in the nation to pass an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after the end of Roe v Wade, but its supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/12/indiana-supreme-court-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked the measure}}}\uc2  passed by the Republican-dominated legislature later in 2022.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 As the Senate\u8217\'20\'19s top Republican, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has played a major role in creating the court\u8217\'20\'19s current conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 He famously stopped Barack Obama from filing a vacancy on the court with one of his nominees, then presided over the confirmation of three of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s justices \u8211\'20\'13 all of whom gave their support to the court\u8217\'20\'19s decisions overturning Roe v Wade, affirmative action and, today, blocking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student debt relief plan.\par \par \uc2 McConnell is of course happy with that most recent decision. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what he has to say:\par \par \uc2 The American people know that the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s student loan socialism plan would be a raw deal for hardworking taxpayers. Now that the supreme court has rejected the administration\u8217\'20\'19s position in Biden v Nebraska, they can know that it\u8217\'20\'19s illegal.\par \par \uc2 The president of the United States cannot hijack 20-year-old emergency powers to pad the pockets of his high-earning base and make suckers out of working families who choose not to take on student debt. The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision today deals a heavy blow to Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 distorted and outsized view of executive power.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Democratic senator Elizabeth Warren is a longtime advocate for cancelling student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what she had to say about the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s attempt to partially do so:\par \par \uc2 enltrThe same Supreme Court that overturned Roe now refuses to follow the plain language of the law on student loan cancellation. This fight is not over. The President has more tools to cancel student debt \u8212\'20\'14 and he must use them.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Elizabeth Warren (@SenWarren) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenWarren/status/1674792587864199174?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrMore than 40 million hard working Americans are waiting for the help that President Biden promised them, and they expect this administration to throw everything they\u8217\'20\'19ve got into the fight until they make good on this commitment.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Elizabeth Warren (@SenWarren) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenWarren/status/1674792619787063296?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s proposal would have canceled some \u8211\'20\'13 but not all \u8211\'20\'13 of the federal debt many Americans have taken out to funder higher education. The supreme court has now put a stop to it. Here\u8217\'20\'19s more about what that means, from the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/erum-salam"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Erum Salam}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The supreme court has ruled against the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s student {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2022/aug/24/student-loan-forgiveness-who-qualifies-pell-amount"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 debt forgiveness plan}}}\uc2  in a blow to up to 40 million borrowers in the US.\par \par \uc2 The case, Biden v Nebraska, against the plan was originally {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2023/feb/28/us-student-loan-debt-us-supreme-court-hears-arguments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought by}}}\uc2  a coalition of six Republican-led states \u8211\'20\'13 Arkansas, Iowa, Kansas, Missouri, Nebraska and South Carolina \u8211\'20\'13 arguing that Congress should have approved any student loan debt cancellation plan and that the Biden administration and the US education department are abusing emergency authority.\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s loan forgiveness plan, announced last August, $20,000 per person of federal student debt would have been forgiven for borrowers who were Pell grant recipients and up to $10,000 of debt would have been forgiven for other borrowers.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/student-loan-forgiveness-supreme-court-ruling-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court rules against Biden\u8217\'20\'19s student loan relief program}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden plans 'new actions' to protect student loan borrowers\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will today announce \u8220\'20\'1Cnew actions\u8221\'20\'1D to protect people who take out student loans, Reuters reports, citing a White House source.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we strongly disagree with the court, we prepared for this scenario,\u8221\'20\'1D the source said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe president will make clear he\u8217\'20\'19s not done fighting yet, and will announce new actions to protect student loan borrowers \u8230\'20\'26 The president will make clear he\u8217\'20\'19s not done fighting yet, and will announce new actions to protect student loan borrowers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ruling against debt relief 'cruel', Senate's Democratic leader says, vowing, 'fight will not end here'\par \par \uc2 The Senate\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is out with a statement both condemning the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student debt relief program and claiming that the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative justices are, essentially, in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s pocket:\par \par \uc2 This disappointing and cruel ruling shows the callousness of the Maga Republican-controlled supreme court. The hypocrisy is clear: as justices accept lavish, six-figure gifts, they don\u8217\'20\'19t dare to help Americans saddled with student loan debt, instead siding with the powerful, big-monied interests.\par \par \uc2 The fight will not end here. The Biden administration has remaining legal routes to provide broad-based student debt cancellation. With the pause on student loan payments set to expire in weeks, I call upon the administration to do everything in its power to deliver for millions of working- and middle-class Americans struggling with student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . Photograph: Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Luis Maga\u241\'00\'F1a/AP\par \par \uc2 Public opinions polls {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/4732/supreme-court.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have shown}}}\uc2  the supreme court is increasingly disliked by Americans, and chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  seems to know it.\par \par \uc2 At the conclusion of his majority opinion in Biden v Nebraska, the case in which Roberts and the court\u8217\'20\'19s five other conservative justices ruled against the president\u8217\'20\'19s program to relieve some federal student loan debt, he inserts a plea for understanding.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt has become a disturbing feature of some recent opinions to criticize the decisions with which they disagree as going beyond the proper role of the judiciary,\u8221\'20\'1D writes Roberts, who was appointed to his post by Republican president \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  in 2005.\par \par \uc2 He continues:\par \par \uc2 Reasonable minds may disagree with our analysis \u8211\'20\'13 in fact, at least three do \u8230\'20\'26 We do not mistake this plainly heartfelt disagreement for disparagement. It is important that the public not be misled either. Any such misperception would be harmful to this institution and our country.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 Republicans cheer court ruling against Biden debt relief plan\par \par \uc2 GOP party leaders and presidential candidates are pleased as punch that the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives have stopped \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from relieving some federal student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s House speaker Kevin McCarthy:\par \par \uc2 enltrBREAKING \u8212\'20\'14> President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s student loan giveaway is ruled UNLAWFUL. The 87% of Americans without student loans are no longer forced to pay for the 13% who do.\par \par \uc2 This builds on the Fiscal Responsibility Act\u8217\'20\'19s end to the payment pause. The President must follow the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kevin McCarthy (@SpeakerMcCarthy) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SpeakerMcCarthy/status/1674793965886009348?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Or former vice-president and current presidential candidate \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who said:\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s massive trillion-dollar student loan bailout subsidizes the education of elites on the backs of hardworking Americans, and it was an egregious violation of the Constitution for him to attempt to do so unilaterally with the stroke of the executive pen. I am pleased that the Court struck down the Radical Left\u8217\'20\'19s effort to use the money of taxpayers who played by the rules and repaid their debts in order to cancel the debt of bankers and lawyers in New York, San Francisco, and Washington, D.C. I am honored to have played a role in appointing three of the Justices that ensured today\u8217\'20\'19s welcomed decision, and as President I will continue to appoint judges who will strictly apply the law and enforce our Constitution\u8217\'20\'19s separation of powers.\par \par \uc2 And Nikki Haley, once \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s United Nations ambassador, now a competitor for the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s presidential nomination:\par \par \uc2 A president cannot just wave his hand and eliminate loans for students he favors, while leaving out all those who worked hard to pay back their loans or made other career choices. The Supreme Court was right to throw out \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s power grab.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court conservatives close term by stopping debt relief and affirmative action, curbing LGTBQ+ protections\par \par \uc2 It took years of work and consecutive victories in crucial elections for Republican lawmakers to create the current conservative majority on the supreme court, but it paid off for the GOP over the past two days as the justices handed down multiple rulings in the party\u8217\'20\'19s favor.\par \par \uc2 Today, the six conservatives on the nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest court blocked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s attempt to relieve some federal student loan debt at the request of a group of Republican attorneys general, and also ruled against a Colorado law intended to protect the LGBTQ+ community from discrimination by businesses.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday, they issued a ruling that did away for good with race-conscious admissions at universities, a practice that has long been in the crosshairs of the American right.\par \par \uc2 As is usually the case with the supreme court, the implications of these rulings will take months to filter across the country, but one things is clear: it has once again been a good term for Republicans and their policies at the nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest court.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn every respect, the Court today exceeds its proper, limited role in our Nation\u8217\'20\'19s governance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Thus begins justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s dissent against the conservative majority\u8217\'20\'19s decision stopping \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from relieving some federal student loan debt. She was joined by her two fellow liberals on the court.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a long read with lots in it, but here\u8217\'20\'19s a good encapsulation of Kagan\u8217\'20\'19s counterargument:\par \par \uc2 The Court reads statutes unnaturally, seeking to cabin their evident scope. And the Court applies heightened-specificity requirements, thwarting Congress\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to ensure adequate responses to unforeseen events. The result here is that the Court substitutes itself for Congress and the Executive Branch in making national policy about student-loan forgiveness. Congress authorized the forgiveness plan (among many other actions); the Secretary put it in place; and the President would have been accountable for its success or failure. But this Court today decides that some 40 million Americans will not receive the benefits the plan provides, because (so says the Court) that assistance is too \u8220\'20\'1Csignifican[t].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , a conservative, authored the majority opinion striking down \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attempt to relieve some student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 He concluded:\par \par \uc2 Last year, the Secretary of Education established the first comprehensive student loan forgiveness program, invoking the Higher Education Relief Opportunities for Students Act of 2003 (HEROES Act) for authority to do so. The Secretary\u8217\'20\'19s plan canceled roughly $430 billion of federal student loan balances, completely erasing the debts of 20 million borrowers and lowering the median amount owed by the other 23 million from $29,400 to $13,600 \u8230\'20\'26 Six States sued, arguing that the HEROES Act does not authorize the loan cancellation plan. We agree.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court conservatives strike down Biden's student debt relief program\par \par \uc2 In their second opinion, the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority struck down \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s program to relieve some federal student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 The three liberal justices dissented.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Court turns down first challenge to Biden student loan relief plan\par \par \uc2 The supreme court unanimously turned down one of two challenges to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student loan relief plan, saying the respondents had no standing.\par \par \uc2 We are waiting for the court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion in the second challenge to the program.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scotusblog.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SCOTUSblog}}}\uc2 , which has somebody inside the supreme court, reports that \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  read aloud from his majority opinion in the Colorado LGBTQ+ rights case, and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  is doing the same from her dissent.\par \par \uc2 That explains the delay between releasing opinions.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is not done issuing opinions.\par \par \uc2 The two cases left both concern \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s program to cancel some federal student loan debt. We expect to get both decisions in a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote the dissent in the Colorado LGTBQ+ rights case, which was joined by the court\u8217\'20\'19s two other liberal justices.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday, the Court, for the first time in its history, grants a business open to the public a constitutional right to refuse to serve members of a protected class,\u8221\'20\'1D Sotomayor wrote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSpecifically, the Court holds that the First Amendment exempts a website design company from a state law that prohibits the company from denying wedding websites to same-sex couples if the company chooses to sell those websites to the public.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She continues:\par \par \uc2 A business open to the public seeks to deny gay and lesbian customers the full and equal enjoyment of its services based on the owner\u8217\'20\'19s religious belief that same-sex marriages are \u8216\'20\'18false\u8217\'20\'19. The business argues, and a majority of the Court agrees, that because the business offers services that are customized and expressive, the Free Speech Clause of the First Amendment shields the business from a generally applicable law that prohibits discrimination in the sale of publicly available goods and services. That is wrong. Profoundly wrong\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur Constitution contains no right to refuse service to a disfavored group,\u8221\'20\'1D Sotomayor writes. \u8220\'20\'1CI dissent.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the meat of the majority opinion in the Colorado LGBTQ+ rights case, as written by conservative justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn this case, Colorado seeks to force an individual to speak in ways that align with its views but defy her conscience about a matter of major significance,\u8221\'20\'1D writes Gorsuch, who ruled against the Colorado law, along with the court\u8217\'20\'19s five other conservatives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut, as this Court has long held, the opportunity to think for ourselves and to express those thoughts freely is among our most cherished liberties and part of what keeps our Republic strong. Of course, abiding the Constitution\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to the freedom of speech means all of us will encounter ideas we consider \u8216\'20\'18unattractive\u8217\'20\'19, \u8216\'20\'18misguided, or even hurtful\u8217\'20\'19, But tolerance, not coercion, is our Nation\u8217\'20\'19s answer. The First Amendment envisions the United States as a rich and complex place where all persons are free to think and speak as they wish, not as the government demands.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court conservatives rule against Colorado law protecting LGBTQ+ rights\par \par \uc2 In its first decision of the day, the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s six conservatives ruled against a Colorado law meant to ban discrimination by businesses against members of the LGBTQ+ community.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court to announce decisions\par \par \uc2 The supreme court will in a few minutes release their opinions in what are expected to be the last three cases outstanding from this term. The conservative-dominated bench may issue decisions concerning \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student loan relief program, and a Colorado law meant to protect LGBTQ+ rights.\par \par \uc2 Follow along here as the decisions come out.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives banded together to strike down race-conscious admissions at universities. It\u8217\'20\'19s too soon to say how colleges nationwide will react to their ruling, but the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/edwin-rios"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Edwin Rios}}}\uc2  reports that if the experience of two major universities is any indication, the decision could mean much fewer Black and Indigenous American students in student bodies:\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to strike down race-consciousness in college admissions will upend the academic landscape for millions of people. But students in nine states already live in a post-affirmative action world.\par \par \uc2 In 1996, Californians voted to ban race-conscious affirmative action policies in the state\u8217\'20\'19s public universities. Since then, eight other states \u8211\'20\'13 Arizona, Florida, Idaho, Michigan, Nebraska, New Hampshire, Oklahoma and Washington \u8211\'20\'13 have also barred race-based considerations, often through ballot initiatives approved by the states\u8217\'20\'19 voters. Some universities in these states report that the bans have made it significantly harder to achieve racial diversity on their campuses.\par \par \uc2 The University of California and the University of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/michigan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michigan}}}\uc2 , whose representatives submitted arguments to the supreme court in support of race-conscious admissions, are two such schools. In the years since Michigan voters ended affirmative action in 2006, for instance, the number of Black and Indigenous American students at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  has plummeted. The same happened at \uc2 UC Berkeley\uc2  and UCLA after voters approved a ban in 1996. And despite developing numerous strategies to supplement the lack of affirmative action policies with other outreach tactics, the representatives wrote, their efforts have failed.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/30/affirmative-action-ban-state-colleges-racial-disparities-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A cautionary tale\u8217\'20\'19: colleges in states with affirmative action bans report racial disparities}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 The group behind 303 Creative LLC v Elenis, the supreme court case that could roll back LGBTQ+ rights, depending on how the justices rule, has seen a surge in revenue in recent years, and channeled that money to other groups promoting rightwing causes, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/adam-gabbatt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Adam Gabbatt}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 A rightwing Christian \u8220\'20\'1Chate group\u8221\'20\'1D which is behind a host of legal efforts to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, remove LGBTQ+ protections and demonize trans people has seen a huge increase in its funding and has funneled some of that money to a slew of smaller anti-LGBTQ+ and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups across the US, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  can reveal.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/19/alliance-defending-freedom-lgbtq-rights-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Alliance Defending Freedom}}}\uc2  (\uc2 ADF\uc2 ), a registered nonprofit behind the ongoing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msnbc.com/deadline-white-house/deadline-legal-blog/neil-gorsuch-303-creative-supreme-court-rcna91471"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 303 Creative supreme court case}}}\uc2  which could chip away at LGBTQ+ rights, saw its revenue surge by more than $25m between 2020 and 2021, a period in which a rightwing obsession with transgender rights and sexual orientation saw almost 200 anti-LGBTQ+ bills {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/nbc-out/out-politics-and-policy/nearly-240-anti-lgbtq-bills-filed-2022-far-targeting-trans-people-rcna20418"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduced}}}\uc2  in states around the US.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/30/christian-hate-group-funding-us-anti-lgbtq-anti-abortion-organizations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Revealed: Christian legal nonprofit funds US anti-LGBTQ+ and anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  organizations}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 In major LGBTQ+ rights case before supreme court, all is not as it seems\par \par \uc2 The supreme court could today issue its opinion in 303 Creative LLC v Elenis, which deals with a Colorado law that prohibits businesses from discriminating against gay people, or stating that they would. There\u8217\'20\'19s no telling how they might rule, but the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority could use the case as an opportunity to roll back LGBTQ+ protections nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/sam-levine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Levine}}}\uc2  reported that it appears a document with a major role in the case could have been fabricated \u8211\'20\'13 but that fact may not make a difference in the case.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from his story:\par \par \uc2 The veracity of a key document in a major LGBTQ+ rights case before the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court}}}\uc2  has come under question, raising the possibility that important evidence cited in it might be wrong or even falsified.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is expected to issue a ruling on Friday in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/303-creative-llc-v-elenis/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 303 Creative LLC v Elenis}}}\uc2 , which deals with a challenge to a Colorado law prohibiting public-serving businesses from discriminating against gay people as well as any statements announcing such a policy.\par \par \uc2 The suit centers on Lorie Smith, a website designer who does not want to provide her services for gay weddings because of her religious objections.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, she says, a gay man named Stewart requested her services for help with his upcoming wedding. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are getting married early next year and would love some design work done for our invites, placenames etc. We might also stretch to a website,\u8221\'20\'1D reads a message he apparently sent her through her website.\par \par \uc2 In court filings, her lawyers produced a copy of the inquiry.\par \par \uc2 But Stewart, who requested his last name be withheld for privacy, said in an interview with the Guardian that he never sent the message, even though it correctly lists his email address and telephone number. He has also been happily married to a woman for the last 15 years, he said. The news was first reported by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newrepublic.com/article/173987/mysterious-case-fake-gay-marriage-website-real-straight-man-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the New Republic}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In fact, until he received a call this week from a reporter from the magazine, Stewart said he had no idea he was somehow tied up in a case that had made it to the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can confirm I did not contact 303 Creative about a website,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s fraudulent insomuch as someone is pretending to be me and looking to marry someone called Mike. That\u8217\'20\'19s not me.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s most concerning to me is that this is kind of like the one main piece of evidence that\u8217\'20\'19s been part of this case for the last six-plus years and it\u8217\'20\'19s false,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CNobody\u8217\'20\'19s checked it. Anybody can pick up the phone, write an email, send a text, to verify whether that was correct information.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/29/supreme-court-lgbtq-document-veracity-colorado"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Key document may be fake in LGBTQ+ rights case before US supreme court}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Student debt relief, LGBTQ+ protections at risk as supreme court issues more opinions\par \par \uc2 Good morning, US politics blog readers. The supreme court will likely issue their final opinions of their term at 10am ET, and two cases with major implications are yet to be decided. The first concerns \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student debt relief plan, which conservative groups are asking the court to halt. The other is a challenge, again by a rightwing group, to a Colorado law protecting LGBTQ+ rights, which centers on a web designer who did not want to work with a same-sex couple. The court is dominated by conservative justices, and while they maintained the status quo on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/08/supreme-court-voting-rights-decision-allen-milligan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voting rights}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/27/supreme-court-north-carolina-voting-rights-maps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 election law}}}\uc2  earlier this term, they again showed their willingness to upend longstanding practice with yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s ruling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/29/us-supreme-court-affirmative-action-harvard-unc-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 against affirmative action}}}\uc2 . We\u8217\'20\'19ll see if that streak continues in the cases they could decide today.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else is happening:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will participate in a moderated conversation on reproductive and mental health, among other topics, at New Orleans\u8217\'20\'19s ESSENCE Festival of Culture at 3.50pm CT.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Biden elaborated on his comment that the current supreme court is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot a normal court\u8221\'20\'1D in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/29/supreme-court-rulings-latest-affirmative-action-student-debt-us-news-biden-trump?page=with:block-649de8888f08ff8b601509d3#block-649de8888f08ff8b601509d3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an interview with MSNBC}}}\uc2  last night.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and other Republican 2024 presidential hopefuls {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/moms-for-liberty-trump-desantis-2024-republicans-8e17f7587bba9cf6dd316c3ef2eb6a19"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will appear today}}}\uc2  at an event hosted by Moms for Liberty, which distinguishes itself among conservative groups for its opposition to classroom teaching of race and gender identity.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230630ej6u001ms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
