\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The fight over US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights in the year without Roe \u8211\'20\'13 photo essay\b0\par\par\uc2 Photo essay by Kasia Strek, as told to Abigail Abrams \par \uc2 1,916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2023\par 17:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A look back on the year since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade with the Dobbs decision, and the advocates who aren\u8217\'20\'19t giving up\par \par \uc2 While the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade sent shockwaves around the country, many activists, physicians and advocacy groups closely engaged in the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US were not surprised.\par \par \uc2 Since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s establishment of the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates and conservative lawmakers have been chipping away at it. Restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased over the last decade, and by the mid-2010s, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/paz4bv/last-clinics-seven-states-one-abortion-clinic-left"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seven states}}}\uc2  had just one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic left. In Mississippi, where the Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization case that ultimately ended Roe originated, the state\u8217\'20\'19s one clinic did not provide abortions beyond 16 weeks of pregnancy, meaning many people already had to travel to find care.\par \par \uc2 For residents in a state with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2022/demo/p60-277.html&sa=D&source=docs&ust=1687551917054085&usg=AOvVaw05ZeEU66NlRo7tD_piCQ3O"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest poverty rate}}}\uc2  in the nation, where there is no guaranteed childcare, and where elected officials have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/medicaid/issue-brief/status-of-state-medicaid-expansion-decisions-interactive-map/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 refused to expand Medicaid}}}\uc2  to cover low-income workers, that often meant obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely. For those who do move forward with pregnancies, the state doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make that easy either. The states like Mississippi that have now passed the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are also typically the ones with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncsl.org/labor-and-employment/state-minimum-wages"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lowest minimum wages}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/28/upshot/abortion-bans-states-social-services.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the least generous policies }}}\uc2  that support families, least access to education and to health care. In many southern states, the rates of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/other/state-indicator/maternal-deaths-and-mortality-rates-per-100000-live-births/?currentTimeframe=0&sortModel=%7B%22colId%22:%22Maternal%20Mortality%20Rate%20per%20100,000%20live%20Births%22,%22sort%22:%22desc%22%7D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 maternal }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/pressroom/sosmap/infant_mortality_rates/infant_mortality.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 infant mortality}}}\uc2  and morbidity are among the highest in the country. The US has the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/blog/2022/us-maternal-mortality-crisis-continues-worsen-international-comparison"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest maternal death rate}}}\uc2  of any wealthy country, and Black women suffer most \u8211\'20\'13 they\u8217\'20\'19re {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2020/maternal-mortality-rates-2020.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three to four times}}}\uc2  as likely to die from pregnancy-related complications as white women.\par \par \uc2 The ways the US healthcare system has treated Black people continues to affect their health in profound ways, says Dr Nina Ragunanthan, an OB-GYN who serves the largely low-income Black community in Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s Delta region.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou take a group of people that have been so abused for generations,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe essential stress from that passes down.\u8221\'20\'1D Ragunanthan and other doctors say these inherited health challenges combined with ongoing racism mean that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and their subsequent effects, from medical providers moving away from states with restrictive laws to the potential of criminal penalties, have a disproportionate impact on Black Americans.\par \par \uc2 In the days after the Dobbs decision, protesters flooded the Pink House, as the Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization clinic was known. The Pink House Defenders, the group of volunteers who escorted patients to and from the clinic, were used to seeing regular, sometimes heated demonstrations outside, and so tried to keep things as normal as possible for patients, said Derenda Hancock, the group\u8217\'20\'19s founder. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are just trying to be here for the patients until the last moment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics stopped providing care the moment the Dobbs decision came down as they scrambled to interpret what the ruling meant for their state. The Pink House closed its doors for good on 6 July, and as the summer stretched on, more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans took effect. Sixty-six clinics across 15 states stopped offering abortions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/2022/10/100-days-post-roe-least-66-clinics-across-15-us-states-have-stopped-offering-abortion-care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the first 100 days}}}\uc2  after Roe fell, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research form that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Clinics in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal saw huge increases in patients seeking care, and they tried to expand hours and staffing to meet those needs.\par \par \uc2 I went home with his birth certificate and an empty box with a black flower on it. \u8211\'20\'13 Ebony Jones\par \par \uc2 Doctors, patients and activists spoke out, drawing attention to the ripple effects that new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws were having not only on those who sought abortions, but also on people experiencing pregnancy complications, miscarriages and on those who used medications that were caught up in the uncertainty around the new legislation. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and advocates, meanwhile, argued that the laws were clear and doctors were at fault for any confusion about when the laws applied.\par \par \uc2 In August, the new era of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions faced its first test at the ballot box when Kansas voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.kansascity.com/news/politics-government/election/article263832087.html&sa=D&source=docs&ust=1687552120786292&usg=AOvVaw2vaCEeHFn33WlS6XtsX5Np"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  decisively rejected a measure}}}\uc2  that would have opened the state to future \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. The surprising win for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters served as a symbol of a brewing backlash to the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision, and added fuel to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/13/us/politics/republicans-abortion-national-ban.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 debates within the Republican party}}}\uc2  about how far it should go with its new ability to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator Lindsay Graham proposed a national 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in September, but other conservative states faltered as they tried to agree on limits for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the constraints of Roe.\par \par \uc2 The intensity of these fights increased as the country headed toward the midterm elections, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suddenly at the forefront of nearly every campaign. Members of Congress held hearings on the topic. Advocates flooded into states where more ballot measures addressed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and where voters were now acutely aware of the power that state and local elected officials have over their lives.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, reproductive justice advocates, who had been warning for years that Roe was not enough to guarantee \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, ramped up their plans to help people find care. Some clinics that stopped offering abortions remained open in states with bans to provide other reproductive health services, gender-affirming care and follow-up appointments for people who terminated their pregnancies out of state.\par \par \uc2 Grassroots groups known as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds raised money to help people afford abortions and the travel, lodging, childcare and extra costs that come with seeking medical assistance far from home. People like Michelle Colon, founder of Shero in Mississippi, organized volunteers to drive more than 50 women at least 10 hours north to Illinois to obtain abortions. Colon herself drove at least 20 times between the end of June and the fall, sometimes bringing more than one patient in her car and paying for travel costs herself. She is now among the very few individuals left providing such help in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 With the country focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a way it hadn\u8217\'20\'19t been for years, advocates tried to cut through the stigma around the topic. They held conferences, speakouts and encouraged politicians to talk about the issue on the campaign trail leading up to the November midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 On 8 November 2022, voters delivered a ringing endorsement of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in key states, establishing new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in Michigan, California and Vermont, while rejecting efforts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or criminalize providers in Kentucky and Montana. Elsewhere, voters supported governors and other state level candidates who backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in places like Wisconsin, Michigan, Pennsylvania, Kansas and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 As the post-Roe reality set in, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, which have been on the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s list of \u8220\'20\'1Cessential medicines\u8221\'20\'1D for years and now account for more than half of US abortions, proved a key flash point.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists see the pills, a two-drug regimen also known as medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a key way to help people get care privately and take some of the load off brick-and-mortar clinics. Telehealth services like Hey Jane, Choix and \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Demand expanded in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal. The number of abortions provided by virtual-only telehealth providers increased from an average of 4,025 abortions a month (or 5% of all abortions) before Dobbs to 7,461 a month (9% of all abortions) in the first nine months after the decision, according to the Society of Family Planning\u8217\'20\'19s new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/WeCountReport_6.12.23.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WeCount report}}}\uc2 . For people who couldn\u8217\'20\'19t or didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to use official channels, activists expanded underground networks run by groups like Las Libres and Red Necesito Arbortar that are delivering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to Americans from as far away as Mexico and India.\par \par \uc2 Those who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  see pills as enabling a way around their favored laws. While the vast majority of Americans did not want to see Roe overturned and public opinion has only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/506759/broader-support-abortion-rights-continues-post-dobbs.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 solidified in favor}}}\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since last summer, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement does not care if its positions are not widely popular. This year, Christian legal advocacy group \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  filed a lawsuit aiming to force the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to take mifepristone, one of the two drugs used for medication abortions, off the market nationwide.\par \par \uc2 After a Trump-appointed federal judge in Texas agreed with the group, the supreme court eventually blocked the restrictions and maintained access for now. But the case is likely to end up back at the supreme court, where it could still upend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in every state.\par \par \uc2 She asked me to show her a photo of my oldest son. And then she asked \u8216\'20\'18what if I kill him? \u8211\'20\'13 Amanda Furdge\par \par \uc2 The 2023 legislative session brought a number of new state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions, though some of these have now been blocked by courts. Democratic-leaning states pushed back by allocating funding and passing new protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers and providers within their borders. In other places, conservative lawmakers sought to limit ballot measures related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A group of patients in Texas who say they were denied medically necessary abortions despite risks to their lives and their fetuses, sued the state seeking to clarify exceptions to its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and as physicians in other states highlighted similar situations also arguing for more health-based exceptions, lawmakers often proved skeptical and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates encouraged them to hold strong.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the spring, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds continued to help people access care. But while many groups in places as diverse as Louisiana, Arizona and Colorado doubled and tripled their fundraising from before Dobbs, they are now warning that funding is lagging as the public\u8217\'20\'19s attention wanes. The New York \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Fund, which has helped patients from 29 states, Washington DC and six countries access abortions in the last year, says it is on pace to run out of money by October if it does not receive more support.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, health problems continue and people like Colon, one of the few people helping residents in her state find the resources to travel and obtain abortions, says the situation feels almost like Jenga, the children\u8217\'20\'19s game that involves precariously removing wooden blocks from a tower until they collapse. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re just pulling away everything,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s only a couple more blocks left. You\u8217\'20\'19ve got these other blocks just kind of teetering.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 We never thought that we would be the ones who would be able to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to women in the US. \u8211\'20\'13 Veronica Cruz\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not clear what will happen when the last block is gone. More lawsuits and legal attacks are coming, OB-GYNs are leaving states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and training for the next generation of medical residents in states with restrictions remains uncertain.\par \par \uc2 But for now, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are not giving up.\par \par \uc2 Colon says she feels like she is fighting back against the new laws every time she gets someone to an appointment to terminate their pregnancy if that\u8217\'20\'19s what they want. \u8220\'20\'1CYou may have put up all of these hurdles, but we\u8217\'20\'19re still getting folks there,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re basically crawling over fucking glass, we\u8217\'20\'19re climbing mountains, yet we\u8217\'20\'19re still getting people there.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230626ej6q0023a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
