\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: Russia attempts to restore calm after Wagner mutiny\b0\par\par\uc2 Nicola Slawson \par \uc2 2,000 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2023\par 11:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Footage shows defence minister \uc2 Sergei Shoigu\uc2  visiting troops in Ukraine as counter-terror measures ended. Plus, how El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o is affecting global heating in 2023Don\u8217\'20\'19t already get First Thing in your inbox? Sign up here\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s defense minister has appeared on state TV and emergency counter-terrorism measures have been cancelled in Moscow and surrounding regions as the Kremlin seeks to restore calm after \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s failed mutiny.\par \par \uc2 The defense ministry released footage today that it claimed showed \uc2 Sergei Shoigu\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cvisiting the forward command post of one of the formations of the \u8216\'20\'18western\u8217\'20\'19 group of troops\u8221\'20\'1D. In the video, Shoigu is shown riding in a vehicle and arriving at a command post, where he listens to reports from officers and pores over a battlefield map.\par \par \uc2 If confirmed, the footage would be the first sighting of Shoigu since Prigozhin, the leader of the Wagner mercenary group, declared war on him and his ministry on Friday.\par \par \uc2 But the video was released without sound and it was unclear when and where it was filmed. Russian news agencies have in the past released pre-filmed segments called \u8220\'20\'1Cpreserves\u8221\'20\'1D, attempting to show that officials including \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  were working in the Kremlin when they could be hundreds of miles away.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s happening in Moscow? A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/25/it-was-an-emotional-day-calm-returns-to-moscow-after-wagner-stand-down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sense of normality returned}}}\uc2  to Moscow yesterday after Prigozhin halted his assault on the Russian capital under a deal that defused an unprecedented challenge to Putin\u8217\'20\'19s authority. Security forces were seen disassembling barricades that had been hurriedly put up as Prigozhin\u8217\'20\'19s Wagner troops approached along the M4 road from Rostov-on-Don, while workers began repairing roads leading up to the capital that were destroyed to stop the warlord\u8217\'20\'19s advances before he ordered their withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What does the future hold for Prigozhin and Wagner after the mutiny? Despite the seemingly failed mutiny and Peskov\u8217\'20\'19s statements, Wagner fighters seemed to be in a celebratory mood on Saturday, tooting their horns as they left Rostov-on-Don. Several Telegram channels linked to Wagner were similarly upbeat, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/25/what-does-the-future-hold-for-prigozhin-and-wagner-after-the-mutiny"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggesting that the uprising had reached its goals}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We can\u8217\'20\'19t rest or relent\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  reiterates support of staunch \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions\par \par \uc2 Despite their unpopularity with the American public, former Republican vice-president and 2024 White House hopeful \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  increased his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/26/mike-pence-reiterates-support-abortion-restrictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hardline support of staunch \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restrictions}}}\uc2 , saying: \u8220\'20\'1CWe just can\u8217\'20\'19t rest or relent until we restore the sanctity of life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pence \u8211\'20\'13 in an interview on Fox News Sunday \u8211\'20\'13 made clear that he viewed bringing the elimination of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cto the center of American law\u8221\'20\'1D as both essential and \u8220\'20\'1Ca winning issue\u8221\'20\'1D for the Republican party trying to wrest back control of the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m pro-life, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t apologize for it,\u8221\'20\'1D Pence boasted to host Shannon Bream, even though polling shows most Americans favor keeping the termination of pregnancies legal in most cases.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization ruling that the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  handed down one year ago Saturday in effect left it up to states to decide whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal within their boundaries. Many states have taken steps to severely limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , setting off a seismic shift in how doctors are training to provide reproductive care to how far patients in need of attention must travel.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What did Pence say about Roe v Wade? On Sunday the former vice-president hailed the Dobbs decision as \u8220\'20\'1Ca historic victory\u8221\'20\'1D that sent the 1973 Roe v Wade ruling establishing federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8220\'20\'1Cto the ash heap of history\u8221\'20\'1D. But he also suggested it didn\u8217\'20\'19t go far enough and reiterated a call he had publicly made days earlier for his fellow Republicans to rally behind setting \u8220\'20\'1Ca minimum standard\u8221\'20\'1D and implementing a nationwide ban on abortions beyond 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 enduring fealty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on display at conference after his indictment\par \par \uc2 Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/25/republicans-trump-conference"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enduring loyalty to Donald Trump}}}\uc2  was on vivid display at a conservative conference this weekend, convened just two weeks after the former president was indicted on 37 federal charges related to his alleged mishandling of classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Addressing this year\u8217\'20\'19s Road to Majority conference Saturday, Trump lashed out against federal prosecutors, who have accused the former president of intentionally withholding classified documents from authorities and obstructing justice in his efforts to keep those materials concealed. Trump, who could soon face additional charges in Washington and Georgia, told the supportive crowd that he considered each of his two indictments so far to be \u8220\'20\'1Ca great badge of courage\u8221\'20\'1D as he ran to unseat the Democratic incumbent, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has weaponized law enforcement to interfere in our elections,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump told the audience. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m being indicted for you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump was among several Republican presidential candidates to speak at the conference, held in Washington and hosted by the rightwing evangelical group Faith and Freedom Coalition.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What did his opponents say? His message was echoed by some of his presidential primary opponents. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the Florida governor, promised to replace the \uc2 FBI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s director, \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , who was appointed during Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidency. The South Carolina US senator, Tim Scott, pledged to fire the attorney general, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , and \u8220\'20\'1Cchange the trajectory of this nation by focussing on restoring confidence and integrity\u8221\'20\'1D in the US justice department.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Did anyone speak against him? Yes. Former New Jersey governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , did not shy away from directly confronting Trump, accusing him of \u8220\'20\'1Cletting us down\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 India\u8217\'20\'19s finance minister has derided comments by Barack Obama that \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s government should protect the rights of minority Muslims, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/26/obama-remarks-on-indias-treatment-of-muslims-hypocritical-minister"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accusing Obama of being hypocritical}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CHe was commenting on Indian Muslims \u8230\'20\'26 having bombed Muslim-majority countries from Syria to Yemen \u8230\'20\'26 during his presidency,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Nirmala Sitharaman\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Greece\u8217\'20\'19s new prime minister,\par \par \uc2 Kyriakos Mitsotakis\par \par \uc2 , vowed to immediately press ahead with his ambitious reform programme after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/25/conservative-mitsotakis-expected-to-win-greek-general-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 winning a decisive victory}}}\uc2  in the general elections yesterday. The New Democracy leader said his commanding 24-point lead had given him a \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong mandate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A worker at San Antonio\u8217\'20\'19s international airport died after being sucked into a jet\u8217\'20\'19s engine late on Friday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/25/texas-airport-worker-dies-after-being-sucked-into-delta-jet-engine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 officials said}}}\uc2 . A source briefed directly on the case told the Guardian on Sunday that it appeared the worker had \u8220\'20\'1Cintentionally stepped in front of the live engine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Authorities on Sunday were testing the water quality along a stretch of the Yellowstone River where mangled train cars carrying hazardous materials remained after crashing into the waterway\par \par \uc2 after a bridge collapse. The seven mangled train cars were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/25/montana-officials-testing-yellowstone-river-water-at-site-of-rail-bridge-collapse"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 carrying hot asphalt and molten sulfur}}}\uc2  when they fell.\par \par \uc2 Stat of the day: New Jersey man flies 23m miles with lifetime United pass \u8216\'20\'18like a sultan\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 A US man who bought a lifetime pass from \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  three decades ago has \u8220\'20\'1Clived like a sultan\u8221\'20\'1D ever since, according to a report, flying multiples of miles more than the Apollo 11 spacecraft in the process. Tom Stuker, from New Jersey, paid $290,000 for the pass in 1990, according to the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , a decision he said is the \u8220\'20\'1Cbest investment of my life\u8221\'20\'1D. Stuker\u8217\'20\'19s mileage in 2019 alone \u8220\'20\'1Ccovered more than six trips to the moon\u8221\'20\'1D, the Post reported. That year Stuker took 373 flights, covering 1.46m miles. Had he paid cash for the flights, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2023/jun/25/new-jersey-man-lifetime-united-airlines-pass"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it would have cost $2.44m}}}\uc2 . As for the environmental impact, Stuker seemed unfazed in an interview with GQ in 2020. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not adding to the footprint,\u8221\'20\'1D Stuker said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe plane is going to fly whether I\u8217\'20\'19m on it or not. It would be much more relevant if I was flying in a private jet. Those are the people who can help the environment much more than I can if they flew commercial.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: Attacks on LGBTQ+ Americans cast long shadow over Pride month\par \par \uc2 St Cloud, Florida, a suburb of Orlando, hosted its first official Pride celebration just last year. This year, the organizers made the difficult decision to cancel, in large part because of the state\u8217\'20\'19s recent law effectively banning drag shows with minors in attendance. The other reason was a death threat; a sign appeared in a nearby community that read: \u8220\'20\'1CKill all the gays.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For decades in the US, Pride events have been a celebrated \u8211\'20\'13 and even profitable \u8211\'20\'13 part of the month of June, dedicated to the history and culture of the LGBTQ+ community. But recent legislative attacks on transgender Americans and hateful political rhetoric about queer identity have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/25/lgbtq-americans-pride-month"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cast a shadow over Pride this year}}}\uc2 , even in states traditionally supportive of gay rights. Extremists have cast threats and attacks against members of the LGBTQ+ community, forcing events to ramp up security or cancel. Elected officials have tried \u8211\'20\'13 and sometimes succeeded \u8211\'20\'13 to ban the emblematic rainbow flag. Companies have backtracked on their support.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Pride across the US: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/25/pride-across-the-us-celebration-and-defiance-in-the-face-of-threats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 celebration and defiance in the face of threats}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26 or this: \u8216\'20\'18Let the world know\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 elderly survivors of the Tulsa race massacre push for justice\par \par \uc2 In 1921, a white mob descended on the neighborhood of Greenwood, home to a business district known as Black Wall Street, killing an estimated 300 people and looting and burning businesses and homes. Thousands were left homeless and living in a hastily constructed internment camp. It became known as the Tulsa race massacre. For most Americans it is the stuff of history books and museum exhibits, as foreign and far away as Charles Lindbergh or the Wall Street crash. For Viola Ford Fletcher, it is a childhood scar that never went away. Now 109 and still dressing to the nines with earrings and bracelets, she is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/25/tulsa-race-massacre-survivors-congress-justice-reparations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 oldest living survivor of the massacre}}}\uc2  and is still demanding accountability. There has never been any direct compensation from the city of Tulsa or the state of Oklahoma for massacre survivors or their descendants. Racial disparities, compounded by gentrification and urban planning, persist in Tulsa today.\par \par \uc2 Climate check: El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o \u8211\'20\'13 how the weather event is affecting global heating in 2023\par \par \uc2 The planet is being hit with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/jun/24/el-nino-how-the-weather-event-is-affecting-global-heating-in-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 double-whammy of global heating in 2023}}}\uc2 . On top of the inexorable rise in global temperature caused by greenhouse gas emissions is an emerging El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o. This sporadic event is the biggest natural influence on year-to-year weather and adds a further spurt of warmth to an already overheating world. The result is supercharged extreme weather, hitting lives and livelihoods. The last significant El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o from 2014 to 2016 led to each of those years successively breaking the global temperature record and 2016 remains the hottest year ever recorded. Now El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o has begun again and may already be driving new temperature records, with record heatwaves on land from Puerto Rico to China and record heatwaves in the seas around the UK.\par \par \uc2 Last Thing: Harvard professor who studies honesty accused of falsifying data in studies\par \par \uc2 In an ironic twist in the world of behavioral science, a Harvard professor who studies honesty has been accused of data fraud. Over the last few weeks, allegations have surfaced against \uc2 Francesca Gino\uc2 , a prominent \uc2 Harvard Business School (HBS) \uc2 professor who has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/education/2023/jun/25/harvard-professor-data-fraud"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused of falsifying results}}}\uc2  in several behavioral science studies. On June 16, the Chronicle of Higher Education reported that Max Bazerman, an \uc2 HBS\uc2  professor and co-author who published a paper in 2012 alongside Gino, said that Harvard informed him that it believed one of the studies overseen by Gino had falsified results. The paper in question is on findings published in \u8211\'20\'13 and later retracted by \u8211\'20\'13 the Proceedings of the \uc2 National Academy of Sciences\uc2  and surrounds an experiment that asked participants to fill out tax and insurance paperwork.\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230626ej6q0018k\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 atleast5(abortion) OR at